1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
[JEFF] Alright. Cyrus, I have to ask you something before we start. When was the last

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,800
[JEFF] time you actually thought about your refrigerator?

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,600
[CYRUS] Like, thought about it as a thing? As in, appreciated it?

4
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:19,600
[JEFF] As in — considered the fact that it exists. That it's just sitting there in

5
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:25,600
[JEFF] your kitchen, humming away, keeping your leftovers cold. When did you last think about that?

6
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:31,400
[CYRUS] I mean, honestly? Never. I open the door, I grab what I need, I close

7
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:37,200
[CYRUS] it. It's like — you don't think about your floor, right? It's just there.

8
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:42,000
[JEFF] Exactly. And that's kind of the whole premise of this show. Right? These technologies that

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,800
[JEFF] are so woven into how we live that we've stopped seeing them. They're invisible. You

10
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:51,600
[JEFF] only notice them when they break.

11
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,266
[CYRUS] Which I have experienced, by the way. The compressor went out in our old fridge

12
00:00:56,266 --> 00:01:00,933
[CYRUS] one summer, and I'm like — what the heck do we do? We couldn't even

13
00:01:00,933 --> 00:01:05,600
[CYRUS] keep milk for a day.

14
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:10,300
[JEFF] Yeah, and that's the interesting thing. Because that feeling — that sudden helplessness — that's

15
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:15,000
[JEFF] actually the story we're going to tell today. The whole story. The world discovering it

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,700
[JEFF] needed something it didn't know existed, and then the world becoming completely unable to live

17
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:24,400
[JEFF] without it.

18
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:24,800
[CYRUS] Refrigeration.

19
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:29,400
[JEFF] Refrigeration. Cold, Built In. And I want to start with a scene, because I think

20
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:34,000
[JEFF] the scene is the whole episode in miniature.

21
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,666
[JEFF] It's February of eighteen-oh-six. Boston harbor. And there's a twenty-three year old standing at the

22
00:01:39,666 --> 00:01:45,333
[JEFF] dock watching something that everyone — literally everyone around him — thinks is insane. His

23
00:01:45,333 --> 00:01:51,000
[JEFF] name is Frederic Tudor. He comes from a good Boston family, the kind where his

24
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,666
[JEFF] father studied law under John Adams. And Frederic had rejected Harvard at age thirteen, declared

25
00:01:56,666 --> 00:02:02,333
[JEFF] formal education a waste of time, and spent the past two years completely consumed by

26
00:02:02,333 --> 00:02:08,000
[JEFF] one idea. He wants to ship ice to the Caribbean.

27
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,600
[CYRUS] I'm sorry — ship ice. To the Caribbean.

28
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:17,600
[JEFF] Ice. Cut from a frozen pond outside Boston. Loaded onto a boat. Shipped to Martinique.

29
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:20,400
[CYRUS] Like — natural ice? From a pond?

30
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:26,160
[JEFF] A hundred and thirty tons of it, packed in sawdust, on a brig called the

31
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:31,920
[JEFF] Favorite. And here's the thing. He had spent two years trying to find a ship

32
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:37,680
[JEFF] captain who would take the cargo. Every single captain in Boston said no. They were

33
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:43,440
[JEFF] convinced the melting ice would sink the ship, or that the whole idea was simply

34
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:49,200
[JEFF] lunacy. Tudor had to buy the brig himself just to guarantee passage.

35
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:54,400
[CYRUS] Okay, so — no one in Boston thought this was a good idea.

36
00:02:54,400 --> 00:03:00,080
[JEFF] The Boston Gazette ran a notice the same week the ship cleared customs. And I

37
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:05,760
[JEFF] love this. The headline is — and this is not a joke, this is the

38
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:11,440
[JEFF] actual headline — "No JOKE." Capital N, capital J, capital O, capital K, capital E.

39
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:17,120
[JEFF] "No JOKE. A vessel has cleared at the Custom House for Martinique with a cargo

40
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:22,800
[JEFF] of ice. We hope this will not prove a slippery speculation."

41
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:24,000
[CYRUS] "Slippery speculation."

42
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,800
[JEFF] Slippery. Speculation. The whole city was laughing.

43
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:31,600
[CYRUS] Okay, so — how does it go? Does the ice make it?

44
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:36,933
[JEFF] Sort of. Some of it makes it. But when the ship arrives in Martinique, there's

45
00:03:36,933 --> 00:03:42,266
[JEFF] no icehouse. There's nowhere to put the ice. A lot of it melts. Tudor loses

46
00:03:42,266 --> 00:03:47,600
[JEFF] money on the whole voyage. And that's just the beginning — over the next seven

47
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,933
[JEFF] years, he's going to go to debtor's prison at least twice. He's going to be

48
00:03:52,933 --> 00:03:58,266
[JEFF] mocked as "the Ice Fool." He's going to spend years hiding from the sheriff when

49
00:03:58,266 --> 00:04:03,600
[JEFF] he can't pay his creditors.

50
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:05,600
[CYRUS] So it was a disaster.

51
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:10,133
[JEFF] ... He dies in eighteen sixty-four. Worth the equivalent of roughly two hundred million dollars

52
00:04:10,133 --> 00:04:14,666
[JEFF] in today's money. Having invented a global industry out of something Bostonians scraped off frozen

53
00:04:14,666 --> 00:04:19,200
[JEFF] ponds and considered worthless.

54
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:20,000
[CYRUS] Wow.

55
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,200
[JEFF] "The Ice Fool." That's what they called him.

56
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:24,800
[CYRUS] That is a story.

57
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:30,560
[JEFF] And what I love about it — the thing that made me want to start

58
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:36,320
[JEFF] there — is that the Boston Gazette's joke? That is the whole episode. That's the

59
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:42,080
[JEFF] thesis. Every single wave of refrigeration we're going to cover today has that same dynamic.

60
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:47,840
[JEFF] Someone sees something that seems completely obvious in retrospect. The world laughs. And then the

61
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:53,600
[JEFF] world becomes utterly, irreversibly dependent on the thing they were laughing at.

62
00:02:36,045 --> 00:02:41,445
[CYRUS] Okay, so let's put some scale on this. Because I think people hear "ice trade"

63
00:02:41,445 --> 00:02:46,845
[CYRUS] and they picture like, a novelty. A curiosity. Not a real industry.

64
00:02:46,845 --> 00:02:52,645
[JEFF] Yeah. I think the way I think about this is — most people know refrigeration

65
00:02:52,645 --> 00:02:58,445
[JEFF] is important. But they don't know HOW important. Like, the numbers genuinely surprised me.

66
00:02:58,445 --> 00:03:04,445
[CYRUS] Start with the adoption curve, because I think that's the one that hit me hardest.

67
00:03:04,445 --> 00:03:07,845
[JEFF] Okay. So here's the number. In nineteen-thirty, eight percent of American homes had a refrigerator.

68
00:03:07,845 --> 00:03:11,245
[JEFF] Eight percent.

69
00:03:11,245 --> 00:03:14,445
[CYRUS] Eight. As in, less than one in ten.

70
00:03:14,445 --> 00:03:20,445
[JEFF] Less than one in ten. And then — by nineteen forty-four, it was eighty-five percent.

71
00:03:20,445 --> 00:03:24,045
[CYRUS] Wait, what? From eight to eighty-five in fourteen years?

72
00:03:24,045 --> 00:03:29,511
[JEFF] Fourteen years. During the Great Depression and World War Two. Like, the financial conditions are

73
00:03:29,511 --> 00:03:34,978
[JEFF] arguably as bad as they've ever been in American life, and somehow this technology goes

74
00:03:34,978 --> 00:03:40,444
[JEFF] from a rich person's luxury to something nearly every household has.

75
00:03:40,444 --> 00:03:45,244
[CYRUS] That's... that's one of the fastest technology adoptions in American history. I'm trying to think

76
00:03:45,244 --> 00:03:50,044
[CYRUS] of something comparable. Like, smartphones didn't even do that.

77
00:03:50,044 --> 00:03:55,324
[JEFF] Yeah, and we'll get into why it happened that fast — it's actually a really

78
00:03:55,324 --> 00:04:00,604
[JEFF] interesting story. But the scale of what it displaced... Get this, get this. At its

79
00:04:00,604 --> 00:04:05,884
[JEFF] peak, before the mechanical age, the natural ice trade — just cutting ice from frozen

80
00:04:05,884 --> 00:04:11,164
[JEFF] ponds and lakes and shipping it around the world — employed ninety thousand workers and

81
00:04:11,164 --> 00:04:16,444
[JEFF] twenty-five thousand horses. Nationally, across America.

82
00:04:16,444 --> 00:04:19,644
[CYRUS] Ninety thousand people worked in the ice trade.

83
00:04:19,644 --> 00:04:25,511
[JEFF] And it was capitalized at the equivalent of six hundred and sixty million dollars in

84
00:04:25,511 --> 00:04:31,378
[JEFF] today's money. The entire industry. It wasn't a curiosity — it was a major economic

85
00:04:31,378 --> 00:04:37,245
[JEFF] sector. American ships were carrying more tonnage of ice than any commodity except cotton.

86
00:04:37,245 --> 00:04:39,645
[CYRUS] And that entire industry... went away.

87
00:04:39,645 --> 00:04:44,178
[JEFF] That entire industry went away. And we'll talk about what happened to those ninety thousand

88
00:04:44,178 --> 00:04:48,711
[JEFF] workers. Or actually — the disturbing part is that we can't talk about it, because

89
00:04:48,711 --> 00:04:53,244
[JEFF] nobody wrote it down.

90
00:04:53,244 --> 00:04:54,844
[CYRUS] What do you mean?

91
00:04:54,844 --> 00:05:00,644
[JEFF] I mean there's essentially no record of what happened to them. An entire workforce of

92
00:05:00,644 --> 00:05:06,444
[JEFF] ninety thousand people disappeared, and history just... moved on. We'll come back to that.

93
00:05:06,444 --> 00:05:10,444
[CYRUS] Okay. What about the scale of what refrigeration is today?

94
00:05:10,444 --> 00:05:15,778
[JEFF] So this is the one that I find kind of mind-bending. Twenty-five to thirty percent

95
00:05:15,778 --> 00:05:21,111
[JEFF] of all global electricity — all of it, everything generated by every power plant in

96
00:05:21,111 --> 00:05:26,444
[JEFF] the world — is consumed by refrigeration and air conditioning.

97
00:05:26,444 --> 00:05:28,044
[CYRUS] That can't be right.

98
00:05:28,044 --> 00:05:33,844
[JEFF] That's the IEA number. International Energy Agency. The global cold chain — moving food from

99
00:05:33,844 --> 00:05:39,644
[JEFF] farm to shelf — touches seventy percent of all food consumed worldwide. Seven zero.

100
00:05:39,644 --> 00:05:43,244
[CYRUS] So if the cold chain goes dark tonight —

101
00:05:43,244 --> 00:05:48,311
[JEFF] Civilization has a very bad week. I mean, within days you're looking at food spoilage

102
00:05:48,311 --> 00:05:53,378
[JEFF] on a scale that would be — it's actually hard to model. And here's the

103
00:05:53,378 --> 00:05:58,444
[JEFF] thing that should sit with people: thirteen percent of all food produced globally is lost

104
00:05:58,444 --> 00:06:03,511
[JEFF] right now to inadequate refrigeration. Thirteen percent. Enough to feed nearly a billion people. The

105
00:06:03,511 --> 00:06:08,578
[JEFF] cold chain fixed hunger for the rich world. It's still not there for the poor

106
00:06:08,578 --> 00:06:13,645
[JEFF] one.

107
00:06:13,645 --> 00:06:19,245
[CYRUS] So we built this completely essential system — and we still haven't finished it.

108
00:06:19,245 --> 00:06:24,045
[JEFF] We still haven't finished it. And we started from a guy shipping pond ice to

109
00:06:24,045 --> 00:06:28,845
[JEFF] the Caribbean on a ship nobody wanted to crew.

110
00:06:28,845 --> 00:06:33,745
[CYRUS] Okay so before we get into how we got here — I want to make

111
00:06:33,745 --> 00:06:38,645
[CYRUS] sure people understand what "before" actually felt like. Because I think it's easy to hear

112
00:06:38,645 --> 00:06:43,545
[CYRUS] "before refrigeration" and think "oh, things were a little less convenient." But it was way

113
00:06:43,545 --> 00:06:48,445
[CYRUS] more serious than that.

114
00:06:48,445 --> 00:06:52,845
[JEFF] Yeah, the way I think about this is — the World Before refrigeration isn't just

115
00:06:52,845 --> 00:06:57,245
[JEFF] inconvenient. In some cases, it's actively deadly.

116
00:06:57,245 --> 00:07:00,045
[CYRUS] And you have a story about this.

117
00:07:00,045 --> 00:07:04,311
[JEFF] I do. So let's go to New York City. Eighteen-fifties. The city's booming — over

118
00:07:04,311 --> 00:07:08,578
[JEFF] five hundred thousand people. And half of all children in the city die before their

119
00:07:08,578 --> 00:07:12,845
[JEFF] fifth birthday.

120
00:07:12,845 --> 00:07:13,245
[CYRUS] Half.

121
00:07:13,245 --> 00:07:18,559
[JEFF] Half. And a primary killer is milk. Not because the cows are sick — well,

122
00:07:18,559 --> 00:07:23,873
[JEFF] actually, sometimes because the cows are sick — but because of what they're being fed

123
00:07:23,873 --> 00:07:29,187
[JEFF] and where they're living. So here's what's happening. There are these large distilleries operating across

124
00:07:29,187 --> 00:07:34,502
[JEFF] Manhattan and Brooklyn. And they have this side business where they pen cows — hundreds

125
00:07:34,502 --> 00:07:39,816
[JEFF] of them — in sheds attached directly to the distillery. No pasture, no sunlight. The

126
00:07:39,816 --> 00:07:45,130
[JEFF] cows never go outside. And they're fed nothing but hot distillery slop. The leftover mash

127
00:07:45,130 --> 00:07:50,445
[JEFF] from whiskey production.

128
00:07:50,445 --> 00:07:52,445
[CYRUS] They're feeding cows whiskey waste.

129
00:07:52,445 --> 00:07:57,805
[JEFF] Just the waste, yeah. The hot, steaming leftover mash. And the cows develop open sores.

130
00:07:57,805 --> 00:08:03,165
[JEFF] Their tails literally rot and fall off from disease. Many lose their teeth. They just

131
00:08:03,165 --> 00:08:08,525
[JEFF] stand there in their own filth, shoulder to shoulder, producing milk until they drop dead.

132
00:08:08,525 --> 00:08:13,885
[JEFF] And THAT milk — the milk from these wrecked, diseased cows — that's what's being

133
00:08:13,885 --> 00:08:19,245
[JEFF] sold to the families of New York.

134
00:08:19,245 --> 00:08:23,645
[CYRUS] But — someone would notice, right? If the milk looked wrong?

135
00:08:23,645 --> 00:08:29,005
[JEFF] Ah. Well. The vendors — and this is the part I keep coming back to,

136
00:08:29,005 --> 00:08:34,365
[JEFF] because this detail is the one that got me — the vendors are doctoring the

137
00:08:34,365 --> 00:08:39,725
[JEFF] milk. They add chalk to whiten it. Flour and starch to thicken it. Eggs, Plaster

138
00:08:39,725 --> 00:08:45,085
[JEFF] of Paris, water. A dash of molasses to sweeten it. So what mothers buy for

139
00:08:45,085 --> 00:08:50,445
[JEFF] their babies looks like perfectly normal milk.

140
00:08:50,445 --> 00:08:52,045
[CYRUS] Oh, that is monstrous.

141
00:08:52,045 --> 00:08:57,325
[JEFF] Between fifty and eighty percent of all milk sold in major northeastern cities is this

142
00:08:57,325 --> 00:09:02,605
[JEFF] "swill milk." And an estimated eight thousand babies die per year in New York City

143
00:09:02,605 --> 00:09:07,885
[JEFF] alone from what's in it — tuberculosis, cholera, other pathogens. That's a contemporary estimate from

144
00:09:07,885 --> 00:09:13,165
[JEFF] the New York Times, not a modern recalculation. So I want to be precise: that's

145
00:09:13,165 --> 00:09:18,445
[JEFF] what they believed at the time.

146
00:09:18,445 --> 00:09:20,045
[CYRUS] And this gets exposed?

147
00:09:20,045 --> 00:09:25,645
[JEFF] On May eighth, eighteen fifty-eight. A journalist named Frank Leslie runs a five-thousand-word investigation in

148
00:09:25,645 --> 00:09:31,245
[JEFF] his illustrated newspaper. He calls the distillery dairies "milk murderers." He runs illustrations of the

149
00:09:31,245 --> 00:09:36,845
[JEFF] conditions — cows standing in their own filth, tails gone. He asks readers directly: "Are

150
00:09:36,845 --> 00:09:42,445
[JEFF] you aware what kind of milk you are drinking?" The New York Times follows with

151
00:09:42,445 --> 00:09:48,045
[JEFF] an editorial. The headline is "How We Poison Our Children."

152
00:09:48,045 --> 00:09:49,645
[CYRUS] And what's the solution?

153
00:09:49,645 --> 00:09:55,245
[JEFF] Well, that's the thing. There's no workaround. You can make people shop for food every

154
00:09:55,245 --> 00:10:00,845
[JEFF] morning. You can have them buy the meat the day they're going to eat it

155
00:10:00,845 --> 00:10:06,445
[JEFF] — there are no leftovers in the modern sense, because food won't last overnight in

156
00:10:06,445 --> 00:10:12,045
[JEFF] summer. You can pickle things, dry them, jam them. But you cannot make milk safe.

157
00:10:12,045 --> 00:10:17,645
[JEFF] You cannot see the bacteria. The icebox existed — it was a wooden cabinet with

158
00:10:17,645 --> 00:10:23,245
[JEFF] a block of melting natural ice in it — but it slowed spoilage. It didn't

159
00:10:23,245 --> 00:10:28,845
[JEFF] stop it. And the poorest families, who could only afford swill milk at six cents

160
00:10:28,845 --> 00:10:34,445
[JEFF] a quart, had no options at all.

161
00:10:34,445 --> 00:10:38,045
[CYRUS] I mean — six cents a quart of poison.

162
00:10:38,045 --> 00:10:39,245
[JEFF] For your baby.

163
00:10:39,245 --> 00:10:41,645
[CYRUS] The World Before is bad.

164
00:10:41,645 --> 00:10:47,200
[JEFF] The World Before is really bad. And I want to say one more thing about

165
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:52,756
[JEFF] this, because I think it gets lost. The World Before isn't just tragic — it's

166
00:10:52,756 --> 00:10:58,311
[JEFF] also different in ways that aren't just negative. There's this whole elaborate food culture built

167
00:10:58,311 --> 00:11:03,867
[JEFF] around the fact that cold doesn't exist. Seasonal eating is absolute — you eat what's

168
00:11:03,867 --> 00:11:09,422
[JEFF] growing where you are, when it's available. Eggs are scarce in fall and winter. Pickling,

169
00:11:09,422 --> 00:11:14,978
[JEFF] smoking, fermenting — these aren't artisanal hobbies, they're survival technologies that people have refined over

170
00:11:14,978 --> 00:11:20,533
[JEFF] generations. The iceman coming every morning is a daily social institution. When refrigeration arrives, it's

171
00:11:20,533 --> 00:11:26,089
[JEFF] going to sweep all of that away. The solution is going to change the culture

172
00:11:26,089 --> 00:11:31,644
[JEFF] as much as the problem.

173
00:11:31,644 --> 00:11:36,444
[CYRUS] Alright. So where does it start? Because clearly this gets solved. Eventually.

174
00:11:36,444 --> 00:11:42,244
[JEFF] It does. In waves. And each wave is actually its own drama — its own

175
00:11:42,244 --> 00:11:48,044
[JEFF] breakthrough, its own resistance, its own casualties. Here's what we're going to cover today.

176
00:11:48,044 --> 00:11:53,530
[JEFF] Wave One is the Ice Trade. And this one starts with Tudor — the guy

177
00:11:53,530 --> 00:11:59,016
[JEFF] we already met. But the story gets bigger and wilder than one obsessive with a

178
00:11:59,016 --> 00:12:04,502
[JEFF] boat. He builds a global empire from frozen pond water. Ships ice sixteen thousand miles

179
00:12:04,502 --> 00:12:09,987
[JEFF] to Calcutta. Gets the whole world hooked on cold for the first time. And then

180
00:12:09,987 --> 00:12:15,473
[JEFF] nature stops cooperating. This is Frederic Tudor's story, and it's a story about how you

181
00:12:15,473 --> 00:12:20,959
[JEFF] manufacture desire out of nothing — and what happens when the supply chain you've built

182
00:12:20,959 --> 00:12:26,445
[JEFF] turns out to depend on weather.

183
00:12:26,445 --> 00:12:30,978
[CYRUS] I'll be reacting to that one because I had no idea how that story ends.

184
00:12:30,978 --> 00:12:35,511
[CYRUS] Like, I genuinely did not know where the Ice Trade goes, and it goes somewhere

185
00:12:35,511 --> 00:12:40,045
[CYRUS] I did not expect.

186
00:12:40,045 --> 00:12:42,045
[JEFF] Yeah. You will react.

187
00:12:42,045 --> 00:12:47,778
[CYRUS] Wave Two is the Machine Age. And this one... okay, this is my wave, and

188
00:12:47,778 --> 00:12:53,511
[CYRUS] here's why I wanted to tell it. The Ice Trade established the desire for cold.

189
00:12:53,511 --> 00:12:59,245
[CYRUS] Wave Two is about what happens when a better technology comes along to meet that

190
00:12:59,245 --> 00:13:04,978
[CYRUS] desire — and the people who got rich off the old technology do everything in

191
00:13:04,978 --> 00:13:10,711
[CYRUS] their power to stop it. There's a doctor in Florida who invents the ice machine,

192
00:13:10,711 --> 00:13:16,445
[CYRUS] and the ice industry destroys him. There's a German professor selling ammonia machines to breweries

193
00:13:16,445 --> 00:13:22,178
[CYRUS] in the Midwest. And there's a Chicago meatpacker who finds a way to route around

194
00:13:22,178 --> 00:13:27,911
[CYRUS] every railroad company in America to ship dressed beef coast to coast. The machines work.

195
00:13:27,911 --> 00:13:33,645
[CYRUS] But they run on chemicals that can kill you.

196
00:13:33,645 --> 00:13:39,445
[JEFF] And this wave is where Cyrus's instincts really come in — because the resistance here

197
00:13:39,445 --> 00:13:45,245
[JEFF] isn't just stubbornness. The incumbents had real leverage. Real arguments. And they used both.

198
00:13:45,245 --> 00:13:50,845
[CYRUS] A hundred percent. The resisters were not stupid. That's kind of the whole point.

199
00:13:50,845 --> 00:13:56,495
[JEFF] Wave Three is Cold Comes Home. And this one is the most modern-feeling, which is

200
00:13:56,495 --> 00:14:02,145
[JEFF] maybe why it's the hardest to see clearly. A chemist inhales a mysterious gas and

201
00:14:02,145 --> 00:14:07,795
[JEFF] blows out a candle — that's the moment refrigerators become safe enough for every kitchen

202
00:14:07,795 --> 00:14:13,445
[JEFF] in America. Adoption explodes during the Great Depression, which seems backward until you understand why.

203
00:14:13,445 --> 00:14:19,095
[JEFF] An orphaned, self-taught Black inventor puts refrigeration on trucks and completes the cold chain. And

204
00:14:19,095 --> 00:14:24,745
[JEFF] then the twist, which I genuinely did not see coming when I first read this:

205
00:14:24,745 --> 00:14:30,395
[JEFF] the chemical that made it all safe? It's quietly destroying the ozone layer. The solution

206
00:14:30,395 --> 00:14:36,045
[JEFF] created a problem that took decades to see.

207
00:14:36,045 --> 00:14:42,045
[CYRUS] And that's the through-line of the whole episode, right? Every wave, someone's right that the

208
00:14:42,045 --> 00:14:48,045
[CYRUS] resistance has a point. And every wave, the world becomes more dependent on cold anyway.

209
00:14:48,045 --> 00:14:52,711
[JEFF] Each wave asks the same question — can we trust manufactured cold? And the answer

210
00:14:52,711 --> 00:14:57,378
[JEFF] is never simple. The resisters were often right about the risks. They were just wrong

211
00:14:57,378 --> 00:15:02,045
[JEFF] about the direction of travel.

212
00:15:02,045 --> 00:15:07,245
[CYRUS] Alright. Let's go. Wave One. Tudor, the Ice Fool, and how one guy with a

213
00:15:07,245 --> 00:15:12,445
[CYRUS] terrible idea made the world want something it had never tasted.

214
00:15:12,445 --> 00:15:14,045
[JEFF] Let's do it.

215
00:09:39,617 --> 00:09:44,683
[JEFF] So we set up the world before cold — the swill milk, the daily shopping

216
00:09:44,683 --> 00:09:49,750
[JEFF] runs, the sense that summer was just a season you survived. Now I want to

217
00:09:49,750 --> 00:09:54,816
[JEFF] get into how all of that starts to change. And it starts with one of

218
00:09:54,816 --> 00:09:59,883
[JEFF] the most improbable business stories I have ever come across. Not improbable in a "wow,

219
00:09:59,883 --> 00:10:04,950
[JEFF] that was clever" way. Improbable in a "this man should not have succeeded and yet"

220
00:10:04,950 --> 00:10:10,016
[JEFF] way.

221
00:10:10,016 --> 00:10:13,616
[CYRUS] I love that framing. Who are we talking about?

222
00:10:13,616 --> 00:10:19,016
[JEFF] So you already met Tudor in our opening — the dock, eighteen oh-six, the Gazette

223
00:10:19,016 --> 00:10:24,416
[JEFF] joke. But that scene started mid-story. I want to go back a year, because the

224
00:10:24,416 --> 00:10:29,816
[JEFF] thing that unlocks who he is happens at a dinner table. And the way I

225
00:10:29,816 --> 00:10:35,216
[JEFF] think about Frederic Tudor — the thing that makes the whole story make sense —

226
00:10:35,216 --> 00:10:40,616
[JEFF] is this: he is the guy at the dinner table who hears a joke, and

227
00:10:40,616 --> 00:10:46,016
[JEFF] instead of laughing, starts taking notes.

228
00:10:46,016 --> 00:10:47,616
[CYRUS] What was the joke?

229
00:10:47,616 --> 00:10:53,456
[JEFF] So it's around eighteen oh five. Tudor is twenty-two years old. He's at dinner with

230
00:10:53,456 --> 00:10:59,296
[JEFF] his family, and his brother William — just riffing, making conversation — says something like,

231
00:10:59,296 --> 00:11:05,136
[JEFF] wouldn't it be something if we could ship ice from the ponds here in New

232
00:11:05,136 --> 00:11:10,976
[JEFF] England down to the Caribbean? Where it's hot and everyone would love it? And everyone

233
00:11:10,976 --> 00:11:16,816
[JEFF] at the table laughs. Classic dinner-party idea. Totally absurd. And William moves on.

234
00:11:16,816 --> 00:11:18,016
[CYRUS] But Frederic doesn't.

235
00:11:18,016 --> 00:11:23,696
[JEFF] Frederic does not move on. Frederic goes home and starts a journal. And in that

236
00:11:23,696 --> 00:11:29,376
[JEFF] journal, he begins mapping out, seriously, how you would actually do this. How you'd cut

237
00:11:29,376 --> 00:11:35,056
[JEFF] the ice, how you'd insulate it, how you'd ship it, how you'd store it on

238
00:11:35,056 --> 00:11:40,736
[JEFF] arrival. He spends the next two years trying to get a ship captain — any

239
00:11:40,736 --> 00:11:46,416
[JEFF] ship captain in Boston — to carry this cargo to Martinique.

240
00:11:46,416 --> 00:11:50,416
[CYRUS] What was the objection? Like, why wouldn't they take it?

241
00:11:50,416 --> 00:11:55,750
[JEFF] Two things. First, they thought the melting ice would flood the hold and sink the

242
00:11:55,750 --> 00:12:01,083
[JEFF] ship. Second — and this is the part I find really interesting — they just

243
00:12:01,083 --> 00:12:06,416
[JEFF] thought the whole thing was lunacy. There was no framework for "ice as a commodity."

244
00:12:06,416 --> 00:12:11,750
[JEFF] Ice was something you scraped off a pond. It was worthless. The idea that you'd

245
00:12:11,750 --> 00:12:17,083
[JEFF] load it onto a ship and sail it to the tropics was, in their view,

246
00:12:17,083 --> 00:12:22,417
[JEFF] just... not a serious idea.

247
00:12:22,417 --> 00:12:24,417
[CYRUS] So what does he do?

248
00:12:24,417 --> 00:12:29,817
[JEFF] He buys his own ship. He can't find anyone to take the cargo, so he

249
00:12:29,817 --> 00:12:35,217
[JEFF] literally purchases a brig called the Favorite, loads it with a hundred and thirty tons

250
00:12:35,217 --> 00:12:40,617
[JEFF] of ice cut from a pond near Saugus, Massachusetts, and sails for Martinique. It's February,

251
00:12:40,617 --> 00:12:46,017
[JEFF] eighteen oh six. Tudor is twenty-three years old.

252
00:12:46,017 --> 00:12:49,217
[CYRUS] That is... a commitment to a dinner-table joke.

253
00:12:49,217 --> 00:12:55,083
[JEFF] Right? And the Boston Gazette — when the ship clears customs — runs a little

254
00:12:55,083 --> 00:13:00,950
[JEFF] item. They write: "No JOKE. A vessel has cleared at the Custom House for Martinique

255
00:13:00,950 --> 00:13:06,816
[JEFF] with a cargo of ice. We hope this will not prove a slippery speculation."

256
00:13:06,816 --> 00:13:11,216
[CYRUS] "Slippery speculation." That's actually pretty good for eighteen oh six.

257
00:13:11,216 --> 00:13:16,931
[JEFF] They were enjoying themselves. The press absolutely thought this man was out of his mind.

258
00:13:16,931 --> 00:13:22,645
[JEFF] And here's the thing — the first voyage is a disaster. The ice gets to

259
00:13:22,645 --> 00:13:28,359
[JEFF] Martinique, but there's no icehouse to put it in. Nobody had built anywhere to store

260
00:13:28,359 --> 00:13:34,074
[JEFF] it. So it just melts. Tudor loses money. He goes back to Boston and tries

261
00:13:34,074 --> 00:13:39,788
[JEFF] again. And again. He goes to debtor's prison at least twice in the next seven

262
00:13:39,788 --> 00:13:45,502
[JEFF] years. At one point, he can't leave the state of Massachusetts because of his creditors.

263
00:13:45,502 --> 00:13:51,216
[JEFF] He becomes known, publicly, in Boston, as the Ice Fool.

264
00:13:51,216 --> 00:13:54,616
[CYRUS] So he's not a triumphant visionary at this point. He's a guy who people are

265
00:13:54,616 --> 00:13:58,016
[CYRUS] laughing at.

266
00:13:58,016 --> 00:14:03,216
[JEFF] He's a cautionary tale. And this is the part I keep coming back to. During

267
00:14:03,216 --> 00:14:08,416
[JEFF] one of those stretches — debtor's prison, everyone thinks he's nuts — he writes in

268
00:14:08,416 --> 00:14:13,616
[JEFF] his diary: "He who gives back at the first repulse and without striking the second

269
00:14:13,616 --> 00:14:18,816
[JEFF] blow despairs of success, has never been, is not, and never will be a hero

270
00:14:18,816 --> 00:14:24,016
[JEFF] in war, love or business."

271
00:14:24,016 --> 00:14:27,216
[CYRUS] He's writing himself motivational quotes in debtor's prison.

272
00:14:27,216 --> 00:14:32,950
[JEFF] He is. And look — I want to be honest about this, because I think

273
00:14:32,950 --> 00:14:38,683
[JEFF] about it a lot. There's a real survivorship bias in that story. A lot of

274
00:14:38,683 --> 00:14:44,416
[JEFF] people have written that exact diary entry, in that exact circumstance, and stayed broke and

275
00:14:44,416 --> 00:14:50,150
[JEFF] failed and nobody ever read their journals. Tudor is the one who made it. That

276
00:14:50,150 --> 00:14:55,883
[JEFF] doesn't make his persistence meaningless. But it does mean "the obsessive who refused to quit"

277
00:14:55,883 --> 00:15:01,616
[JEFF] is not a formula — it's a description of one outcome.

278
00:15:01,616 --> 00:15:05,016
[CYRUS] That's fair. What actually changed? Like, what turns Tudor from the Ice Fool into the

279
00:15:05,016 --> 00:15:08,416
[CYRUS] Ice King?

280
00:15:08,416 --> 00:15:14,216
[JEFF] Two things. One is a technical insight. One is a marketing insight. And together they're

281
00:15:14,216 --> 00:15:20,016
[JEFF] what make the whole thing work. The technical insight is Nathaniel Wyeth. Wyeth runs the

282
00:15:20,016 --> 00:15:25,816
[JEFF] Fresh Pond Hotel in Cambridge. He becomes Tudor's foreman, managing the ice-cutting operations at Fresh

283
00:15:25,816 --> 00:15:31,616
[JEFF] Pond. And around eighteen twenty-five, Wyeth invents what's called the horse-drawn ice plow.

284
00:15:31,616 --> 00:15:32,416
[CYRUS] What's that?

285
00:15:32,416 --> 00:15:37,731
[JEFF] So before Wyeth, you harvested ice the way you'd imagine — men with hand saws,

286
00:15:37,731 --> 00:15:43,045
[JEFF] scoring the surface and cutting blocks. It's slow, it's expensive, it's inconsistent. What Wyeth builds

287
00:15:43,045 --> 00:15:48,359
[JEFF] is essentially two parallel iron runners about twenty inches apart, with saw teeth along the

288
00:15:48,359 --> 00:15:53,674
[JEFF] bottom, pulled by a horse across the surface of the pond. The horse drags the

289
00:15:53,674 --> 00:15:58,988
[JEFF] thing back and forth in a grid pattern — scores the ice perfectly, uniform blocks,

290
00:15:58,988 --> 00:16:04,302
[JEFF] twenty inches by twenty inches, all the way across the pond. Then you just pop

291
00:16:04,302 --> 00:16:09,616
[JEFF] the blocks out.

292
00:16:09,616 --> 00:16:12,416
[CYRUS] How much does that change the economics?

293
00:16:12,416 --> 00:16:17,016
[JEFF] It cuts harvesting costs from thirty cents a ton to ten cents a ton. Triples

294
00:16:17,016 --> 00:16:21,616
[JEFF] efficiency. Suddenly you can harvest at scale that was impossible by hand. Before Wyeth's plow,

295
00:16:21,616 --> 00:16:26,216
[JEFF] Tudor could dream about shipping ice to Calcutta. After it, he could actually do the

296
00:16:26,216 --> 00:16:30,816
[JEFF] math.

297
00:16:30,816 --> 00:16:36,816
[CYRUS] And Wyeth — he's basically the co-founder. He's the engineer who makes the vision real.

298
00:16:36,816 --> 00:16:42,350
[JEFF] He's exactly that. And here's what I find kind of haunting about it. Tudor becomes

299
00:16:42,350 --> 00:16:47,883
[JEFF] the Ice King — that's his actual nickname, what history remembers him as. He dies

300
00:16:47,883 --> 00:16:53,416
[JEFF] in eighteen sixty-four worth the equivalent of about two hundred million dollars in today's money.

301
00:16:53,416 --> 00:16:58,950
[JEFF] Total vindication. The family joke made good. And Nathaniel Wyeth, the man who invented the

302
00:16:58,950 --> 00:17:04,483
[JEFF] technology that made all of it possible, dies in eighteen fifty-six without any real public

303
00:17:04,483 --> 00:17:10,016
[JEFF] recognition. History remembers Tudor. Almost nobody knows Wyeth.

304
00:17:10,016 --> 00:17:12,816
[CYRUS] The engineer who made the visionary possible.

305
00:17:12,816 --> 00:17:17,616
[JEFF] Yeah. I mean, it's a pattern you see over and over again. The person with

306
00:17:17,616 --> 00:17:22,416
[JEFF] the story tends to get remembered. The person who built the thing the story runs

307
00:17:22,416 --> 00:17:27,216
[JEFF] on... tends not to. Wyeth's name is on a museum for antique ice tools. That's

308
00:17:27,216 --> 00:17:32,017
[JEFF] pretty much it.

309
00:17:32,017 --> 00:17:38,017
[CYRUS] Okay. So you've got the plow — that's the technical fix. What's the marketing insight?

310
00:17:38,017 --> 00:17:43,317
[JEFF] This is the part where Tudor genuinely impresses me as a thinker. Because the problem

311
00:17:43,317 --> 00:17:48,617
[JEFF] he's facing in new markets — Martinique, Havana, Charleston, New Orleans — isn't just logistics.

312
00:17:48,617 --> 00:17:53,917
[JEFF] It's that nobody there has ever tasted a cold drink. They have no frame of

313
00:17:53,917 --> 00:17:59,217
[JEFF] reference. You can't want something you've never experienced.

314
00:17:59,217 --> 00:18:02,417
[CYRUS] Right. You can't sell demand that doesn't exist.

315
00:18:02,417 --> 00:18:07,317
[JEFF] Exactly. So Tudor's solution — and this is the line I want to make sure

316
00:18:07,317 --> 00:18:12,217
[JEFF] we quote directly, because he writes it down himself — is: "A man who has

317
00:18:12,217 --> 00:18:17,117
[JEFF] drank his drinks cold at the same expense for one week can never be presented

318
00:18:17,117 --> 00:18:22,017
[JEFF] with them warm again."

319
00:18:22,017 --> 00:18:24,817
[CYRUS] Oh that's good. That's a real insight.

320
00:18:24,817 --> 00:18:30,217
[JEFF] It is a real insight. And the strategy that comes from it is: give the

321
00:18:30,217 --> 00:18:35,617
[JEFF] ice away. He goes into every new market and gives ice for free to the

322
00:18:35,617 --> 00:18:41,017
[JEFF] bartenders. To the tavern owners. And the customers taste cold drinks for the first time.

323
00:18:41,017 --> 00:18:46,417
[JEFF] And after that, they want them. And they'll pay for them. Tudor doesn't ask people

324
00:18:46,417 --> 00:18:51,817
[JEFF] if they want cold. He puts it in their hands and lets them discover that

325
00:18:51,817 --> 00:18:57,217
[JEFF] they can't imagine life without it.

326
00:18:57,217 --> 00:19:00,817
[CYRUS] He manufactured the craving before he charged for it.

327
00:19:00,817 --> 00:19:06,177
[JEFF] That's exactly right. And once you understand that, the whole story makes sense. Tudor isn't

328
00:19:06,177 --> 00:19:11,537
[JEFF] selling ice. He's selling an expectation. He's making cold feel like a basic feature of

329
00:19:11,537 --> 00:19:16,897
[JEFF] life. And once you've done that — once enough people believe that cold drinks are

330
00:19:16,897 --> 00:19:22,257
[JEFF] just what drinks are supposed to be — you've got something much more valuable than

331
00:19:22,257 --> 00:19:27,617
[JEFF] a product. You've got a civilization-level habit.

332
00:19:27,617 --> 00:19:33,083
[CYRUS] Okay, I have to ask. Because that journey — from Boston harbor to New England

333
00:19:33,083 --> 00:19:38,550
[CYRUS] to the Caribbean — feels almost manageable, even if it took decades to get right.

334
00:19:38,550 --> 00:19:44,017
[CYRUS] But I keep reading about Calcutta. That's a different category entirely.

335
00:19:44,017 --> 00:19:49,331
[JEFF] Yeah. That is a different category. Cyrus, I want to tell you about one specific

336
00:19:49,331 --> 00:19:54,645
[JEFF] voyage, because I think it captures everything about what Tudor was doing at the peak

337
00:19:54,645 --> 00:19:59,959
[JEFF] of his ambition. And even knowing how it turns out — it still sounds impossible.

338
00:19:59,959 --> 00:20:05,274
[JEFF] It's May twelfth, eighteen thirty-three. Tudor is fifty years old. He's been at this for

339
00:20:05,274 --> 00:20:10,588
[JEFF] twenty-seven years. He's conquered the Caribbean and the American South. Cold drinks are the norm

340
00:20:10,588 --> 00:20:15,902
[JEFF] in New Orleans and Charleston. The ice trade is real now. But he's been thinking

341
00:20:15,902 --> 00:20:21,217
[JEFF] about India.

342
00:20:21,217 --> 00:20:21,617
[CYRUS] India.

343
00:20:21,617 --> 00:20:27,517
[JEFF] Calcutta, specifically. Halfway around the world. The voyage — Boston to Calcutta, via the Cape

344
00:20:27,517 --> 00:20:33,417
[JEFF] of Good Hope — is sixteen thousand miles. It will take four months. Through tropical

345
00:20:33,417 --> 00:20:39,317
[JEFF] heat, essentially the entire way. He loads a brig called the Tuscany with a hundred

346
00:20:39,317 --> 00:20:45,217
[JEFF] and eighty tons of New England ice, packed in sawdust. And he sends it.

347
00:20:45,217 --> 00:20:49,017
[CYRUS] Hold on. Four months. In tropical heat. He's sending ice across the equator, around Africa,

348
00:20:49,017 --> 00:20:52,817
[CYRUS] across the Indian Ocean.

349
00:20:52,817 --> 00:20:53,217
[JEFF] Correct.

350
00:20:53,217 --> 00:20:59,217
[CYRUS] And he lost how much of it and still thought this was going to work?

351
00:20:59,217 --> 00:21:05,217
[JEFF] So — and this is what gets me — he loses about eighty tons. The

352
00:21:05,217 --> 00:21:11,217
[JEFF] Tuscany arrives in Calcutta on September thirteenth, eighteen thirty-three. Four months and seven days after

353
00:21:11,217 --> 00:21:17,217
[JEFF] it left Boston. And of the hundred and eighty tons loaded... roughly a hundred survive.

354
00:21:17,217 --> 00:21:21,217
[CYRUS] He loses nearly half his cargo. And it's still profitable?

355
00:21:21,217 --> 00:21:26,861
[JEFF] It is still profitable. Because the price of ice in Calcutta — having never existed

356
00:21:26,861 --> 00:21:32,505
[JEFF] before — is high enough that a hundred tons of surviving cargo more than covers

357
00:21:32,505 --> 00:21:38,150
[JEFF] the voyage. But here's the part I find extraordinary. There are crowds at the port

358
00:21:38,150 --> 00:21:43,794
[JEFF] when the Tuscany arrives. Rumors have spread through the city. A lot of people in

359
00:21:43,794 --> 00:21:49,439
[JEFF] Calcutta believe the whole thing is an elaborate joke — ice from the other side

360
00:21:49,439 --> 00:21:55,083
[JEFF] of the world? — and they've come to watch the joke land. When they actually

361
00:21:55,083 --> 00:22:00,728
[JEFF] unload the blocks, observers call them "crystal blocks of Yankee coldness." And one Indian onlooker

362
00:22:00,728 --> 00:22:06,372
[JEFF] touches a block of ice for the first time and believes he's been burnt. He

363
00:22:06,372 --> 00:22:12,017
[JEFF] thinks it's so cold it burned him.

364
00:22:12,017 --> 00:22:16,817
[CYRUS] ... He thought he was burnt. He thought ice was burning him.

365
00:22:16,817 --> 00:22:21,350
[JEFF] Another man asks Captain Littlefield — the captain of the Tuscany — whether ice grows

366
00:22:21,350 --> 00:22:25,883
[JEFF] on trees in America. He's trying to understand where this substance comes from, and trees

367
00:22:25,883 --> 00:22:30,417
[JEFF] is his best hypothesis.

368
00:22:30,417 --> 00:22:35,017
[CYRUS] I mean — what else would you think? You've never seen ice. You're in Calcutta

369
00:22:35,017 --> 00:22:39,617
[CYRUS] in September. It has to come from somewhere.

370
00:22:39,617 --> 00:22:45,217
[JEFF] The Calcutta government is so excited by the shipment that they grant it duty-free status

371
00:22:45,217 --> 00:22:50,817
[JEFF] on the spot. They allow night unloading — skip the customs formalities — just to

372
00:22:50,817 --> 00:22:56,417
[JEFF] get the ice ashore faster. And within three days of the ship's arrival, Calcutta's British

373
00:22:56,417 --> 00:23:02,017
[JEFF] residents have raised enough money to commission a proper icehouse. The proprietors start displaying a

374
00:23:02,017 --> 00:23:07,617
[JEFF] huge block of ice behind a glass window every day, replacing it regularly. It becomes

375
00:23:07,617 --> 00:23:13,217
[JEFF] a spectacle. People come just to look at it.

376
00:23:13,217 --> 00:23:16,017
[CYRUS] Tudor built a market in three days.

377
00:23:16,017 --> 00:23:21,137
[JEFF] He built a market in three days. And Calcutta becomes one of his most profitable

378
00:23:21,137 --> 00:23:26,257
[JEFF] destinations for the next twenty years — an estimated two hundred and twenty thousand dollars

379
00:23:26,257 --> 00:23:31,377
[JEFF] in profits over that period. He eventually builds three icehouses in India: Calcutta, Bombay, and

380
00:23:31,377 --> 00:23:36,497
[JEFF] Madras. The Madras ice house, built on Marina Beach, still survives today. It's a museum

381
00:23:36,497 --> 00:23:41,617
[JEFF] now — Vivekananda House.

382
00:23:41,617 --> 00:23:46,617
[CYRUS] Huh. So there's a building standing in India right now that exists because a twenty-three-year-old

383
00:23:46,617 --> 00:23:51,617
[CYRUS] in Boston refused to let go of a dinner-table joke.

384
00:23:51,617 --> 00:23:57,550
[JEFF] There is. And there's a line from Henry David Thoreau that I think is just

385
00:23:57,550 --> 00:24:03,483
[JEFF] — I mean, when I read this, I had to put the book down for

386
00:24:03,483 --> 00:24:09,417
[JEFF] a second. Thoreau is watching ice harvesters working at Walden Pond, a few miles from

387
00:24:09,417 --> 00:24:15,350
[JEFF] where Tudor started all of this. And he writes: "Thus it appears that the sweltering

388
00:24:15,350 --> 00:24:21,283
[JEFF] inhabitants of Charleston and New Orleans, of Madras and Bombay and Calcutta, drink at my

389
00:24:21,283 --> 00:24:27,216
[JEFF] well. The pure Walden water is mingled with the sacred water of the Ganges."

390
00:24:27,216 --> 00:24:28,416
[CYRUS] That's beautiful.

391
00:24:28,416 --> 00:24:33,116
[JEFF] Right? Thoreau is watching something he considers a pastoral scene — ice cutters on his

392
00:24:33,116 --> 00:24:37,816
[JEFF] pond — and he traces the line all the way to India. He understands that

393
00:24:37,816 --> 00:24:42,516
[JEFF] this ordinary thing happening in front of him is connected to the other side of

394
00:24:42,516 --> 00:24:47,216
[JEFF] the world.

395
00:24:47,216 --> 00:24:52,816
[CYRUS] Okay. So walk me through what this industry actually looks like at its peak, because

396
00:24:52,816 --> 00:24:58,416
[CYRUS] I want to understand the scale before we get to what goes wrong.

397
00:24:58,416 --> 00:25:03,516
[JEFF] Yeah, so — by the eighteen fifties, the natural ice trade is not a curiosity

398
00:25:03,516 --> 00:25:08,616
[JEFF] anymore. It is a pillar of American commercial life. Think about the numbers. Ninety thousand

399
00:25:08,616 --> 00:25:13,716
[JEFF] workers. Twenty-five thousand horses. The industry is capitalized at the equivalent of six hundred and

400
00:25:13,716 --> 00:25:18,816
[JEFF] sixty million dollars in today's money.

401
00:25:18,816 --> 00:25:20,416
[CYRUS] That's an enormous industry.

402
00:25:20,416 --> 00:25:25,856
[JEFF] It's second only to cotton in American shipping tonnage before the Civil War. Think about

403
00:25:25,856 --> 00:25:31,296
[JEFF] that. Cotton is the engine of the antebellum Southern economy, one of the great commodities

404
00:25:31,296 --> 00:25:36,736
[JEFF] of the era. And ice — just behind it. In New York City, fifteen hundred

405
00:25:36,736 --> 00:25:42,176
[JEFF] ice delivery trucks are making their rounds every single day. Two-thirds of households in Boston

406
00:25:42,176 --> 00:25:47,616
[JEFF] and New York are getting daily ice deliveries.

407
00:25:47,616 --> 00:25:50,816
[CYRUS] So it's not a luxury at this point.

408
00:25:50,816 --> 00:25:56,350
[JEFF] It's infrastructure. That's the shift that happens in the eighteen fifties. It's not "rich people

409
00:25:56,350 --> 00:26:01,883
[JEFF] get ice delivered" — it's that if you live in an American city and you're

410
00:26:01,883 --> 00:26:07,416
[JEFF] middle class, you expect an iceman at your door. He comes in the morning, you

411
00:26:07,416 --> 00:26:12,950
[JEFF] tell him how big a block you need, he carries it on his back in

412
00:26:12,950 --> 00:26:18,483
[JEFF] a leather apron and puts it in your icebox. You go about your day. The

413
00:26:18,483 --> 00:26:24,016
[JEFF] iceman becomes as routine as the mail carrier.

414
00:26:24,016 --> 00:26:30,016
[CYRUS] And on the Hudson River — that's where most of New York's ice comes from?

415
00:26:30,016 --> 00:26:34,616
[JEFF] Most of it. Between Poughkeepsie and Albany, there are a hundred and thirty-five major ice

416
00:26:34,616 --> 00:26:39,216
[JEFF] warehouses along the river. They harvest in winter, pack in sawdust, store in those massive

417
00:26:39,216 --> 00:26:43,816
[JEFF] icehouses, and deliver through the summer. It's a remarkably sophisticated supply chain for the eighteen

418
00:26:43,816 --> 00:26:48,416
[JEFF] hundreds.

419
00:26:48,416 --> 00:26:51,616
[CYRUS] And all of it runs on one assumption.

420
00:26:51,616 --> 00:26:54,816
[JEFF] All of it runs on one assumption. That winter will come. That the river will

421
00:26:54,816 --> 00:26:58,016
[JEFF] freeze.

422
00:26:58,016 --> 00:27:02,950
[CYRUS] So let me ask this as a business question, because it's the question I keep

423
00:27:02,950 --> 00:27:07,883
[CYRUS] circling. What happens to a business whose entire supply chain is dependent on weather? Like

424
00:27:07,883 --> 00:27:12,816
[CYRUS] — eventually, this has to break, right?

425
00:27:12,816 --> 00:27:18,350
[JEFF] It breaks. And here's the thing — it breaks in exactly the way you'd expect,

426
00:27:18,350 --> 00:27:23,883
[JEFF] which somehow doesn't make it less catastrophic when it happens. Let me tell you about

427
00:27:23,883 --> 00:27:29,416
[JEFF] the winter of eighteen eighty-nine into eighteen ninety. Cyrus, I've been reading about the ice

428
00:27:29,416 --> 00:27:34,950
[JEFF] trade for weeks now, and the Ice Famine of eighteen ninety is the detail I

429
00:27:34,950 --> 00:27:40,483
[JEFF] could not get out of my head. Because it's not just a bad winter. It's

430
00:27:40,483 --> 00:27:46,016
[JEFF] the moment the industry's whole premise gets exposed.

431
00:27:46,016 --> 00:27:46,816
[CYRUS] What happened?

432
00:27:46,816 --> 00:27:52,016
[JEFF] The winter of eighteen eighty-nine to eighteen ninety is one of the warmest on record.

433
00:27:52,016 --> 00:27:57,216
[JEFF] Temperatures in January hit the highest ever recorded in some areas. And the Hudson River

434
00:27:57,216 --> 00:28:02,416
[JEFF] — the backbone of New York's ice supply — doesn't freeze. The great ice fields

435
00:28:02,416 --> 00:28:07,616
[JEFF] simply don't form. There's nothing to harvest.

436
00:28:07,616 --> 00:28:08,016
[CYRUS] Nothing?

437
00:28:08,016 --> 00:28:13,716
[JEFF] Nothing. The Knickerbocker Ice Company — New York's dominant supplier — scrambles. They try to

438
00:28:13,716 --> 00:28:19,416
[JEFF] import ice from upstate, from further inland, eventually from Maine, where some ice has actually

439
00:28:19,416 --> 00:28:25,116
[JEFF] formed. But the transportation costs are brutal, and Maine's supply can't come close to meeting

440
00:28:25,116 --> 00:28:30,816
[JEFF] New York's appetite — which, remember, is three million tons per year.

441
00:28:30,816 --> 00:28:32,016
[CYRUS] Three million tons.

442
00:28:32,016 --> 00:28:37,536
[JEFF] Three million tons. That's what New York City consumes in a year. And the supply

443
00:28:37,536 --> 00:28:43,056
[JEFF] collapses almost completely. The price of ice in New York goes to ten dollars a

444
00:28:43,056 --> 00:28:48,576
[JEFF] ton. In Cincinnati, it hits twenty dollars a ton. A newspaper in Savannah — smugly,

445
00:28:48,576 --> 00:28:54,096
[JEFF] I might add — runs an item noting that Georgia's ice prices are far lower:

446
00:28:54,096 --> 00:28:59,616
[JEFF] five to seven fifty a ton, despite ninety-degree days.

447
00:28:59,616 --> 00:29:01,616
[CYRUS] Why is Savannah doing fine?

448
00:29:01,616 --> 00:29:06,216
[JEFF] Because Southern cities, facing warmer winters and more supply unreliability, have been quietly investing in

449
00:29:06,216 --> 00:29:10,816
[JEFF] mechanical ice-making machines for years. The South is better prepared than the North — which

450
00:29:10,816 --> 00:29:15,416
[JEFF] is a genuinely surreal sentence to write about any kind of technological infrastructure in eighteen

451
00:29:15,416 --> 00:29:20,016
[JEFF] ninety.

452
00:29:20,016 --> 00:29:24,216
[CYRUS] That's a great detail. The South, having spent decades with worse natural ice supply, actually

453
00:29:24,216 --> 00:29:28,416
[CYRUS] ran toward the solution faster.

454
00:29:28,416 --> 00:29:33,856
[JEFF] Right. They had to. And the irony doesn't land fully until you realize that this

455
00:29:33,856 --> 00:29:39,296
[JEFF] is the moment the South has a structural advantage because they faced the problem first.

456
00:29:39,296 --> 00:29:44,736
[JEFF] But here's the part of the famine that really gets me. The hardest hit are

457
00:29:44,736 --> 00:29:50,176
[JEFF] not the wealthy families — they can absorb the price increase. The hardest hit are

458
00:29:50,176 --> 00:29:55,616
[JEFF] the urban poor. People who bought ice the way they bought coal — by the

459
00:29:55,616 --> 00:30:01,056
[JEFF] bucket, in small amounts, every day, at the worst margins. Those families are now paying

460
00:30:01,056 --> 00:30:06,496
[JEFF] the highest price increases, in absolute terms, to protect their food. Hospitals start giving out

461
00:30:06,496 --> 00:30:11,936
[JEFF] ice for free during heat waves. On at least one occasion, that leads to what

462
00:30:11,936 --> 00:30:17,376
[JEFF] the newspapers describe as an ice riot — crowds fighting for limited supplies at distribution

463
00:30:17,376 --> 00:30:22,816
[JEFF] points.

464
00:30:22,816 --> 00:30:24,016
[CYRUS] An ice riot.

465
00:30:24,016 --> 00:30:28,016
[JEFF] An ice riot. In New York City. In eighteen ninety. Because there isn't enough frozen

466
00:30:28,016 --> 00:30:32,016
[JEFF] river water to go around.

467
00:30:32,016 --> 00:30:36,816
[CYRUS] ... And ninety thousand workers in this industry. What happens to them?

468
00:30:36,816 --> 00:30:42,656
[JEFF] This is the part of the story that kind of haunts me. Because I went

469
00:30:42,656 --> 00:30:48,496
[JEFF] looking for that answer — what happened to the ninety thousand workers when the natural

470
00:30:48,496 --> 00:30:54,336
[JEFF] ice trade collapses over the next few decades? And the honest answer is: we don't

471
00:30:54,336 --> 00:31:00,176
[JEFF] really know. There are almost no accounts. No records of specific support programs. No organized

472
00:31:00,176 --> 00:31:06,016
[JEFF] assistance. No newspaper features about displaced ice harvesters trying to find new work.

473
00:31:06,016 --> 00:31:06,416
[CYRUS] Nothing?

474
00:31:06,416 --> 00:31:11,776
[JEFF] Almost nothing. Because ice harvesting was mostly seasonal supplemental labor. Farmers in winter. River workers.

475
00:31:11,776 --> 00:31:17,136
[JEFF] Brickyard guys who needed winter income. When the ice trade declined — and it declines

476
00:31:17,136 --> 00:31:22,496
[JEFF] over forty years, not all at once — most of those people just... quietly lost

477
00:31:22,496 --> 00:31:27,856
[JEFF] a piece of their income and went back to doing other things. There's no single

478
00:31:27,856 --> 00:31:33,216
[JEFF] dramatic moment of displacement to write about.

479
00:31:33,216 --> 00:31:38,016
[CYRUS] An entire workforce of ninety thousand people just disappears from the record.

480
00:31:38,016 --> 00:31:44,016
[JEFF] We don't even know what happened to them. And the ice warehouses along the Hudson

481
00:31:44,016 --> 00:31:50,016
[JEFF] — those great huge insulated buildings — most of them eventually just get abandoned. A

482
00:31:50,016 --> 00:31:56,016
[JEFF] lot of them are destroyed by lightning. The communities that built their winter economy around

483
00:31:56,016 --> 00:32:02,016
[JEFF] ice harvesting lose that piece of their livelihood over decades. And nobody writes it down.

484
00:32:02,016 --> 00:32:06,716
[CYRUS] Okay. I want to make sure we give the resisters their due here, because I

485
00:32:06,716 --> 00:32:11,416
[CYRUS] think the story of the ice trade is unusual. Most of the time, when we

486
00:32:11,416 --> 00:32:16,116
[CYRUS] talk about resistance to a new technology, there's an incumbent fighting back. But Tudor's situation

487
00:32:16,116 --> 00:32:20,816
[CYRUS] is different.

488
00:32:20,816 --> 00:32:25,716
[JEFF] Yeah, you're right about that. Tudor's first wave of resistance is almost entirely conceptual. The

489
00:32:25,716 --> 00:32:30,616
[JEFF] incumbent isn't an industry — it's a belief system. The ship captains who refused to

490
00:32:30,616 --> 00:32:35,516
[JEFF] carry his cargo weren't protecting a business. They genuinely believed it was impossible. That's a

491
00:32:35,516 --> 00:32:40,416
[JEFF] different kind of resistance.

492
00:32:40,416 --> 00:32:45,216
[CYRUS] And in tropical markets, the consumers themselves — they're not rejecting something they had before.

493
00:32:45,216 --> 00:32:50,016
[CYRUS] They're being asked to want something they've never experienced.

494
00:32:50,016 --> 00:32:55,816
[JEFF] And that's actually the harder problem. You can argue with an incumbent. You can undercut

495
00:32:55,816 --> 00:33:01,616
[JEFF] their price. You can show that your thing works and theirs doesn't. But how do

496
00:33:01,616 --> 00:33:07,416
[JEFF] you argue someone into wanting something they've literally never tasted? Tudor's answer is: you don't

497
00:33:07,416 --> 00:33:13,216
[JEFF] argue. You give it to them. You let the experience make the argument.

498
00:33:13,216 --> 00:33:18,416
[CYRUS] Which is why the free ice to bartenders is such a genius move.

499
00:33:18,416 --> 00:33:24,016
[JEFF] It's one of the great go-to-market strategies in business history, I think. He's not asking

500
00:33:24,016 --> 00:33:29,616
[JEFF] "would you like to try cold drinks?" He's just making cold drinks exist. And then

501
00:33:29,616 --> 00:33:35,216
[JEFF] the question of whether to go back to warm becomes almost unthinkable.

502
00:33:35,216 --> 00:33:38,816
[CYRUS] There's something almost uncomfortable about it, right? Like — it's brilliant, but it's also... he's

503
00:33:38,816 --> 00:33:42,416
[CYRUS] engineering a dependency.

504
00:33:42,416 --> 00:33:48,016
[JEFF] He absolutely is. And he knows it. He writes it down explicitly: once you've tasted

505
00:33:48,016 --> 00:33:53,616
[JEFF] cold, you can't want warm. He's not discovering a preference — he's installing one. And

506
00:33:53,616 --> 00:33:59,216
[JEFF] he knows that once installed, it can't be uninstalled. That's the power of it. That's

507
00:33:59,216 --> 00:34:04,816
[JEFF] also what makes it, over time, the world's problem. Because you can't un-want cold once

508
00:34:04,816 --> 00:34:10,416
[JEFF] you've tasted it. But you can run out of winter.

509
00:34:10,416 --> 00:34:14,016
[CYRUS] By the time the famine hits in eighteen ninety, what does the industry actually look

510
00:34:14,016 --> 00:34:17,616
[CYRUS] like on paper?

511
00:34:17,616 --> 00:34:22,816
[JEFF] At the moment of peak — late eighteen eighties — it's enormous. Ninety thousand workers,

512
00:34:22,816 --> 00:34:28,016
[JEFF] twenty-five thousand horses. But underneath that scale, there are cracks that everyone who's paying attention

513
00:34:28,016 --> 00:34:33,216
[JEFF] can see. First: the supply is purely weather-dependent. You can insulate against summer losses with

514
00:34:33,216 --> 00:34:38,416
[JEFF] good icehouses. You cannot insulate against a warm January. Second, and this is the one

515
00:34:38,416 --> 00:34:43,616
[JEFF] that kills the industry's moral argument just as much as warm winters: the ice itself

516
00:34:43,616 --> 00:34:48,816
[JEFF] is getting dirty.

517
00:34:48,816 --> 00:34:50,816
[CYRUS] What do you mean dirty?

518
00:34:50,816 --> 00:34:56,302
[JEFF] The Hudson River — the primary source for New York's ice — is getting fouled

519
00:34:56,302 --> 00:35:01,788
[JEFF] with industrial waste. Sewage. Factory runoff. Through the eighteen eighties and nineties, the quality of

520
00:35:01,788 --> 00:35:07,274
[JEFF] the natural ice is genuinely declining. The industry's whole marketing position had been "natural ice

521
00:35:07,274 --> 00:35:12,759
[JEFF] is purer than anything a machine could make." That argument requires the ice to actually

522
00:35:12,759 --> 00:35:18,245
[JEFF] be pure. By nineteen oh seven, New York specialists are declaring Hudson River ice potentially

523
00:35:18,245 --> 00:35:23,731
[JEFF] contaminated with typhoid germs. In nineteen oh nine, the state Department of Health condemns forty-one

524
00:35:23,731 --> 00:35:29,216
[JEFF] icehouses around Albany, Troy, and Rensselaer.

525
00:35:29,216 --> 00:35:32,616
[CYRUS] So the industry spent decades attacking mechanical refrigeration as "unnatural." And they ended up selling

526
00:35:32,616 --> 00:35:36,016
[CYRUS] typhoid ice.

527
00:35:36,016 --> 00:35:41,883
[JEFF] That's the irony. The thing that was supposed to be the argument for natural ice

528
00:35:41,883 --> 00:35:47,750
[JEFF] — its purity — becomes the argument against it. And by the time that happens,

529
00:35:47,750 --> 00:35:53,616
[JEFF] there's an alternative. The famines of eighteen eighty and eighteen ninety don't kill the industry

530
00:35:53,616 --> 00:35:59,483
[JEFF] overnight. The industry dies slowly over the next thirty years. But those warm winters are

531
00:35:59,483 --> 00:36:05,350
[JEFF] the diagnosis. They're the moment the world discovers that it has built an addiction —

532
00:36:05,350 --> 00:36:11,216
[JEFF] a real, deep, civilization-level need for cold — on something it cannot control.

533
00:36:11,216 --> 00:36:14,016
[CYRUS] And that's the bridge to Wave Two.

534
00:36:14,016 --> 00:36:19,916
[JEFF] That's exactly the bridge. The world now expects cold. Two-thirds of American urban households depend

535
00:36:19,916 --> 00:36:25,816
[JEFF] on daily ice. But nature can't be trusted to supply it on schedule. The ice

536
00:36:25,816 --> 00:36:31,716
[JEFF] itself is becoming contaminated. Someone needs to build a machine — a real machine —

537
00:36:31,716 --> 00:36:37,616
[JEFF] that makes cold on demand. Whatever the weather. Whatever the season. Whatever the river.

538
00:36:37,616 --> 00:36:41,016
[CYRUS] And there's someone who tried, right? Who actually built that machine — and got destroyed

539
00:36:41,016 --> 00:36:44,416
[CYRUS] for it.

540
00:36:44,416 --> 00:36:50,416
[JEFF] There's someone who tried and built it and got destroyed for it. His name was

541
00:36:50,416 --> 00:36:56,416
[JEFF] John Gorrie. He was a doctor in Florida. And the natural ice industry, at the

542
00:36:56,416 --> 00:37:02,416
[JEFF] height of its power, saw him coming and decided to make an example of him.

543
00:37:02,416 --> 00:37:07,016
[CYRUS] We'll get to Gorrie in a minute. But first — before we leave Wave One

544
00:37:07,016 --> 00:37:11,616
[CYRUS] entirely — I want to land this. Tudor dies worth two hundred million dollars, equivalent.

545
00:37:11,616 --> 00:37:16,216
[CYRUS] He goes from the Ice Fool to the Ice King. And what does he leave

546
00:37:16,216 --> 00:37:20,816
[CYRUS] behind?

547
00:37:20,816 --> 00:37:26,416
[JEFF] He leaves behind an expectation. That is his real legacy. Not the ice. Not the

548
00:37:26,416 --> 00:37:32,016
[JEFF] warehouses. Not the ships. He leaves behind a world that believes cold is a feature

549
00:37:32,016 --> 00:37:37,616
[JEFF] of civilized life. Bartenders know to chill drinks. Households know to expect a delivery. Hotels

550
00:37:37,616 --> 00:37:43,216
[JEFF] know to have an icebox. Restaurants know that ice in a glass is not a

551
00:37:43,216 --> 00:37:48,816
[JEFF] novelty — it's the minimum standard. He manufactured a desire that had not existed before

552
00:37:48,816 --> 00:37:54,416
[JEFF] him. And once manufactured, that desire could never be undone. The question of who could

553
00:37:54,416 --> 00:38:00,016
[JEFF] actually satisfy it — reliably, at scale, on demand, regardless of the weather — that

554
00:38:00,016 --> 00:38:05,616
[JEFF] becomes the story of the next century.

555
00:38:05,616 --> 00:38:08,016
[CYRUS] That's the road into Wave Two.

556
00:38:08,016 --> 00:38:10,416
[JEFF] That's the road into Wave Two.

557
00:24:54,771 --> 00:24:58,371
[CYRUS] So Jeff — you ended Wave One with the ice industry on thin ice. Pun

558
00:24:58,371 --> 00:25:01,971
[CYRUS] very much intended.

559
00:25:01,971 --> 00:25:08,171
[JEFF] Yeah. The famine of eighteen ninety really was the moment the whole thing cracked. Ninety

560
00:25:08,171 --> 00:25:14,371
[JEFF] thousand workers. An entire economy built on winter. And then winter just... didn't show up.

561
00:25:14,371 --> 00:25:19,411
[CYRUS] And that's exactly where my part of this story picks up. Because what happens next

562
00:25:19,411 --> 00:25:24,451
[CYRUS] is fascinating, right? The world has now been addicted to cold. Tudor did that. But

563
00:25:24,451 --> 00:25:29,491
[CYRUS] the supply is unreliable. The rivers are getting filthy. And somewhere out there, engineers are

564
00:25:29,491 --> 00:25:34,531
[CYRUS] asking the obvious question: why does cold have to come from nature? Why can't we

565
00:25:34,531 --> 00:25:39,571
[CYRUS] just make it?

566
00:25:39,571 --> 00:25:43,971
[JEFF] Right? Like, we make heat whenever we want. Why not cold?

567
00:25:43,971 --> 00:25:49,571
[CYRUS] Exactly. And the answer to that question turns out to be one of the most

568
00:25:49,571 --> 00:25:55,171
[CYRUS] dramatic — and honestly, one of the most tragic — stories in this whole history.

569
00:25:55,171 --> 00:26:00,771
[CYRUS] Because the person who actually figured it out first? He didn't get rich. He didn't

570
00:26:00,771 --> 00:26:06,371
[CYRUS] get famous. He got destroyed. And I want to start there.

571
00:26:06,371 --> 00:26:07,571
[JEFF] Set it up.

572
00:26:07,571 --> 00:26:13,491
[CYRUS] So picture Apalachicola, Florida. Eighteen forties. It's a small city, but it's a prosperous one

573
00:26:13,491 --> 00:26:19,411
[CYRUS] — at that point, the third-largest port on the Gulf Coast. Cotton is moving through

574
00:26:19,411 --> 00:26:25,331
[CYRUS] this town. And the most respected man in Apalachicola is a physician named John Gorrie.

575
00:26:25,331 --> 00:26:31,251
[CYRUS] Doctor Gorrie is not just a doctor. He's the postmaster. He's on the city council.

576
00:26:31,251 --> 00:26:37,171
[CYRUS] He's a bank director. If Apalachicola had a mayor's table, Gorrie was at it.

577
00:26:37,171 --> 00:26:41,171
[JEFF] I love this setup. Pillar of the community. Completely established.

578
00:26:41,171 --> 00:26:47,171
[CYRUS] Completely established. And Apalachicola, like every Gulf Coast city in the eighteen forties, gets hammered

579
00:26:47,171 --> 00:26:53,171
[CYRUS] every summer by yellow fever and malaria. And Gorrie — this is the thing that

580
00:26:53,171 --> 00:26:59,171
[CYRUS] makes his story so specific — Gorrie becomes convinced that cold air can cure tropical

581
00:26:59,171 --> 00:27:05,171
[CYRUS] disease. This is pre-germ-theory. The prevailing idea is that bad air causes illness. And Gorrie

582
00:27:05,171 --> 00:27:11,171
[CYRUS] takes that framework and builds his own version: cool the air, you cool the sickness.

583
00:27:11,171 --> 00:27:16,771
[JEFF] So he's not just tinkering with a machine. He's trying to save his patients.

584
00:27:16,771 --> 00:27:22,304
[CYRUS] He's trying to save his patients. That's the whole story in one line. He starts

585
00:27:22,304 --> 00:27:27,837
[CYRUS] by hanging basins of ice from the ceilings of hospital rooms — letting cold air

586
00:27:27,837 --> 00:27:33,370
[CYRUS] sink down over feverish patients. It actually works, in the sense that it makes them

587
00:27:33,370 --> 00:27:38,904
[CYRUS] more comfortable. But ice in Florida is expensive. It has to be shipped from the

588
00:27:38,904 --> 00:27:44,437
[CYRUS] North. And it's unreliable. And so Gorrie starts asking: what if we didn't need natural

589
00:27:44,437 --> 00:27:49,970
[CYRUS] ice? What if you could make it mechanically?

590
00:27:49,970 --> 00:27:55,570
[JEFF] Which at the time would have sounded like saying, I'm going to bottle lightning.

591
00:27:55,570 --> 00:28:00,270
[CYRUS] Right? I'm going to bottle lightning. But here's what Gorrie figures out. And this is

592
00:28:00,270 --> 00:28:04,970
[CYRUS] the thing I want to make sure we explain properly — because the principle he

593
00:28:04,970 --> 00:28:09,670
[CYRUS] discovers is the principle that every refrigerator and every air conditioner on Earth still runs

594
00:28:09,670 --> 00:28:14,370
[CYRUS] on today.

595
00:28:14,370 --> 00:28:18,970
[JEFF] Okay — actually, before you get into Gorrie's machine, I want to make sure I

596
00:28:18,970 --> 00:28:23,570
[JEFF] have this right myself, because I'll admit the word "compression" gets thrown around and I

597
00:28:23,570 --> 00:28:28,170
[JEFF] don't always have a clear picture of what's actually happening. What is the actual mechanism

598
00:28:28,170 --> 00:28:32,770
[JEFF] here?

599
00:28:32,770 --> 00:28:37,810
[CYRUS] So here's the thing I couldn't wrap my head around until I actually read how

600
00:28:37,810 --> 00:28:42,850
[CYRUS] this works. And it genuinely surprised me — like, the mental model I had was

601
00:28:42,850 --> 00:28:47,890
[CYRUS] completely wrong. Because a refrigerator doesn't create cold. That's the thing that flips it. It

602
00:28:47,890 --> 00:28:52,930
[CYRUS] doesn't make cold — it moves heat. It pumps heat from inside the box to

603
00:28:52,930 --> 00:28:57,970
[CYRUS] outside the box.

604
00:28:57,970 --> 00:28:59,970
[JEFF] Okay. Walk me through that.

605
00:28:59,970 --> 00:29:05,370
[CYRUS] The best analogy I've seen for this is sweating. Like, think about what happens when

606
00:29:05,370 --> 00:29:10,770
[CYRUS] you sweat on a hot day. The sweat evaporates off your skin — and when

607
00:29:10,770 --> 00:29:16,170
[CYRUS] it evaporates, it absorbs heat from your body. That's why you feel cooler. Your body

608
00:29:16,170 --> 00:29:21,570
[CYRUS] is, like, literally losing heat to the evaporating moisture.

609
00:29:21,570 --> 00:29:26,170
[JEFF] Right. Which is why dry heat feels different from humid heat — in humidity the

610
00:29:26,170 --> 00:29:30,770
[JEFF] sweat can't evaporate so you can't cool down.

611
00:29:30,770 --> 00:29:36,199
[CYRUS] Exactly. So a refrigerator does the same thing, but with a special fluid in a

612
00:29:36,199 --> 00:29:41,628
[CYRUS] closed loop. The fluid — the refrigerant — evaporates inside the fridge, absorbing heat from

613
00:29:41,628 --> 00:29:47,056
[CYRUS] your food. The inside gets cooler. Then a compressor squeezes the gas back down, heats

614
00:29:47,056 --> 00:29:52,485
[CYRUS] it up, and the hot gas flows through coils on the outside of the machine

615
00:29:52,485 --> 00:29:57,913
[CYRUS] — like, those coils on the back of your refrigerator — and it releases the

616
00:29:57,913 --> 00:30:03,342
[CYRUS] heat into your kitchen. Then the gas condenses back into liquid, flows back inside, and

617
00:30:03,342 --> 00:30:08,770
[CYRUS] the whole cycle starts again.

618
00:30:08,770 --> 00:30:13,970
[JEFF] So the heat that was inside my refrigerator is now... in my kitchen.

619
00:30:13,970 --> 00:30:19,770
[CYRUS] It is literally in your kitchen. Your refrigerator is warming your kitchen slightly in order

620
00:30:19,770 --> 00:30:25,570
[CYRUS] to keep your food cold. It's just moving heat from one place to another.

621
00:30:25,570 --> 00:30:29,770
[JEFF] But wait — if that's the whole mechanism, why didn't someone build this in like

622
00:30:29,770 --> 00:30:33,970
[JEFF] seventeen fifty? What's the hard part?

623
00:30:33,970 --> 00:30:39,037
[CYRUS] That's exactly the question. And the answer is: the refrigerant. The fluid doing the work.

624
00:30:39,037 --> 00:30:44,104
[CYRUS] Because you need something that, like, evaporates at the right temperature, that can be compressed

625
00:30:44,104 --> 00:30:49,170
[CYRUS] back into a liquid, and that can be cycled through a machine reliably. And in

626
00:30:49,170 --> 00:30:54,237
[CYRUS] the eighteen forties, the options were ammonia, sulfur dioxide, and methyl chloride. All of which

627
00:30:54,237 --> 00:30:59,304
[CYRUS] work. All of which are toxic. All of which can kill a family if they

628
00:30:59,304 --> 00:31:04,370
[CYRUS] leak.

629
00:31:04,370 --> 00:31:08,770
[JEFF] So the machine is technically sound. The chemistry is the problem.

630
00:31:08,770 --> 00:31:13,970
[CYRUS] The machine is sound. The chemistry is the problem. And we're going to come back

631
00:31:13,970 --> 00:31:19,170
[CYRUS] to that, because it's the bridge to Wave Three. But Gorrie doesn't know any of

632
00:31:19,170 --> 00:31:24,370
[CYRUS] this yet. He just knows that if you compress air, release it rapidly, the expansion

633
00:31:24,370 --> 00:31:29,570
[CYRUS] produces intense cold. He builds a machine. It works. He makes ice, mechanically, in Apalachicola,

634
00:31:29,570 --> 00:31:34,770
[CYRUS] Florida, in the eighteen forties.

635
00:31:34,770 --> 00:31:35,970
[JEFF] That's genuinely extraordinary.

636
00:31:35,970 --> 00:31:41,570
[CYRUS] It's extraordinary. Okay — here is the story I could not get out of my

637
00:31:41,570 --> 00:31:47,170
[CYRUS] head while I was reading about this. Because what happens next is one of the

638
00:31:47,170 --> 00:31:52,770
[CYRUS] most brutal things in this whole history. On May sixth, eighteen fifty-one, Gorrie receives U.S.

639
00:31:52,770 --> 00:31:58,370
[CYRUS] Patent number eight thousand eighty for his ice-making machine. It's a genuine triumph. He demonstrates

640
00:31:58,370 --> 00:32:03,970
[CYRUS] it publicly. He's made ice mechanically. He's proved it works.

641
00:32:03,970 --> 00:32:05,570
[JEFF] So what goes wrong?

642
00:32:05,570 --> 00:32:10,704
[CYRUS] His financial backer dies. That same year. Eighteen fifty-one. The year of the patent. And

643
00:32:10,704 --> 00:32:15,837
[CYRUS] the northern ice industry — the ice kings and their successors, the people building the

644
00:32:15,837 --> 00:32:20,970
[CYRUS] empire that Tudor started — they see Gorrie's machine as an existential threat. And a

645
00:32:20,970 --> 00:32:26,104
[CYRUS] New York newspaper runs a line that became notorious. [deliberate] "There is a crank down

646
00:32:26,104 --> 00:32:31,237
[CYRUS] in Apalachicola, Florida, that thinks he can make ice by his machine as good as

647
00:32:31,237 --> 00:32:36,370
[CYRUS] God Almighty."

648
00:32:36,370 --> 00:32:39,770
[JEFF] He proved it. He made ice in Florida. In the eighteen forties. That's not a

649
00:32:39,770 --> 00:32:43,170
[JEFF] small thing.

650
00:32:43,170 --> 00:32:47,837
[CYRUS] Not "this technology is interesting but needs development." Not "let's see if the economics work."

651
00:32:47,837 --> 00:32:52,504
[CYRUS] "There is a crank." "As good as God Almighty." That's the framing. That this man

652
00:32:52,504 --> 00:32:57,170
[CYRUS] is both delusional and blasphemous.

653
00:32:57,170 --> 00:33:03,170
[JEFF] And with the backer gone, no new investor is going to step into that headwind.

654
00:33:03,170 --> 00:33:08,904
[CYRUS] No one will touch it. Gorrie suspects — and multiple sources reference this suspicion, though

655
00:33:08,904 --> 00:33:14,637
[CYRUS] there's no definitive proof — that the ice industry itself was behind the press campaign.

656
00:33:14,637 --> 00:33:20,370
[CYRUS] That the northern ice syndicate orchestrated a smear campaign to protect their monopoly.

657
00:33:20,370 --> 00:33:25,970
[JEFF] I mean, whether or not he could prove it, the effect was the same.

658
00:33:25,970 --> 00:33:31,437
[CYRUS] The effect was the same. The press attacks land. The backer is dead. Potential investors

659
00:33:31,437 --> 00:33:36,904
[CYRUS] stay away. Gorrie's financial situation collapses. He's sued by a London debt collector for over

660
00:33:36,904 --> 00:33:42,370
[CYRUS] six thousand five hundred dollars in unpaid interest — which is a crushing amount at

661
00:33:42,370 --> 00:33:47,837
[CYRUS] the time. And Gorrie, the most respected man in Apalachicola — the physician, the postmaster,

662
00:33:47,837 --> 00:33:53,304
[CYRUS] the bank director — suffers what his biographers describe as a nervous breakdown from which

663
00:33:53,304 --> 00:33:58,770
[CYRUS] he never recovered. He retreats into seclusion.

664
00:33:58,770 --> 00:34:00,370
[JEFF] At fifty-one years old.

665
00:34:00,370 --> 00:34:06,120
[CYRUS] He dies on June twenty-ninth, eighteen fifty-five. In seclusion, in Apalachicola. At fifty-one. Survived by

666
00:34:06,120 --> 00:34:11,870
[CYRUS] his wife Caroline and two children. He never profits from his invention. He is buried

667
00:34:11,870 --> 00:34:17,620
[CYRUS] in Magnolia Cemetery. And here is the part that I keep coming back to: today,

668
00:34:17,620 --> 00:34:23,370
[CYRUS] John Gorrie is one of Florida's two representatives in the National Statuary Hall at the

669
00:34:23,370 --> 00:34:29,120
[CYRUS] United States Capitol. His statue stands in Washington. There's a museum in Apalachicola with a

670
00:34:29,120 --> 00:34:34,870
[CYRUS] replica of his machine. The basic principle he invented — compress a gas, expand it,

671
00:34:34,870 --> 00:34:40,620
[CYRUS] produce cold — powers a three hundred billion dollar global industry. He was right. He

672
00:34:40,620 --> 00:34:46,370
[CYRUS] was completely right. And the world destroyed him for it.

673
00:34:46,370 --> 00:34:51,170
[JEFF] I think there's a real interesting intersection here with Tudor's story. Because Tudor also got

674
00:34:51,170 --> 00:34:55,970
[JEFF] mocked. The Boston Gazette, "this will not prove a slippery speculation." He went to debtor's

675
00:34:55,970 --> 00:35:00,770
[JEFF] prison. But Tudor had enough resources to keep going, long enough to win. Gorrie didn't

676
00:35:00,770 --> 00:35:05,570
[JEFF] have that runway.

677
00:35:05,570 --> 00:35:11,470
[CYRUS] And I'd add — Tudor had a commercial product anyone could understand. Cold drinks. The

678
00:35:11,470 --> 00:35:17,370
[CYRUS] value proposition was immediate. Gorrie's machine was competing against an existing industry with enormous political

679
00:35:17,370 --> 00:35:23,270
[CYRUS] and financial power. He needed investors at exactly the moment the press was calling him

680
00:35:23,270 --> 00:35:29,170
[CYRUS] a crank. The deck was stacked in a way that wasn't true for Tudor.

681
00:35:29,170 --> 00:35:34,290
[JEFF] Yeah, and look — I don't want to romanticize the persistence angle too much. There's

682
00:35:34,290 --> 00:35:39,410
[JEFF] a real survivorship bias in that framing. Plenty of inventors who were equally persistent, equally

683
00:35:39,410 --> 00:35:44,530
[JEFF] right, died broke and unknown and nobody built a museum for them. The thing that's

684
00:35:44,530 --> 00:35:49,650
[JEFF] tragic about Gorrie isn't just that he failed — it's that he was provably right,

685
00:35:49,650 --> 00:35:54,770
[JEFF] and he still failed.

686
00:35:54,770 --> 00:35:59,970
[CYRUS] A hundred percent. He was right. The machine worked. The patent was real. And the

687
00:35:59,970 --> 00:36:05,170
[CYRUS] mechanism that destroyed him wasn't a market failing to recognize value — it was an

688
00:36:05,170 --> 00:36:10,370
[CYRUS] incumbent industry actively suppressing it. That's a different thing.

689
00:36:10,370 --> 00:36:15,970
[JEFF] So what happens after Gorrie? Who finally figures out how to make this commercial?

690
00:36:15,970 --> 00:36:21,628
[CYRUS] So here's where the story gets interesting — and this is the thing that I

691
00:36:21,628 --> 00:36:27,285
[CYRUS] think is the key to how industrial technology actually diffuses. Because what Gorrie got wrong

692
00:36:27,285 --> 00:36:32,942
[CYRUS] wasn't the technology. What he got wrong was the market. He was trying to convince

693
00:36:32,942 --> 00:36:38,599
[CYRUS] the world that manufactured cold was better than natural ice. And that's a really hard

694
00:36:38,599 --> 00:36:44,256
[CYRUS] sell in eighteen fifty-one. But twenty years later, a German professor of engineering named Carl

695
00:36:44,256 --> 00:36:49,913
[CYRUS] von Linde figures out who actually needs cold so badly they'll pay for a machine

696
00:36:49,913 --> 00:36:55,570
[CYRUS] — and it's not the general public. It's breweries.

697
00:36:55,570 --> 00:36:55,970
[JEFF] Breweries.

698
00:36:55,970 --> 00:37:00,930
[CYRUS] Specifically Bavarian breweries. And here's why. Lager — the cold-fermented beer that would eventually dominate

699
00:37:00,930 --> 00:37:05,890
[CYRUS] global beer culture — lager requires cold fermentation. Consistent, reliable cold. And under Bavarian law

700
00:37:05,890 --> 00:37:10,850
[CYRUS] at the time, brewing was literally forbidden between April twenty-third and September twenty-ninth because warm

701
00:37:10,850 --> 00:37:15,810
[CYRUS] summer fermentation produced inferior, often unsafe beer. You couldn't make lager in summer. The law

702
00:37:15,810 --> 00:37:20,770
[CYRUS] said so.

703
00:37:20,770 --> 00:37:24,570
[JEFF] So there's a law that creates a pain point, and the pain point is perfectly

704
00:37:24,570 --> 00:37:28,370
[JEFF] calibrated for Linde's machine.

705
00:37:28,370 --> 00:37:34,104
[CYRUS] It's a perfect fit. Linde sells his first ammonia refrigerator to the Spaten Brewery in

706
00:37:34,104 --> 00:37:39,837
[CYRUS] Munich in eighteen seventy-three. And from there — I love this number — by eighteen

707
00:37:39,837 --> 00:37:45,570
[CYRUS] ninety, he has sold seven hundred and forty-seven machines. Almost all to breweries.

708
00:37:45,570 --> 00:37:48,370
[JEFF] Seven forty-seven. That's a very specific number.

709
00:37:48,370 --> 00:37:53,637
[CYRUS] Right? It's so specific it's obviously from the actual records. Seven hundred and forty-seven machines

710
00:37:53,637 --> 00:37:58,904
[CYRUS] in seventeen years. And he founds a company in eighteen seventy-nine — Linde AG —

711
00:37:58,904 --> 00:38:04,170
[CYRUS] that is still operating today as Linde plc, one of the largest industrial gas companies

712
00:38:04,170 --> 00:38:09,437
[CYRUS] in the world. Linde didn't try to change public psychology. He found the one market

713
00:38:09,437 --> 00:38:14,704
[CYRUS] where the pain was specific, measurable, and had no other solution. And he sold them

714
00:38:14,704 --> 00:38:19,970
[CYRUS] the solution. That's it.

715
00:38:19,970 --> 00:38:24,770
[JEFF] Yeah, I think that's a really important point. Gorrie was trying to change minds. Linde

716
00:38:24,770 --> 00:38:29,570
[JEFF] was solving a problem people already knew they had.

717
00:38:29,570 --> 00:38:34,570
[CYRUS] Distribution versus technology. You've said this before, right? The technology doesn't win just because it

718
00:38:34,570 --> 00:38:39,570
[CYRUS] works. It wins when it finds the right entry point.

719
00:38:39,570 --> 00:38:44,770
[JEFF] Product, product, product. And in this case: market fit, market fit, market fit.

720
00:38:44,770 --> 00:38:50,270
[CYRUS] Exactly. So by the eighteen eighties, industrial refrigeration is real. Breweries have it. Cold-storage warehouses

721
00:38:50,270 --> 00:38:55,770
[CYRUS] start appearing in the eighteen eighties — the first large-scale food preservation infrastructure that's not

722
00:38:55,770 --> 00:39:01,270
[CYRUS] ice-dependent. And then this guy from Cape Cod shows up in Chicago with an idea

723
00:39:01,270 --> 00:39:06,770
[CYRUS] that's going to blow up the entire American railroad system.

724
00:39:06,770 --> 00:39:07,570
[JEFF] Gustavus Swift.

725
00:39:07,570 --> 00:39:13,170
[CYRUS] Gustavus Swift. Born eighteen thirty-nine. Starts buying cattle at age fourteen on Cape Cod. Moves

726
00:39:13,170 --> 00:39:18,770
[CYRUS] to Chicago. And Swift has an insight that now seems completely obvious — but in

727
00:39:18,770 --> 00:39:24,370
[CYRUS] eighteen seventy-eight, it was genuinely radical. Like, he looks at the American cattle trade and

728
00:39:24,370 --> 00:39:29,970
[CYRUS] sees an enormous, systematic waste. Sixty percent of a live cow is waste — bones,

729
00:39:29,970 --> 00:39:35,570
[CYRUS] organs, hide. And the railroads were shipping all of it. Live cattle from the western

730
00:39:35,570 --> 00:39:41,170
[CYRUS] ranges to Chicago, then live cattle again by railroad from Chicago to Eastern cities, so

731
00:39:41,170 --> 00:39:46,770
[CYRUS] local butchers could do the slaughtering. Every trip: sixty percent of the cargo is stuff

732
00:39:46,770 --> 00:39:52,370
[CYRUS] that's going to be thrown away. Right?

733
00:39:52,370 --> 00:39:55,970
[JEFF] Which is absurd when you say it out loud.

734
00:39:55,970 --> 00:40:01,770
[CYRUS] It's absurd. So Swift's idea: slaughter the animal in Chicago. Ship only the dressed beef

735
00:40:01,770 --> 00:40:07,570
[CYRUS] — the forty percent that's actually edible. You save enormously on freight. He runs the

736
00:40:07,570 --> 00:40:13,370
[CYRUS] numbers: Swift can sell dressed beef seventy-five cents to a dollar cheaper per hundred pounds

737
00:40:13,370 --> 00:40:19,170
[CYRUS] than a local butcher selling meat slaughtered from live cattle shipped by rail.

738
00:40:19,170 --> 00:40:22,370
[JEFF] That's not a rounding error. That's not a slight efficiency improvement. That's a structural cost

739
00:40:22,370 --> 00:40:25,570
[JEFF] advantage.

740
00:40:25,570 --> 00:40:31,342
[CYRUS] That's a fundamental disruption of the economics. But — and here's the thing — to

741
00:40:31,342 --> 00:40:37,113
[CYRUS] do this, you need refrigerated railroad cars. You need to keep that meat cold on

742
00:40:37,113 --> 00:40:42,885
[CYRUS] the journey from Chicago to Boston. So in eighteen seventy-eight, Swift hires an engineer named

743
00:40:42,885 --> 00:40:48,656
[CYRUS] Andrew Chase to design a practical refrigerated railcar. And Chase's solution is actually ingenious: vertical

744
00:40:48,656 --> 00:40:54,428
[CYRUS] hanging racks for the carcasses, with ice cooling from bunkers in the ceiling. Cold air

745
00:40:54,428 --> 00:41:00,199
[CYRUS] sinks naturally — by gravity — over the hanging meat. It circulates evenly. No mechanical

746
00:41:00,199 --> 00:41:05,970
[CYRUS] assistance needed. You re-ice the cars at stations along the route.

747
00:41:05,970 --> 00:41:08,770
[JEFF] So the cooling system is basically passive.

748
00:41:08,770 --> 00:41:13,970
[CYRUS] Passive gravity-fed cooling. Simple. Reliable. And Swift goes to every major American railroad and asks

749
00:41:13,970 --> 00:41:19,170
[CYRUS] them to carry his refrigerated cars. Every single one says no.

750
00:41:19,170 --> 00:41:21,170
[JEFF] Of course they do.

751
00:41:21,170 --> 00:41:26,370
[CYRUS] Of course they do. Because think about it from the railroads' perspective — they have

752
00:41:26,370 --> 00:41:31,570
[CYRUS] invested, like, millions in live-cattle infrastructure. Stock cars specially designed for animals. Animal pens and

753
00:41:31,570 --> 00:41:36,770
[CYRUS] feeding stations along the entire route. The whole ecosystem is built around moving living cattle.

754
00:41:36,770 --> 00:41:41,970
[CYRUS] If dressed beef wins, all of that becomes worthless. They'd be writing off their own

755
00:41:41,970 --> 00:41:47,170
[CYRUS] investments. So they form what amounts to a cartel against Swift. We will not carry

756
00:41:47,170 --> 00:41:52,370
[CYRUS] your refrigerated cars.

757
00:41:52,370 --> 00:41:55,570
[JEFF] So the question is what Swift does next.

758
00:41:55,570 --> 00:42:01,104
[CYRUS] And this is the part I love. Because Swift doesn't fight the cartel. He goes

759
00:42:01,104 --> 00:42:06,637
[CYRUS] around it. He finds one railroad that had never invested heavily in live-cattle infrastructure. The

760
00:42:06,637 --> 00:42:12,170
[CYRUS] Grand Trunk Railway, which runs through Michigan and across Canada into the American Northeast. The

761
00:42:12,170 --> 00:42:17,704
[CYRUS] Grand Trunk had little income from transporting animals on the hoof. They had nothing to

762
00:42:17,704 --> 00:42:23,237
[CYRUS] lose by carrying Swift's cars. So Swift routes his dressed beef north through Michigan, across

763
00:42:23,237 --> 00:42:28,770
[CYRUS] Canada, and down into Boston and New York.

764
00:42:28,770 --> 00:42:31,970
[JEFF] Through Canada. To avoid an American railroad cartel.

765
00:42:31,970 --> 00:42:37,170
[CYRUS] Through Canada. In eighteen eighty, the Swift Refrigerator Line launches. Within a year: nearly two

766
00:42:37,170 --> 00:42:42,370
[CYRUS] hundred refrigerated cars. Three thousand carcasses a week shipped to Boston alone. Within six years

767
00:42:42,370 --> 00:42:47,570
[CYRUS] of starting, Swift is the second-largest meatpacker in America.

768
00:42:47,570 --> 00:42:51,370
[JEFF] That is a remarkable arc. From "every railroad said no" to second-largest meatpacker in the

769
00:42:51,370 --> 00:42:55,170
[JEFF] country in six years.

770
00:42:55,170 --> 00:43:00,542
[CYRUS] Six years. And at this point, the butchers are watching their business collapse in real

771
00:43:00,542 --> 00:43:05,913
[CYRUS] time. Local butchers had a monopoly — if you wanted fresh meat in Boston, you

772
00:43:05,913 --> 00:43:11,285
[CYRUS] bought from someone who slaughtered locally. That monopoly is gone. Swift's beef is cheaper and,

773
00:43:11,285 --> 00:43:16,656
[CYRUS] once consumers trust it, they buy it. And so in eighteen eighty-six, the butchers form

774
00:43:16,656 --> 00:43:22,028
[CYRUS] the National Butchers' Protective Association and organize a formal boycott. They warn consumers: this meat

775
00:43:22,028 --> 00:43:27,399
[CYRUS] was slaughtered in Chicago days ago. It could harbor all kinds of diseases. You don't

776
00:43:27,399 --> 00:43:32,771
[CYRUS] know where it's been.

777
00:43:32,771 --> 00:43:35,571
[JEFF] And that's not a completely unreasonable argument.

778
00:43:35,571 --> 00:43:41,071
[CYRUS] It's not. And I want to give the butchers their due here, because this is

779
00:43:41,071 --> 00:43:46,571
[CYRUS] the thing about resistance that I think this show has to keep saying: the resisters

780
00:43:46,571 --> 00:43:52,071
[CYRUS] were not stupid. Food safety regulation barely existed in eighteen eighty-six. There was no FDA.

781
00:43:52,071 --> 00:43:57,571
[CYRUS] There was no inspection system. When a local butcher slaughtered your beef this morning, you

782
00:43:57,571 --> 00:44:03,071
[CYRUS] could see it was fresh. When someone tells you this cow was slaughtered in Chicago

783
00:44:03,071 --> 00:44:08,571
[CYRUS] a week ago and shipped in a cold box — you don't have any way

784
00:44:08,571 --> 00:44:14,071
[CYRUS] to verify that. The question of whether Swift's beef was actually safe is a genuine

785
00:44:14,071 --> 00:44:19,571
[CYRUS] question, not a paranoid one.

786
00:44:19,571 --> 00:44:24,771
[JEFF] But the butchers also had an obvious financial interest in asking that question.

787
00:44:24,771 --> 00:44:29,571
[CYRUS] Of course. The argument was both reasonable and self-interested. Those two things can coexist. And

788
00:44:29,571 --> 00:44:34,371
[CYRUS] the butchers didn't just make arguments — they organized. They lobbied state legislatures. State laws

789
00:44:34,371 --> 00:44:39,171
[CYRUS] get passed trying to protect local butchers from the Chicago meatpackers. Congress investigates the "Beef

790
00:44:39,171 --> 00:44:43,971
[CYRUS] Trust" multiple times.

791
00:44:43,971 --> 00:44:45,971
[JEFF] So what breaks the resistance?

792
00:44:45,971 --> 00:44:51,504
[CYRUS] Economics. Swift can sell cheaper. Seventy-five cents to a dollar per hundred pounds. That's not

793
00:44:51,504 --> 00:44:57,037
[CYRUS] a marginal difference. And Swift fights back hard — he undercuts local butchers on price,

794
00:44:57,037 --> 00:45:02,571
[CYRUS] he bribes respected local butchers to switch sides and sell Chicago beef, he sells at

795
00:45:02,571 --> 00:45:08,104
[CYRUS] a loss to win customers. The price advantage is just too large to fight through

796
00:45:08,104 --> 00:45:13,637
[CYRUS] a boycott. One butcher testified before a Senate Committee in eighteen ninety: "Very few hold

797
00:45:13,637 --> 00:45:19,171
[CYRUS] on." That's the whole outcome in four words.

798
00:45:19,171 --> 00:45:21,571
[JEFF] "Very few hold on." That's devastating.

799
00:45:21,571 --> 00:45:27,121
[CYRUS] And I'll just note — while Swift is routing around the railroad cartel in America,

800
00:45:27,121 --> 00:45:32,671
[CYRUS] something is happening on the other side of the world at the same moment. February

801
00:45:32,671 --> 00:45:38,221
[CYRUS] second, eighteen eighty: the SS Strathleven arrives in London from Melbourne, nine weeks at sea,

802
00:45:38,221 --> 00:45:43,771
[CYRUS] with fifty tons of frozen beef and mutton. The Pall Mall Gazette says the meat

803
00:45:43,771 --> 00:45:49,321
[CYRUS] is in "admirable condition." A hundred and fifty percent profit. Someone had tried this exact

804
00:45:49,321 --> 00:45:54,871
[CYRUS] voyage in eighteen seventy-three — James Harrison, ice ran out mid-voyage, meat spoiled, he lost

805
00:45:54,871 --> 00:46:00,421
[CYRUS] everything. The Strathleven worked because the technology was better. Harrison was the Gorrie of frozen

806
00:46:00,421 --> 00:46:05,971
[CYRUS] shipping — right idea, wrong moment.

807
00:46:05,971 --> 00:46:09,171
[JEFF] So while Swift is routing around every American railroad, the global cold chain is being

808
00:46:09,171 --> 00:46:12,371
[JEFF] born.

809
00:46:12,371 --> 00:46:17,771
[CYRUS] Exactly. Okay — let me bring this wave home. Who wins, who loses, what changes

810
00:46:17,771 --> 00:46:23,171
[CYRUS] — and then set up why all of this still isn't enough.

811
00:46:23,171 --> 00:46:23,971
[JEFF] Who wins?

812
00:46:23,971 --> 00:46:29,891
[CYRUS] Swift wins. Obviously. From small operator to second-largest meatpacker in six years. Swift and Company

813
00:46:29,891 --> 00:46:35,811
[CYRUS] becomes a corporate giant. Chicago wins — the city is transformed from a prairie cattle

814
00:46:35,811 --> 00:46:41,731
[CYRUS] hub into the meatpacking capital of the world. The Union Stock Yards process millions of

815
00:46:41,731 --> 00:46:47,651
[CYRUS] animals a year. Breweries win — they can produce lager year-round, they can distribute nationally,

816
00:46:47,651 --> 00:46:53,571
[CYRUS] the whole beer economy changes. Linde wins — his company is still operating today.

817
00:46:53,571 --> 00:46:54,771
[JEFF] And who loses?

818
00:46:54,771 --> 00:47:00,371
[CYRUS] Ninety thousand ice workers. This is the number from Wave One, and it's still the

819
00:47:00,371 --> 00:47:05,971
[CYRUS] number here. An entire industry is being replaced by machines over the course of about

820
00:47:05,971 --> 00:47:11,571
[CYRUS] forty years. And there are no stories about what happened to those workers. The historical

821
00:47:11,571 --> 00:47:17,171
[CYRUS] record just... doesn't have them. I find that genuinely chilling. Ninety thousand people, and when

822
00:47:17,171 --> 00:47:22,771
[CYRUS] the industry dies, nobody wrote down what happened to them.

823
00:47:22,771 --> 00:47:28,237
[JEFF] Yeah — I think that's one of those things that we should sit with. It's

824
00:47:28,237 --> 00:47:33,704
[JEFF] not that nobody cared. It's that they were the kind of workers who don't generate

825
00:47:33,704 --> 00:47:39,171
[JEFF] historical records. Day laborers. Seasonal workers. People without names in newspapers.

826
00:47:39,171 --> 00:47:44,656
[CYRUS] Local butchers lose their monopoly on fresh meat. "Very few hold on." And here's the

827
00:47:44,656 --> 00:47:50,142
[CYRUS] one that doesn't get enough attention: the centralization of slaughter creates hellish conditions. When you

828
00:47:50,142 --> 00:47:55,628
[CYRUS] move all the killing to one place — Chicago, the Union Stock Yards — you

829
00:47:55,628 --> 00:48:01,113
[CYRUS] get economies of scale, yes. You also get what Upton Sinclair documented in The Jungle

830
00:48:01,113 --> 00:48:06,599
[CYRUS] in nineteen oh-six. Workers in dangerous conditions, corners cut on hygiene, the full dark side

831
00:48:06,599 --> 00:48:12,085
[CYRUS] of industrialized food production. Swift's disruption wasn't just efficient. It was efficient in ways that

832
00:48:12,085 --> 00:48:17,570
[CYRUS] created a new kind of exploitation.

833
00:48:17,570 --> 00:48:21,770
[JEFF] So the road not taken — I'm genuinely curious about this. If the railroad cartel

834
00:48:21,770 --> 00:48:25,970
[JEFF] had succeeded. If they'd blocked Swift.

835
00:48:25,970 --> 00:48:31,685
[CYRUS] I've thought about this. If the cartel had held, dressed beef would have developed much

836
00:48:31,685 --> 00:48:37,399
[CYRUS] more slowly. Regional meatpacking persists longer. Local butchers keep their monopoly. The Union Stock Yards

837
00:48:37,399 --> 00:48:43,113
[CYRUS] don't become what they became. Food safety regulation might have developed before the abuses it

838
00:48:43,113 --> 00:48:48,828
[CYRUS] was designed to address, rather than in response to them. The resisters were defending a

839
00:48:48,828 --> 00:48:54,542
[CYRUS] legitimate local food system as well as a monopoly. You could make an argument that

840
00:48:54,542 --> 00:49:00,256
[CYRUS] the world they were defending was in some ways healthier — more distributed, more accountable,

841
00:49:00,256 --> 00:49:05,971
[CYRUS] less brutal. It just also happened to be more expensive.

842
00:49:05,971 --> 00:49:08,771
[JEFF] And economic gravity doesn't care about healthier.

843
00:49:08,771 --> 00:49:13,571
[CYRUS] Economic gravity doesn't care. Seventy-five cents per hundred pounds wins. Every time.

844
00:49:13,571 --> 00:49:18,904
[CYRUS] Okay, so. Where does this leave us? Because I want to make sure we're clear

845
00:49:18,904 --> 00:49:24,237
[CYRUS] about the state of the world at this moment. It's the nineteen-twenties. Mechanical refrigeration works.

846
00:49:24,237 --> 00:49:29,571
[CYRUS] Breweries have it. Meatpackers have it. The cold-storage warehouses exist. The refrigerated railroad car is

847
00:49:29,571 --> 00:49:34,904
[CYRUS] everywhere. By nineteen fourteen — I love this number — artificial ice production has actually

848
00:49:34,904 --> 00:49:40,237
[CYRUS] surpassed natural ice harvesting in the United States. Twenty-six million tons artificial, twenty-four million tons

849
00:49:40,237 --> 00:49:45,571
[CYRUS] natural. The machines have won.

850
00:49:45,571 --> 00:49:50,904
[JEFF] The machines have won. And as we touched on earlier — the bitter coda is

851
00:49:50,904 --> 00:49:56,237
[JEFF] that the industry that spent decades attacking mechanical refrigeration as impure and unnatural ended up

852
00:49:56,237 --> 00:50:01,571
[JEFF] selling typhoid ice. The argument collapsed by its own weight.

853
00:50:01,571 --> 00:50:04,371
[JEFF] But it still doesn't work in homes.

854
00:50:04,371 --> 00:50:09,571
[CYRUS] It still doesn't work in homes. That's the thing. All of this — Linde, Swift,

855
00:50:09,571 --> 00:50:14,771
[CYRUS] the refrigerated railroad car, the cold-storage warehouse — all of it is industrial. Big machines.

856
00:50:14,771 --> 00:50:19,971
[CYRUS] Commercial applications. The factory gets cold. The kitchen doesn't.

857
00:50:19,971 --> 00:50:21,971
[JEFF] Because of the refrigerant problem.

858
00:50:21,971 --> 00:50:27,704
[CYRUS] Because of the refrigerant problem. And this is real. This isn't theoretical. In Cincinnati in

859
00:50:27,704 --> 00:50:33,437
[CYRUS] nineteen twenty, ammonia fumes escape from a butcher shop's ice machine at Eighteenth and Main

860
00:50:33,437 --> 00:50:39,171
[CYRUS] Streets — they have to evacuate an entire city block. In nineteen twenty-eight, a packing

861
00:50:39,171 --> 00:50:44,904
[CYRUS] company has a leak so bad it requires firefighters in gas masks. In nineteen twenty-nine,

862
00:50:44,904 --> 00:50:50,637
[CYRUS] methyl chloride deaths in Chicago prompt panic across the entire refrigeration industry. And the people

863
00:50:50,637 --> 00:50:56,371
[CYRUS] who are wealthy enough to buy early home refrigerators — they're putting them in their

864
00:50:56,371 --> 00:51:02,104
[CYRUS] backyards, or on their porches. Not inside their homes. Because they are terrified. And that

865
00:51:02,104 --> 00:51:07,837
[CYRUS] fear is entirely rational. Methyl chloride is colorless and nearly odorless. You wouldn't know it

866
00:51:07,837 --> 00:51:13,571
[CYRUS] was leaking until everyone in your family was dead.

867
00:51:13,571 --> 00:51:18,371
[JEFF] So the engineering problem is solved. The chemistry problem is still open.

868
00:51:18,371 --> 00:51:24,313
[CYRUS] The chemistry problem is open. And in Berlin, a family dies. They're killed by refrigerant

869
00:51:24,313 --> 00:51:30,256
[CYRUS] fumes leaking from their home refrigerator. And when Albert Einstein reads about it in the

870
00:51:30,256 --> 00:51:36,199
[CYRUS] newspaper, he is so disturbed by it that he and his former student, Leo Szilard,

871
00:51:36,199 --> 00:51:42,142
[CYRUS] spend the next eight years designing a safer refrigerator — one with no moving parts,

872
00:51:42,142 --> 00:51:48,085
[CYRUS] no toxic refrigerants. They patent it in nineteen thirty. Getting cold into the factory was

873
00:51:48,085 --> 00:51:54,028
[CYRUS] an engineering problem. Getting cold into the kitchen is a chemistry problem. Someone needs to

874
00:51:54,028 --> 00:51:59,971
[CYRUS] find a refrigerant that won't kill. And that story — that is Jeff's wave.

875
00:51:59,971 --> 00:52:04,237
[JEFF] Yeah. That's mine. And I'll just say — the person who solves it has one

876
00:52:04,237 --> 00:52:08,504
[JEFF] of the most dramatic stories in this whole history. And also one of the most

877
00:52:08,504 --> 00:52:12,771
[JEFF] consequential.

878
00:52:12,771 --> 00:52:18,171
[CYRUS] I will say nothing more. Except: the man who makes refrigerators safe for every kitchen

879
00:52:18,171 --> 00:52:23,571
[CYRUS] in America had already poisoned the planet once. Keep that in mind.

880
00:52:23,571 --> 00:52:26,371
[JEFF] ... That is a genuinely loaded setup.

881
00:52:26,371 --> 00:52:27,571
[CYRUS] That's Wave Three.

882
00:43:13,546 --> 00:43:18,746
[JEFF] Okay, so here's where this story becomes about you and me. Not in the abstract

883
00:43:18,746 --> 00:43:23,946
[JEFF] "technology shapes society" way — I mean literally about the appliance in your kitchen right

884
00:43:23,946 --> 00:43:29,146
[JEFF] now. This is the wave where refrigeration stops being something that happens in factories and

885
00:43:29,146 --> 00:43:34,346
[JEFF] warehouses and starts happening in people's homes.

886
00:43:34,346 --> 00:43:39,346
[CYRUS] Which sounds like it should have been easy. Like, you've already done the hard part

887
00:43:39,346 --> 00:43:44,346
[CYRUS] — you've got mechanical refrigeration working. Just... make it smaller.

888
00:43:44,346 --> 00:43:49,386
[JEFF] Yeah, I thought the same thing. And the answer is: it was not easy. Not

889
00:43:49,386 --> 00:43:54,426
[JEFF] even a little bit. Because it turns out that the refrigerants that worked great in

890
00:43:54,426 --> 00:43:59,466
[JEFF] big industrial systems were, in some cases, genuinely terrifying in a residential setting. We're talking

891
00:43:59,466 --> 00:44:04,506
[JEFF] about chemicals that could leak out of your refrigerator overnight and kill your whole family

892
00:44:04,506 --> 00:44:09,546
[JEFF] in their sleep.

893
00:44:09,546 --> 00:44:10,346
[CYRUS] Wait, what?

894
00:44:10,346 --> 00:44:13,546
[JEFF] I am not being dramatic. This actually happened.

895
00:44:13,546 --> 00:44:15,546
[CYRUS] Okay. Walk me through this.

896
00:44:15,546 --> 00:44:21,412
[JEFF] So the refrigerants that were in use in the nineteen twenties — ammonia, methyl chloride,

897
00:44:21,412 --> 00:44:27,279
[JEFF] sulfur dioxide — all of them worked on the basic principle we covered in the

898
00:44:27,279 --> 00:44:33,145
[JEFF] last wave. But here's the thing: in a brewery or a meatpacking plant, if a

899
00:44:33,145 --> 00:44:39,012
[JEFF] pipe blows, you've got workers who notice it and get out. In a house at

900
00:44:39,012 --> 00:44:44,879
[JEFF] two in the morning, you're asleep. And methyl chloride in particular is nearly colorless. Nearly

901
00:44:44,879 --> 00:44:50,745
[JEFF] odorless. You would never know it was leaking until it was too late.

902
00:44:50,745 --> 00:44:56,745
[CYRUS] That is a terrifying design flaw for something you're supposed to keep in your kitchen.

903
00:44:56,745 --> 00:45:02,745
[JEFF] Right? And the incidents were real. Cincinnati in nineteen twenty — ammonia fumes from a

904
00:45:02,745 --> 00:45:08,745
[JEFF] butcher shop's ice machine force the evacuation of an entire city block. Nineteen twenty-eight —

905
00:45:08,745 --> 00:45:14,745
[JEFF] a packing company leak, firefighters in gas masks. Chicago, August of nineteen twenty-nine — methyl

906
00:45:14,745 --> 00:45:20,745
[JEFF] chloride deaths that send the whole industry into a panic. In Berlin, an entire family

907
00:45:20,745 --> 00:45:26,745
[JEFF] is killed by fumes leaking from their home refrigerator overnight. The story makes the newspapers.

908
00:45:26,745 --> 00:45:29,145
[CYRUS] So people knew. This wasn't hidden.

909
00:45:29,145 --> 00:45:34,745
[JEFF] This was very publicly known. And here's the behavioral tell that I just love as

910
00:45:34,745 --> 00:45:40,345
[JEFF] a data point: families who could afford early refrigerators — and these things cost between

911
00:45:40,345 --> 00:45:45,945
[JEFF] three hundred and five hundred and twenty-five dollars, so we're talking about a significant purchase

912
00:45:45,945 --> 00:45:51,545
[JEFF] — those families were putting their refrigerators in the backyard. On the back porch. In

913
00:45:51,545 --> 00:45:57,145
[JEFF] an outbuilding. Because they didn't trust them inside the house.

914
00:45:57,145 --> 00:46:00,745
[CYRUS] That is the most honest product review I've ever heard. "Five stars — do not

915
00:46:00,745 --> 00:46:04,345
[CYRUS] put inside."

916
00:46:04,345 --> 00:46:09,305
[JEFF] Right? Like, I paid five hundred dollars for this, and I'm keeping it at a

917
00:46:09,305 --> 00:46:14,265
[JEFF] safe distance from my children. That's the state of the market in the late nineteen

918
00:46:14,265 --> 00:46:19,225
[JEFF] twenties. And it is — I want to be clear — that is not irrational.

919
00:46:19,225 --> 00:46:24,185
[JEFF] That is a completely reasonable response to a genuine danger. These people weren't paranoid; they

920
00:46:24,185 --> 00:46:29,145
[JEFF] were correct.

921
00:46:29,145 --> 00:46:31,945
[CYRUS] So the industry has a credibility problem.

922
00:46:31,945 --> 00:46:36,545
[JEFF] The industry has a credibility problem and a chemistry problem, and they're the same problem.

923
00:46:36,545 --> 00:46:41,145
[JEFF] And into this moment walks Thomas Midgley Junior.

924
00:46:41,145 --> 00:46:46,445
[JEFF] Okay, Cyrus. I want to tell you about one specific moment, because I think this

925
00:46:46,445 --> 00:46:51,745
[JEFF] might be the most consequential chemistry demonstration in history. And I mean that — I'm

926
00:46:51,745 --> 00:46:57,045
[JEFF] not reaching for a superlative. This one demonstration changed what ended up in your kitchen.

927
00:46:57,045 --> 00:47:02,345
[JEFF] And it did it in about sixty seconds.

928
00:47:02,345 --> 00:47:03,145
[CYRUS] I'm listening.

929
00:47:03,145 --> 00:47:08,105
[JEFF] It's April nineteen thirty. The American Chemical Society is meeting in Atlanta, Georgia. Thomas Midgley

930
00:47:08,105 --> 00:47:13,065
[JEFF] Junior takes the stage. He's already famous — not entirely for good reasons. He's the

931
00:47:13,065 --> 00:47:18,025
[JEFF] man who gave the world leaded gasoline. He'd actually suffered lead poisoning himself during that

932
00:47:18,025 --> 00:47:22,985
[JEFF] work. So this is a guy who has a complicated relationship with the chemistry of

933
00:47:22,985 --> 00:47:27,945
[JEFF] unintended consequences.

934
00:47:27,945 --> 00:47:30,345
[CYRUS] The resume does not inspire confidence.

935
00:47:30,345 --> 00:47:36,425
[JEFF] No, it does not. But here's what he does. He comes on stage carrying two

936
00:47:36,425 --> 00:47:42,505
[JEFF] things: a small vial, and a candle. He lights the candle. Then he pours the

937
00:47:42,505 --> 00:47:48,585
[JEFF] contents of the vial — this is a new chemical compound his team developed, chlorofluorocarbon,

938
00:47:48,585 --> 00:47:54,665
[JEFF] which he's calling Freon — he heats it to a boil. And then, in front

939
00:47:54,665 --> 00:48:00,745
[JEFF] of the entire assembled American Chemical Society, he takes a deep breath of the vapor.

940
00:48:00,745 --> 00:48:03,145
[CYRUS] He just... inhales it. On stage.

941
00:48:03,145 --> 00:48:07,945
[JEFF] He inhales it on stage. The whole room is watching. If this chemical is toxic,

942
00:48:07,945 --> 00:48:12,745
[JEFF] Midgley is about to collapse. He doesn't collapse. He exhales — directly onto the candle

943
00:48:12,745 --> 00:48:17,545
[JEFF] flame. And the flame goes out.

944
00:48:17,545 --> 00:48:19,545
[CYRUS] Oh, that's good theater.

945
00:48:19,545 --> 00:48:25,412
[JEFF] In one breath, he's proved two things simultaneously. Non-toxic — he just breathed it and

946
00:48:25,412 --> 00:48:31,279
[JEFF] he's fine. Non-flammable — he just used it to put out fire. No member of

947
00:48:31,279 --> 00:48:37,145
[JEFF] that audience can now argue that Freon is dangerous to have in your house.

948
00:48:37,145 --> 00:48:41,545
[CYRUS] That's a killer demo. Like, that is genuinely a killer demo.

949
00:48:41,545 --> 00:48:47,385
[JEFF] It is! And the thing is — it worked. Not just rhetorically. The chemistry was

950
00:48:47,385 --> 00:48:53,225
[JEFF] real. Freon really was non-toxic. It really was non-flammable. It was a genuine solution to

951
00:48:53,225 --> 00:48:59,065
[JEFF] a genuine problem. GM and DuPont immediately set up a joint venture called Kinetic Chemicals

952
00:48:59,065 --> 00:49:04,905
[JEFF] to manufacture and market CFCs. Within five years, manufacturers using Freon sell more than eight

953
00:49:04,905 --> 00:49:10,745
[JEFF] million new refrigerators nationally. Eight million. The adoption explosion begins with one breath.

954
00:49:10,745 --> 00:49:13,145
[CYRUS] So the safety problem was solved.

955
00:49:13,145 --> 00:49:18,517
[JEFF] The immediate safety problem was solved. And I need to plant something here that we'll

956
00:49:18,517 --> 00:49:23,888
[JEFF] come back to, because you already know where this goes. The ozone layer. Freon's safety

957
00:49:23,888 --> 00:49:29,260
[JEFF] was real — but what no one knew in nineteen thirty was that chlorofluorocarbons, once

958
00:49:29,260 --> 00:49:34,631
[JEFF] released into the atmosphere, float all the way up to the stratosphere and destroy ozone

959
00:49:34,631 --> 00:49:40,003
[JEFF] molecules. A historian will later call Midgley "the most environmentally destructive person who ever lived"

960
00:49:40,003 --> 00:49:45,374
[JEFF] — a man who had, quote, "more impact on the atmosphere than any other single

961
00:49:45,374 --> 00:49:50,745
[JEFF] organism in Earth's history."

962
00:49:50,745 --> 00:49:53,545
[CYRUS] Two catastrophic inventions. Leaded gasoline and Freon.

963
00:49:53,545 --> 00:49:59,545
[JEFF] One man. And he never knew about either one. He dies in nineteen forty-four —

964
00:49:59,545 --> 00:50:05,545
[JEFF] and we'll get to his death, which is its own remarkable story — never knowing

965
00:50:05,545 --> 00:50:11,545
[JEFF] that either of his most celebrated inventions would prove catastrophic. The ozone hole isn't confirmed

966
00:50:11,545 --> 00:50:17,545
[JEFF] until nineteen eighty-five. The Montreal Protocol isn't signed until nineteen eighty-seven. Midgley is long gone.

967
00:50:17,545 --> 00:50:21,145
[CYRUS] That is a genuinely strange kind of tragedy. He solved the problem and destroyed something

968
00:50:21,145 --> 00:50:24,745
[CYRUS] he couldn't see.

969
00:50:24,745 --> 00:50:30,045
[JEFF] That's almost exactly the right framing. He solved the problem you could see — families

970
00:50:30,045 --> 00:50:35,345
[JEFF] terrified of their own appliances — and he destroyed something invisible. And we are going

971
00:50:35,345 --> 00:50:40,645
[JEFF] to sit with that irony when we get to the Built In section, because I

972
00:50:40,645 --> 00:50:45,945
[JEFF] think it's the heart of this whole story.

973
00:50:45,945 --> 00:50:50,479
[JEFF] Now, I promised you Midgley's death. Because it is — and I say this with

974
00:50:50,479 --> 00:50:55,012
[JEFF] genuine respect for the man — it is among the more grimly poetic endings in

975
00:50:55,012 --> 00:50:59,545
[JEFF] the history of invention.

976
00:50:59,545 --> 00:51:02,745
[CYRUS] Okay, you've set up my expectations very specifically.

977
00:51:02,745 --> 00:51:08,117
[JEFF] In nineteen forty, Midgley contracts polio. He's severely disabled. And being the engineer that he

978
00:51:08,117 --> 00:51:13,488
[JEFF] is, he invents an elaborate system of ropes and pulleys to help himself get in

979
00:51:13,488 --> 00:51:18,860
[JEFF] and out of bed. Very clever. Very ingenious. On November second, nineteen forty-four, he is

980
00:51:18,860 --> 00:51:24,231
[JEFF] found dead, entangled in his own pulley system — strangled. He's fifty-five years old. The

981
00:51:24,231 --> 00:51:29,603
[JEFF] coroner ruled it a suicide, though people who knew him were divided, and history has

982
00:51:29,603 --> 00:51:34,974
[JEFF] tended to describe it as an accident. I'll leave that ambiguity in place, because I

983
00:51:34,974 --> 00:51:40,346
[JEFF] think it belongs there.

984
00:51:40,346 --> 00:51:44,546
[CYRUS] ... A man known for inventing things that unexpectedly killed people... is killed by his

985
00:51:44,546 --> 00:51:48,746
[CYRUS] own invention. One way or another.

986
00:51:48,746 --> 00:51:52,346
[JEFF] I didn't write this. This is just what happened.

987
00:51:52,346 --> 00:51:53,146
[CYRUS] That's dark.

988
00:51:53,146 --> 00:51:59,066
[JEFF] It is dark. And here's the thing that gives me pause whenever I tell stories

989
00:51:59,066 --> 00:52:04,986
[JEFF] like this — and I think it's worth saying. Midgley was genuinely brilliant. He was

990
00:52:04,986 --> 00:52:10,906
[JEFF] trying to solve real problems. Leaded gasoline was an effort to prevent engine knock. Freon

991
00:52:10,906 --> 00:52:16,826
[JEFF] was an effort to stop families from dying in their sleep. The unintended consequences were

992
00:52:16,826 --> 00:52:22,746
[JEFF] catastrophic, but the intentions were not bad. I think there's something important in that.

993
00:52:22,746 --> 00:52:27,146
[CYRUS] Yeah, I actually appreciate you saying that. It's easy to make him a villain in

994
00:52:27,146 --> 00:52:31,546
[CYRUS] retrospect. But he didn't know. Nobody knew.

995
00:52:31,546 --> 00:52:36,946
[JEFF] And while Midgley was doing his Freon work, do you know who else was trying

996
00:52:36,946 --> 00:52:42,346
[JEFF] to solve the same toxic refrigerant problem? From a completely different angle?

997
00:52:42,346 --> 00:52:43,946
[CYRUS] I genuinely do not.

998
00:52:43,946 --> 00:52:44,746
[JEFF] Albert Einstein.

999
00:52:44,746 --> 00:52:47,946
[CYRUS] ... Sorry. Albert Einstein was... working on refrigerators?

1000
00:52:47,946 --> 00:52:53,346
[JEFF] Albert Einstein and his former student Leo Szilard spent eight years designing a safer refrigerator.

1001
00:52:53,346 --> 00:52:58,746
[JEFF] Their motivation: Einstein had read a newspaper report about a Berlin family killed by toxic

1002
00:52:58,746 --> 00:53:04,146
[JEFF] refrigerant fumes leaking from their home refrigerator. He was so disturbed by it that he

1003
00:53:04,146 --> 00:53:09,546
[JEFF] contacted Szilard and the two of them began designing an alternative. Their idea was a

1004
00:53:09,546 --> 00:53:14,946
[JEFF] refrigerator with no moving parts — which meant no compressor, no seals, nothing to leak.

1005
00:53:14,946 --> 00:53:20,346
[JEFF] They used water, ammonia, and butane with an electromagnetic pump. They got a US patent

1006
00:53:20,346 --> 00:53:25,746
[JEFF] — patent number one million, seven hundred eighty-one thousand, five hundred forty-one — on November

1007
00:53:25,746 --> 00:53:31,146
[JEFF] eleventh, nineteen thirty.

1008
00:53:31,146 --> 00:53:33,946
[CYRUS] The same year as the Freon demo.

1009
00:53:33,946 --> 00:53:38,846
[JEFF] Same year. And you know what? Their design worked. It was less efficient, but it

1010
00:53:38,846 --> 00:53:43,746
[JEFF] was inherently more reliable. Nothing to break. Nothing to leak. But by the time the

1011
00:53:43,746 --> 00:53:48,646
[JEFF] patent came through, Freon had already solved the problem chemically, and the Einstein-Szilard refrigerator never

1012
00:53:48,646 --> 00:53:53,546
[JEFF] found a commercial market.

1013
00:53:53,546 --> 00:53:56,946
[CYRUS] So Einstein invented a refrigerator that the world didn't need because someone else solved the

1014
00:53:56,946 --> 00:54:00,346
[CYRUS] problem differently.

1015
00:54:00,346 --> 00:54:05,446
[JEFF] Right. And here's the strange postscript — in two thousand and eight, Oxford University engineers

1016
00:54:05,446 --> 00:54:10,546
[JEFF] revived the Einstein-Szilard design for potential use in off-grid developing-world applications. A refrigerator that works

1017
00:54:10,546 --> 00:54:15,646
[JEFF] without electricity, with no toxic chemicals, with no moving parts. Nearly a hundred years later,

1018
00:54:15,646 --> 00:54:20,746
[JEFF] it might finally find its purpose.

1019
00:54:20,746 --> 00:54:25,946
[CYRUS] That is a wild sentence. "Einstein's refrigerator might finally be useful in twenty-oh-eight."

1020
00:54:25,946 --> 00:54:28,346
[JEFF] Science history has some incredible timing.

1021
00:54:28,346 --> 00:54:34,186
[JEFF] Okay. So now we have Freon. We have a safe refrigerant. The mechanical problems are

1022
00:54:34,186 --> 00:54:40,026
[JEFF] solved. Prices are coming down — the GE Monitor Top launched in nineteen twenty-seven at

1023
00:54:40,026 --> 00:54:45,866
[JEFF] about five hundred and twenty-five dollars, and by nineteen forty you could get a comparable

1024
00:54:45,866 --> 00:54:51,706
[JEFF] refrigerator for a hundred and fifty-two dollars. The utility companies are even offering ten-dollar-a-month installment

1025
00:54:51,706 --> 00:54:57,546
[JEFF] plans added right to your electric bill. All the friction is being removed.

1026
00:54:57,546 --> 00:54:59,146
[CYRUS] So adoption should explode.

1027
00:54:59,146 --> 00:55:04,266
[JEFF] Adoption does explode. But before we get to the numbers, I want to spend some

1028
00:55:04,266 --> 00:55:09,386
[JEFF] time on the resistance. Because it wasn't just about chemistry. There was a deeper problem

1029
00:55:09,386 --> 00:55:14,506
[JEFF] — a psychological barrier — that had been building for decades before any of this.

1030
00:55:14,506 --> 00:55:19,626
[JEFF] And to understand it, I need to take you back to October twenty-third, nineteen eleven.

1031
00:55:19,626 --> 00:55:24,746
[JEFF] Chicago. The Hotel Sherman.

1032
00:55:24,746 --> 00:55:25,546
[CYRUS] All right.

1033
00:55:25,546 --> 00:55:31,212
[JEFF] Now, here's the setup. By nineteen eleven, cold storage warehouses are everywhere. The industrial refrigeration

1034
00:55:31,212 --> 00:55:36,879
[JEFF] wave is in full swing. But the public has a serious trust problem with refrigerated

1035
00:55:36,879 --> 00:55:42,545
[JEFF] food. Upton Sinclair's The Jungle came out in nineteen oh-six, and it shattered public confidence

1036
00:55:42,545 --> 00:55:48,212
[JEFF] in the food industry. Newspapers are running alarming stories about year-old food being sold as

1037
00:55:48,212 --> 00:55:53,879
[JEFF] fresh. Congress is considering strict limits on how long food can be kept in cold

1038
00:55:53,879 --> 00:55:59,545
[JEFF] storage. The entire cold-storage industry is facing an existential crisis.

1039
00:55:59,545 --> 00:56:02,745
[CYRUS] The industry that brought you modern food safety... is in a trust crisis about food

1040
00:56:02,745 --> 00:56:05,945
[CYRUS] safety.

1041
00:56:05,945 --> 00:56:11,845
[JEFF] Which is genuinely ironic. So the National Poultry, Butter, and Egg Association decides to fight

1042
00:56:11,845 --> 00:56:17,745
[JEFF] back with a spectacle. They invite four hundred guests — including Chicago's mayor and the

1043
00:56:17,745 --> 00:56:23,645
[JEFF] city's health commissioner — to a formal dinner at the Hotel Sherman. The Louis XVI

1044
00:56:23,645 --> 00:56:29,545
[JEFF] room. And every single item on the menu has been cold-stored. Some for months.

1045
00:56:29,545 --> 00:56:30,745
[CYRUS] Wait, every item?

1046
00:56:30,745 --> 00:56:36,479
[JEFF] Everything but, reportedly, the olives in the dry martinis was refrigerated. Including — and this

1047
00:56:36,479 --> 00:56:42,212
[JEFF] is the detail I could not stop thinking about — a capon that had reportedly

1048
00:56:42,212 --> 00:56:47,945
[JEFF] been killed on Valentine's Day. The dinner is in October. That's nine months.

1049
00:56:47,945 --> 00:56:50,745
[CYRUS] ... They served them a nine-month-old chicken.

1050
00:56:50,745 --> 00:56:55,445
[JEFF] At a formal banquet. To the mayor of Chicago. And here's how the host of

1051
00:56:55,445 --> 00:57:00,145
[JEFF] the evening addresses the crowd. He praises them for showing — his exact words —

1052
00:57:00,145 --> 00:57:04,845
[JEFF] "considerable courage in the face of all that has been written in the newspapers" about

1053
00:57:04,845 --> 00:57:09,545
[JEFF] cold-stored food.

1054
00:57:09,545 --> 00:57:13,345
[CYRUS] So the industry knows the food requires courage to eat. And they're saying so out

1055
00:57:13,345 --> 00:57:17,145
[CYRUS] loud at the dinner.

1056
00:57:17,145 --> 00:57:19,545
[JEFF] To the people eating it. Yes.

1057
00:57:19,545 --> 00:57:22,345
[CYRUS] That is peak nineteen eleven energy.

1058
00:57:22,345 --> 00:57:27,412
[JEFF] Now, the guests eat everything. Nobody gets sick. The banquet is declared a success. But

1059
00:57:27,412 --> 00:57:32,479
[JEFF] the press is not won over. The Chicago Inter Ocean runs a scathing op-ed the

1060
00:57:32,479 --> 00:57:37,545
[JEFF] next day. And one newspaper publishes what I think is one of the most darkly

1061
00:57:37,545 --> 00:57:42,612
[JEFF] funny sentences about technological adoption I've ever read. Quote: "The one silver lining is that

1062
00:57:42,612 --> 00:57:47,679
[JEFF] soon everyone who can remember what food is supposed to taste like, pre-refrigeration, will be

1063
00:57:47,679 --> 00:57:52,745
[JEFF] dead."

1064
00:57:52,745 --> 00:57:53,145
[CYRUS] Whoa.

1065
00:57:53,145 --> 00:57:57,345
[JEFF] Right? That's the newspaper saying: the only way to win the trust battle is to

1066
00:57:57,345 --> 00:58:01,545
[JEFF] wait for the skeptics to die.

1067
00:58:01,545 --> 00:58:07,145
[CYRUS] And that is kind of what happened. That's the actual mechanism of adoption sometimes.

1068
00:58:07,145 --> 00:58:12,517
[JEFF] That is sometimes the actual mechanism. And I want to stay with the resistance here

1069
00:58:12,517 --> 00:58:17,888
[JEFF] for a second, because I think it deserves more credit than it usually gets. These

1070
00:58:17,888 --> 00:58:23,260
[JEFF] consumers who didn't trust cold-stored food — they weren't being irrational. This was an era

1071
00:58:23,260 --> 00:58:28,631
[JEFF] when Upton Sinclair had just exposed genuine fraud in the food industry. The question they

1072
00:58:28,631 --> 00:58:34,003
[JEFF] were asking — "does cold equal fresh, or does cold just mean you can't tell

1073
00:58:34,003 --> 00:58:39,374
[JEFF] it's old?" — that is a completely reasonable question. And the industry, frankly, was not

1074
00:58:39,374 --> 00:58:44,745
[JEFF] always answering it honestly.

1075
00:58:44,745 --> 00:58:46,745
[CYRUS] The Milnor Electric Company ad.

1076
00:58:46,745 --> 00:58:48,345
[JEFF] You read the research.

1077
00:58:48,345 --> 00:58:52,745
[CYRUS] "Harmless, odorless and non-poisonous." Referring to methyl chloride. In nineteen twenty-three.

1078
00:58:52,745 --> 00:58:58,245
[JEFF] An industry that had lied about refrigerant safety was asking the public to trust it

1079
00:58:58,245 --> 00:59:03,745
[JEFF] about food safety. And people were skeptical. That skepticism had real reasons behind it. I

1080
00:59:03,745 --> 00:59:09,245
[JEFF] think it's easy to look back and say, "oh, they were just afraid of change."

1081
00:59:09,245 --> 00:59:14,745
[JEFF] They were afraid of being lied to. Again. That's different.

1082
00:59:14,745 --> 00:59:19,412
[CYRUS] A hundred percent. And by the way, the iceman persists well into the nineteen forties

1083
00:59:19,412 --> 00:59:24,079
[CYRUS] in a lot of American cities. This isn't a fringe group holding on. It's a

1084
00:59:24,079 --> 00:59:28,745
[CYRUS] large part of the market.

1085
00:59:28,745 --> 00:59:34,145
[JEFF] Still going to the iceman in the forties. Out of habit, loyalty, and yes —

1086
00:59:34,145 --> 00:59:39,545
[JEFF] some genuine distrust of the new system. Ice delivery persisted because the iceman was part

1087
00:59:39,545 --> 00:59:44,945
[JEFF] of the neighborhood. He came every day. He was a fixture of community life. There

1088
00:59:44,945 --> 00:59:50,345
[JEFF] was even a song — "How'd You Like to Be the Iceman?" — from eighteen

1089
00:59:50,345 --> 00:59:55,745
[JEFF] ninety-nine, capturing the cultural role. When the iceman disappears, something social disappears with him. That's

1090
00:59:55,745 --> 01:00:01,145
[JEFF] a real loss, not just nostalgia.

1091
01:00:01,145 --> 01:00:05,945
[JEFF] Okay, but adoption does happen. And the numbers are extraordinary. In nineteen thirty, eight percent

1092
01:00:05,945 --> 01:00:10,745
[JEFF] of American homes have a refrigerator. Eight percent. By nineteen forty, it's fifty-six percent. By

1093
01:00:10,745 --> 01:00:15,545
[JEFF] nineteen forty-four, eighty-five percent. In roughly fifteen years, refrigerators go from a luxury item to

1094
01:00:15,545 --> 01:00:20,345
[JEFF] being nearly universal.

1095
01:00:20,345 --> 01:00:25,145
[CYRUS] That's a remarkable curve. And this is happening... during the Great Depression?

1096
01:00:25,145 --> 01:00:30,479
[JEFF] During the Great Depression. And here's the counterintuitive thing — this is the part I

1097
01:00:30,479 --> 01:00:35,812
[JEFF] could not wrap my head around at first. The Depression paradoxically accelerated adoption. In the

1098
01:00:35,812 --> 01:00:41,145
[JEFF] worst economic crisis in American history, families bought expensive appliances.

1099
01:00:41,145 --> 01:00:44,745
[CYRUS] How does that work? Like, what's the actual mechanism?

1100
01:00:44,745 --> 01:00:50,012
[JEFF] I think the way to think about it is this: a refrigerator pays for itself.

1101
01:00:50,012 --> 01:00:55,279
[JEFF] If you're living through the Depression and you cannot afford to throw away spoiled food

1102
01:00:55,279 --> 01:01:00,545
[JEFF] — and nobody could — then an appliance that prevents spoilage is not a luxury.

1103
01:01:00,545 --> 01:01:05,812
[JEFF] It's economic rationality. Manufacturers cut prices to move inventory. The utility companies are offering ten

1104
01:01:05,812 --> 01:01:11,079
[JEFF] dollars a month. And families figure out that the fridge reduces their grocery bill enough

1105
01:01:11,079 --> 01:01:16,345
[JEFF] to justify the payment.

1106
01:01:16,345 --> 01:01:19,945
[CYRUS] So the Depression creates exactly the customer who most needs a refrigerator. A family that

1107
01:01:19,945 --> 01:01:23,545
[CYRUS] cannot afford waste.

1108
01:01:23,545 --> 01:01:29,145
[JEFF] Exactly. The Depression creates the customer who most needs a refrigerator. Prices drop from five

1109
01:01:29,145 --> 01:01:34,745
[JEFF] hundred dollars to two hundred dollars to a hundred and fifty-two dollars by nineteen forty.

1110
01:01:34,745 --> 01:01:40,345
[JEFF] General Electric had committed eighteen million dollars to manufacturing alone, and another million to advertising.

1111
01:01:40,345 --> 01:01:45,945
[JEFF] And the ad copy from GE in nineteen twenty-eight is interesting — "The gift that

1112
01:01:45,945 --> 01:01:51,545
[JEFF] simplifies housekeeping and safeguards health." Both benefits. Practical and emotional.

1113
01:01:51,545 --> 01:01:57,545
[CYRUS] That's smart positioning, actually. You're not just selling convenience. You're selling safety for your family.

1114
01:01:57,545 --> 01:02:02,425
[JEFF] And specifically for your children. A lot of the advertising is pitched directly at that

1115
01:02:02,425 --> 01:02:07,305
[JEFF] concern. Which — in an era before antibiotics, when infant mortality was still high and

1116
01:02:07,305 --> 01:02:12,185
[JEFF] contaminated milk was a real killer — that is not a frivolous pitch. The refrigerator

1117
01:02:12,185 --> 01:02:17,065
[JEFF] really did make children safer. The data on infant mortality tracks directly with clean, cold

1118
01:02:17,065 --> 01:02:21,945
[JEFF] milk.

1119
01:02:21,945 --> 01:02:27,679
[JEFF] Okay. So by the late nineteen thirties, refrigeration has conquered the American home. But here's

1120
01:02:27,679 --> 01:02:33,412
[JEFF] the gap. Here's the thing that's still broken. You have refrigerators in people's kitchens. You

1121
01:02:33,412 --> 01:02:39,145
[JEFF] have refrigerated warehouses. But the thing connecting them — the truck — doesn't have refrigeration.

1122
01:02:39,145 --> 01:02:44,879
[JEFF] If you want to ship chickens from St. Paul, Minnesota, to Chicago, you pack them

1123
01:02:44,879 --> 01:02:50,612
[JEFF] in ice and salt and you hope. If it's a long trip, or a hot

1124
01:02:50,612 --> 01:02:56,345
[JEFF] day, or the ice runs out... the shipment spoils. Total loss.

1125
01:02:56,345 --> 01:03:00,345
[CYRUS] So the cold chain has a gap in the middle.

1126
01:03:00,345 --> 01:03:06,245
[JEFF] The cold chain has a gap in the middle. And the person who closes it

1127
01:03:06,245 --> 01:03:12,145
[JEFF] — the person who makes the cold chain continuous from farm to store — is

1128
01:03:12,145 --> 01:03:18,045
[JEFF] someone whose name I had genuinely never heard before researching this episode. And I think

1129
01:03:18,045 --> 01:03:23,945
[JEFF] that's part of the story I want to tell. Because his biography is extraordinary.

1130
01:03:23,945 --> 01:03:25,945
[CYRUS] Who are we talking about?

1131
01:03:25,945 --> 01:03:31,612
[JEFF] Frederick McKinley Jones. Born May seventeenth, eighteen ninety-three, in Covington, Kentucky. His father was an

1132
01:03:31,612 --> 01:03:37,279
[JEFF] Irish railroad worker, his mother was African American, and she left when Jones was very

1133
01:03:37,279 --> 01:03:42,945
[JEFF] young. His father struggled to raise him alone and eventually, when Jones was around seven

1134
01:03:42,945 --> 01:03:48,612
[JEFF] years old, sent him to be raised by a Catholic priest named Father Ryan at

1135
01:03:48,612 --> 01:03:54,279
[JEFF] a rectory in Cincinnati. His father died when Jones was nine. He was an orphan,

1136
01:03:54,279 --> 01:03:59,945
[JEFF] being raised by a Catholic priest, with a sixth-grade education.

1137
01:03:59,945 --> 01:04:03,545
[CYRUS] That is a very specific kind of hard start.

1138
01:04:03,545 --> 01:04:08,825
[JEFF] And here's what he does with it. He leaves school at eleven. Gets a job

1139
01:04:08,825 --> 01:04:14,105
[JEFF] as a cleaning boy at a garage in Cincinnati. Gets promoted to mechanic's helper. By

1140
01:04:14,105 --> 01:04:19,385
[JEFF] the time he's fourteen, he's the shop foreman. At nineteen, he's described as "one of

1141
01:04:19,385 --> 01:04:24,665
[JEFF] the most well-known racers in the Great Lakes region." He's not just working on cars

1142
01:04:24,665 --> 01:04:29,945
[JEFF] — he's racing them. At nineteen.

1143
01:04:29,945 --> 01:04:32,745
[CYRUS] Self-taught mechanic, racing cars at nineteen. Okay.

1144
01:04:32,745 --> 01:04:38,012
[JEFF] He moves to Hallock, Minnesota in nineteen twelve. Works as a mechanic, as a farmhand.

1145
01:04:38,012 --> 01:04:43,279
[JEFF] Serves in World War One as a sergeant — in France, he rewires his entire

1146
01:04:43,279 --> 01:04:48,545
[JEFF] camp for electricity, telephone, and telegraph. After the war, comes back to Hallock. Teaches himself

1147
01:04:48,545 --> 01:04:53,812
[JEFF] electronics. Builds a radio transmitter for the town. During Minnesota winters, he drives physicians on

1148
01:04:53,812 --> 01:04:59,079
[JEFF] house calls through conditions that probably should have been unpassable, and invents a motorized snow

1149
01:04:59,079 --> 01:05:04,345
[JEFF] machine using airplane parts.

1150
01:05:04,345 --> 01:05:07,545
[CYRUS] He just... teaches himself everything. Over and over.

1151
01:05:07,545 --> 01:05:12,917
[JEFF] Every time there's a problem in front of him, he learns what he needs to

1152
01:05:12,917 --> 01:05:18,288
[JEFF] learn to solve it. There's no other way to describe it. And eventually — in

1153
01:05:18,288 --> 01:05:23,660
[JEFF] the late nineteen twenties or around nineteen thirty, sources differ a little on the exact

1154
01:05:23,660 --> 01:05:29,031
[JEFF] date — he goes to Minneapolis to apply for a job at Ultraphone Sound Systems,

1155
01:05:29,031 --> 01:05:34,403
[JEFF] a company run by a man named Joseph Numero. And the receptionist looks at him

1156
01:05:34,403 --> 01:05:39,774
[JEFF] and says — and these are the documented words — "We don't have any jobs

1157
01:05:39,774 --> 01:05:45,145
[JEFF] for a colored boy."

1158
01:05:45,145 --> 01:05:45,945
[CYRUS] Right.

1159
01:05:45,945 --> 01:05:51,225
[JEFF] Jones somehow gets past that, gets to Numero, and Numero hires him. And what Jones

1160
01:05:51,225 --> 01:05:56,505
[JEFF] does at Ultraphone is extraordinary: he takes silent-movie projectors — which are obsolete because sound

1161
01:05:56,505 --> 01:06:01,785
[JEFF] film is taking over — and he converts them into talking projectors using scrap materials.

1162
01:06:01,785 --> 01:06:07,065
[JEFF] He essentially invents solutions to problems that shouldn't be solvable with the parts he has.

1163
01:06:07,065 --> 01:06:12,345
[JEFF] He and Numero become a team.

1164
01:06:12,345 --> 01:06:15,945
[CYRUS] So Numero knows exactly what he has in Jones.

1165
01:06:15,945 --> 01:06:21,203
[JEFF] He knows. And then — and this is the moment — it's nineteen thirty-eight. Joseph

1166
01:06:21,203 --> 01:06:26,460
[JEFF] Numero is on a golf course with a friend who is in the trucking business.

1167
01:06:26,460 --> 01:06:31,717
[JEFF] The friend is venting. He lost a shipment of chickens on a run from St.

1168
01:06:31,717 --> 01:06:36,974
[JEFF] Paul to Chicago. Total loss. The ice and salt couldn't keep them cold for the

1169
01:06:36,974 --> 01:06:42,231
[JEFF] whole trip. And Numero — I love this about him — Numero makes a bet.

1170
01:06:42,231 --> 01:06:47,488
[JEFF] Reportedly six dollars. He says: "I have an engineer who can solve that problem in

1171
01:06:47,488 --> 01:06:52,745
[JEFF] thirty days."

1172
01:06:52,745 --> 01:06:57,545
[CYRUS] He bets six dollars. On his engineer. Against the laws of physics.

1173
01:06:57,545 --> 01:07:03,045
[JEFF] Against the laws of refrigeration, essentially. And he goes back to his shop and tells

1174
01:07:03,045 --> 01:07:08,545
[JEFF] Jones about the bet. And here's what Jones does. He goes to the library. He

1175
01:07:08,545 --> 01:07:14,045
[JEFF] teaches himself refrigeration. From scratch. In a matter of weeks, he designs the Thermo Control

1176
01:07:14,045 --> 01:07:19,545
[JEFF] Model A — a portable, automatic, truck-mounted refrigeration unit.

1177
01:07:19,545 --> 01:07:25,145
[CYRUS] Wait. He just... learned refrigeration and then built a truck refrigeration unit? In weeks?

1178
01:07:25,145 --> 01:07:30,460
[JEFF] In weeks. And I want to be honest — the Model A is a first

1179
01:07:30,460 --> 01:07:35,774
[JEFF] version. It works, but Jones keeps refining it. The Model C, completed in nineteen forty-one,

1180
01:07:35,774 --> 01:07:41,088
[JEFF] is the one that becomes the industry standard. Compact. Lightweight. And crucially: shockproof. Because every

1181
01:07:41,088 --> 01:07:46,403
[JEFF] previous attempt at truck refrigeration had the same problem — road vibrations destroyed the equipment.

1182
01:07:46,403 --> 01:07:51,717
[JEFF] Jones solved it by drawing on his experience with cinema sound equipment, which also had

1183
01:07:51,717 --> 01:07:57,031
[JEFF] to handle vibration. He already knew how to build things that could take a beating

1184
01:07:57,031 --> 01:08:02,345
[JEFF] and keep working.

1185
01:08:02,345 --> 01:08:05,945
[CYRUS] He applied his movie projector knowledge to refrigerated trucks.

1186
01:08:05,945 --> 01:08:11,245
[JEFF] Cross-domain engineering from an engineer with no formal training. And it works. Jones and Numero

1187
01:08:11,245 --> 01:08:16,545
[JEFF] co-found the U.S. Thermo Control Company — later renamed Thermo King — in nineteen thirty-eight.

1188
01:08:16,545 --> 01:08:21,845
[JEFF] Jones is vice president. The patent on the truck refrigeration unit is granted July twelfth,

1189
01:08:21,845 --> 01:08:27,145
[JEFF] nineteen forty. And then World War Two starts.

1190
01:08:27,145 --> 01:08:28,345
[CYRUS] Oh, that timing.

1191
01:08:28,345 --> 01:08:34,145
[JEFF] The technology that started as a solution for spoiled chickens gets drafted. During World War

1192
01:08:34,145 --> 01:08:39,945
[JEFF] Two, Jones's refrigeration units are used to preserve blood plasma, medicine, and food for troops

1193
01:08:39,945 --> 01:08:45,745
[JEFF] in the field. The thing he built to keep a truckload of chickens cold between

1194
01:08:45,745 --> 01:08:51,545
[JEFF] St. Paul and Chicago is now keeping blood plasma cold on a battlefield.

1195
01:08:51,545 --> 01:08:54,745
[CYRUS] ... That's a real thing. That saves lives.

1196
01:08:54,745 --> 01:09:00,145
[JEFF] That saves lives. Real, specific, countable lives. And in nineteen forty-four — same year Midgley

1197
01:09:00,145 --> 01:09:05,545
[JEFF] dies strangled in his own pulley system, I want to say that timing out loud

1198
01:09:05,545 --> 01:09:10,945
[JEFF] because it deserves to be said — in nineteen forty-four, Frederick McKinley Jones becomes the

1199
01:09:10,945 --> 01:09:16,345
[JEFF] first African American elected to the American Society of Refrigeration Engineers. In an era of

1200
01:09:16,345 --> 01:09:21,745
[JEFF] pervasive racial segregation, a self-taught Black orphan from Kentucky is elected to the professional engineering

1201
01:09:21,745 --> 01:09:27,145
[JEFF] society of the industry he reshaped.

1202
01:09:27,145 --> 01:09:30,345
[CYRUS] And how many people have heard of him?

1203
01:09:30,345 --> 01:09:35,545
[JEFF] That's the question. He holds sixty-one patents. Forty of them are for refrigeration. He also

1204
01:09:35,545 --> 01:09:40,745
[JEFF] invents portable X-ray machines and an automatic ticket-dispensing machine that he sells to RCA. He

1205
01:09:40,745 --> 01:09:45,945
[JEFF] dies on February twenty-first, nineteen sixty-one, at sixty-seven years old. Thermo King by then is

1206
01:09:45,945 --> 01:09:51,145
[JEFF] a three million dollar business, and it will become a global leader in transport refrigeration

1207
01:09:51,145 --> 01:09:56,345
[JEFF] worth orders of magnitude more.

1208
01:09:56,345 --> 01:09:57,545
[CYRUS] And the recognition?

1209
01:09:57,545 --> 01:10:02,345
[JEFF] Posthumous. In nineteen ninety-one — thirty years after his death — President George H.W. Bush

1210
01:10:02,345 --> 01:10:07,145
[JEFF] awards him the National Medal of Technology. First Black American to receive it. The president

1211
01:10:07,145 --> 01:10:11,945
[JEFF] presents it to Jones's widow and to Numero's widow in a ceremony at the White

1212
01:10:11,945 --> 01:10:16,745
[JEFF] House Rose Garden.

1213
01:10:16,745 --> 01:10:18,745
[CYRUS] Thirty years after he died.

1214
01:10:18,745 --> 01:10:24,168
[JEFF] Thirty years after he died. And I want to say something about the survivorship bias

1215
01:10:24,168 --> 01:10:29,590
[JEFF] here, because I always try to be honest about this. Jones's story is remarkable —

1216
01:10:29,590 --> 01:10:35,012
[JEFF] and it is remarkable. But it's also true that for every Frederick McKinley Jones who

1217
01:10:35,012 --> 01:10:40,434
[JEFF] overcomes every obstacle and dies a celebrated inventor, there are many, many people who faced

1218
01:10:40,434 --> 01:10:45,857
[JEFF] the same obstacles and didn't get the break, or didn't get the partner, or didn't

1219
01:10:45,857 --> 01:10:51,279
[JEFF] get the particular moment when the right person was on the right golf course making

1220
01:10:51,279 --> 01:10:56,701
[JEFF] the right bet. Jones's genius is real. His persistence is real. And so is the

1221
01:10:56,701 --> 01:11:02,123
[JEFF] pile of structural obstacles that took down other equally brilliant people who never got past

1222
01:11:02,123 --> 01:11:07,545
[JEFF] a receptionist.

1223
01:11:07,545 --> 01:11:12,345
[CYRUS] I think that's right. And it actually makes his story more interesting, not less. It's

1224
01:11:12,345 --> 01:11:17,145
[CYRUS] not a fable. It's a specific confluence of things — genius, timing, Numero, the war

1225
01:11:17,145 --> 01:11:21,945
[CYRUS] — that could have gone differently.

1226
01:11:21,945 --> 01:11:27,545
[JEFF] At multiple moments. The six-dollar bet. Getting past the receptionist. The war creating a military

1227
01:11:27,545 --> 01:11:33,145
[JEFF] application. Any one of those doesn't happen and you don't get Thermo King.

1228
01:11:33,145 --> 01:11:38,945
[JEFF] So. What changed. Let me walk through it. The iceman vanishes. Not immediately — ice

1229
01:11:38,945 --> 01:11:44,745
[JEFF] delivery hangs on into the nineteen forties in many cities. But by the nineteen fifties,

1230
01:11:44,745 --> 01:11:50,545
[JEFF] it's effectively gone. Ninety thousand ice-harvesting jobs plus uncounted delivery jobs — an entire profession

1231
01:11:50,545 --> 01:11:56,345
[JEFF] that had existed for over a century — disappears within roughly one generation.

1232
01:11:56,345 --> 01:12:00,145
[CYRUS] And it takes something social with it. The iceman was a fixture. He came every

1233
01:12:00,145 --> 01:12:03,945
[CYRUS] day. People knew him.

1234
01:12:03,945 --> 01:12:09,545
[JEFF] He came every day. He knew which families had kids. He knew the back entrance.

1235
01:12:09,545 --> 01:12:15,145
[JEFF] For a lot of women who were homemakers, the iceman was one of the regular

1236
01:12:15,145 --> 01:12:20,745
[JEFF] points of contact with the outside world. When he disappears, something genuinely human goes away.

1237
01:12:20,745 --> 01:12:26,345
[JEFF] Ice delivery was a service, but it was also a relationship.

1238
01:12:26,345 --> 01:12:30,145
[CYRUS] The housework paradox you mentioned in the research. The refrigerator is supposed to liberate women

1239
01:12:30,145 --> 01:12:33,945
[CYRUS] from daily shopping, but—

1240
01:12:33,945 --> 01:12:39,745
[JEFF] But what actually happens is that as technology removes some of the burden, expectations rise.

1241
01:12:39,745 --> 01:12:45,545
[JEFF] Weekly shopping replaces daily shopping — that's real. But now women are expected to prepare

1242
01:12:45,545 --> 01:12:51,345
[JEFF] more elaborate meals, offer greater variety, maintain higher standards. The time saved by the refrigerator

1243
01:12:51,345 --> 01:12:57,145
[JEFF] gets absorbed by rising standards for what dinner is supposed to look like.

1244
01:12:57,145 --> 01:13:01,145
[CYRUS] The technology enables more, and then "more" becomes the expectation.

1245
01:13:01,145 --> 01:13:06,845
[JEFF] It's the same dynamic you see with email. It was supposed to save time. But

1246
01:13:06,845 --> 01:13:12,545
[JEFF] now the expectation is that you respond to everything within hours. The technology that saves

1247
01:13:12,545 --> 01:13:18,245
[JEFF] time creates the expectation that fills the time. I think there's a real principle in

1248
01:13:18,245 --> 01:13:23,945
[JEFF] there that we should come back to in the Built In section.

1249
01:13:23,945 --> 01:13:25,545
[CYRUS] Agreed. What else changed?

1250
01:13:25,545 --> 01:13:31,065
[JEFF] Seasonal eating basically ends. Before refrigeration, what you ate in January was determined by what

1251
01:13:31,065 --> 01:13:36,585
[JEFF] was storable — root vegetables, preserved meats, pickles, fermented things. After refrigeration, "fresh" becomes the

1252
01:13:36,585 --> 01:13:42,105
[JEFF] default and the old food traditions — pickling, curing, fermenting — start to decline. They're

1253
01:13:42,105 --> 01:13:47,625
[JEFF] still practiced, but they shift from necessity to hobby. Nobody's making sauerkraut because they have

1254
01:13:47,625 --> 01:13:53,145
[JEFF] to anymore. They're making it because they want to.

1255
01:13:53,145 --> 01:13:56,545
[CYRUS] Which is a kind of loss, even if the refrigerator is genuinely better at keeping

1256
01:13:56,545 --> 01:13:59,945
[CYRUS] food safe.

1257
01:13:59,945 --> 01:14:05,865
[JEFF] There's a thread there worth pulling. And then there's the supermarket. Because Frederick McKinley Jones's

1258
01:14:05,865 --> 01:14:11,785
[JEFF] truck refrigeration doesn't just keep food cold — it makes it possible to build the

1259
01:14:11,785 --> 01:14:17,705
[JEFF] modern food supply chain. If you can refrigerate a truck, you can ship fresh meat

1260
01:14:17,705 --> 01:14:23,625
[JEFF] from a processing plant in Kansas to a grocery store in Philadelphia. You can have

1261
01:14:23,625 --> 01:14:29,545
[JEFF] strawberries in December. You can have the entire grocery store as we know it.

1262
01:14:29,545 --> 01:14:33,545
[CYRUS] The supermarket only works if the whole chain is cold.

1263
01:14:33,545 --> 01:14:39,245
[JEFF] Exactly. And Clarence Birdseye, who'd been watching Inuit fishermen flash-freeze fish in Labrador back in

1264
01:14:39,245 --> 01:14:44,945
[JEFF] nineteen twelve and spent the next decade figuring out how to replicate it commercially —

1265
01:14:44,945 --> 01:14:50,645
[JEFF] he'd already sold his frozen food company for twenty-two million dollars in nineteen twenty-nine. The

1266
01:14:50,645 --> 01:14:56,345
[JEFF] frozen food aisle is waiting. Jones builds the truck that stocks it.

1267
01:14:56,345 --> 01:15:01,945
[CYRUS] So the supermarket is the endgame. The whole infrastructure converges in the grocery store.

1268
01:15:01,945 --> 01:15:06,945
[JEFF] And by the nineteen fifties, that infrastructure is essentially complete. Refrigerated trucks, refrigerated warehouses, refrigerated

1269
01:15:06,945 --> 01:15:11,945
[JEFF] display cases in stores, refrigerators in homes. The cold chain is continuous from farm to

1270
01:15:11,945 --> 01:15:16,945
[JEFF] fork. And sitting invisibly at the center of all of it, molecule by molecule accumulating

1271
01:15:16,945 --> 01:15:21,945
[JEFF] in the stratosphere, is Freon.

1272
01:15:21,945 --> 01:15:26,345
[CYRUS] The thing that made all of it safe enough to happen.

1273
01:15:26,345 --> 01:15:30,145
[JEFF] The thing that made all of it safe enough to happen. And we'll come back

1274
01:15:30,145 --> 01:15:33,945
[JEFF] to what that cost.

1275
01:15:33,945 --> 01:15:39,305
[JEFF] One last thing before we hand this off to the Built In section. I want

1276
01:15:39,305 --> 01:15:44,665
[JEFF] to spend a minute on the road not taken. Because in the nineteen thirties, there

1277
01:15:44,665 --> 01:15:50,025
[JEFF] were actually two different solutions to the toxic refrigerant problem on the table. Midgley's chemical

1278
01:15:50,025 --> 01:15:55,385
[JEFF] solution — Freon — and Einstein and Szilard's mechanical solution. No moving parts, no seals,

1279
01:15:55,385 --> 01:16:00,745
[JEFF] no toxic chemicals, but also no CFCs.

1280
01:16:00,745 --> 01:16:03,945
[CYRUS] And Freon won because it was more efficient.

1281
01:16:03,945 --> 01:16:09,945
[JEFF] Freon won because it was more efficient, and because it arrived right as the market

1282
01:16:09,945 --> 01:16:15,945
[JEFF] was ready to explode, and because GM and DuPont were behind it with enormous resources.

1283
01:16:15,945 --> 01:16:21,945
[JEFF] The Einstein-Szilard fridge was better on reliability. It was worse on efficiency. In nineteen thirty,

1284
01:16:21,945 --> 01:16:27,945
[JEFF] when electricity is cheap and the ozone layer is not a known concern, efficiency wins.

1285
01:16:27,945 --> 01:16:32,745
[CYRUS] If you knew in nineteen thirty what you knew in nineteen eighty-five — about the

1286
01:16:32,745 --> 01:16:37,545
[CYRUS] ozone hole — you might make a different bet.

1287
01:16:37,545 --> 01:16:43,065
[JEFF] You almost certainly make a different bet. The Montreal Protocol happens eventually, and CFCs get

1288
01:16:43,065 --> 01:16:48,585
[JEFF] phased out. But by then, billions of units have been running for decades. The ozone

1289
01:16:48,585 --> 01:16:54,105
[JEFF] hole over Antarctica is real and measurable. It is the largest-scale unintended consequence in the

1290
01:16:54,105 --> 01:16:59,625
[JEFF] history of consumer technology, I would argue. And it starts with one man taking a

1291
01:16:59,625 --> 01:17:05,145
[JEFF] breath on stage in Atlanta in April nineteen thirty.

1292
01:17:05,145 --> 01:17:09,745
[CYRUS] The road not taken being the no-moving-parts refrigerator from the guy who also invented the

1293
01:17:09,745 --> 01:17:14,345
[CYRUS] atomic bomb concept... that's a very specific road.

1294
01:17:14,345 --> 01:17:20,145
[JEFF] The roads available in nineteen thirty were unusual, yes. But the choice between them shaped

1295
01:17:20,145 --> 01:17:25,945
[JEFF] the next fifty years of atmospheric chemistry. And that is not an exaggeration.

1296
01:17:25,945 --> 01:17:29,345
[CYRUS] Okay. I think we need to step back and look at the full arc of

1297
01:17:29,345 --> 01:17:32,745
[CYRUS] this thing.

1298
01:17:32,745 --> 01:17:38,212
[JEFF] We do. From Frederic Tudor in eighteen oh-six to Frederick McKinley Jones in nineteen forty-one

1299
01:17:38,212 --> 01:17:43,679
[JEFF] to Freon silently accumulating through the nineteen fifties, sixties, seventies, and eighties — that is

1300
01:17:43,679 --> 01:17:49,145
[JEFF] a story that is still playing out. Let's bring it home.

1301
01:02:55,159 --> 01:02:58,759
[JEFF] Okay. We've been at this for a while now.

1302
01:02:58,759 --> 01:03:01,959
[CYRUS] Yeah we have. Two hundred years of cold.

1303
01:03:01,959 --> 01:03:07,825
[JEFF] Two hundred years of cold. And I want to just... step back for a second.

1304
01:03:07,825 --> 01:03:13,692
[JEFF] Because I think if you've been following along with us, you'll see something that's really

1305
01:03:13,692 --> 01:03:19,558
[JEFF] hard to see when you're in the middle of any one of these waves.

1306
01:03:19,558 --> 01:03:20,358
[CYRUS] What's that?

1307
01:03:20,358 --> 01:03:25,958
[JEFF] Every single wave ended with somebody getting destroyed. Every one. Tudor buries the natural ice

1308
01:03:25,958 --> 01:03:31,558
[JEFF] guys. The ammonia machines bury Tudor's whole empire. Freon and the sealed compressor bury the

1309
01:03:31,558 --> 01:03:37,158
[JEFF] ammonia guys. The cold chain buries the local butchers. And at every single moment, the

1310
01:03:37,158 --> 01:03:42,758
[JEFF] people getting buried were not crazy. They were not stupid. They were often right about

1311
01:03:42,758 --> 01:03:48,358
[JEFF] the risks — they just couldn't stop what was coming.

1312
01:03:48,358 --> 01:03:53,058
[CYRUS] And here's the thing that gets me — they often had reasonable arguments. The railroads

1313
01:03:53,058 --> 01:03:57,758
[CYRUS] weren't wrong that Swift's refrigerated cars changed their whole business model. The natural ice guys

1314
01:03:57,758 --> 01:04:02,458
[CYRUS] weren't wrong that mechanical machines were dangerous. They were just on the wrong side of

1315
01:04:02,458 --> 01:04:07,158
[CYRUS] the wave.

1316
01:04:07,158 --> 01:04:13,025
[JEFF] Right. And now we're sitting inside the world that all of those waves built. So

1317
01:04:13,025 --> 01:04:18,892
[JEFF] let me just put some numbers on it, because I think they hit differently now

1318
01:04:18,892 --> 01:04:24,758
[JEFF] than they did when we threw them out at the top of the episode.

1319
01:04:24,758 --> 01:04:25,158
[CYRUS] Yeah.

1320
01:04:25,158 --> 01:04:31,158
[JEFF] In eighteen oh six, shipping frozen pond water to the tropics was a joke. The

1321
01:04:31,158 --> 01:04:37,158
[JEFF] Boston Gazette literally called it a "slippery speculation." In eighteen fifty-five, the man who invented

1322
01:04:37,158 --> 01:04:43,158
[JEFF] the underlying principle behind every single refrigerator on Earth died broke and forgotten at fifty-one.

1323
01:04:43,158 --> 01:04:44,358
[CYRUS] Gorrie. Completely humiliated.

1324
01:04:44,358 --> 01:04:49,158
[JEFF] Completely humiliated. In nineteen eleven, eating year-old cold-stored food required — and I love this

1325
01:04:49,158 --> 01:04:53,958
[JEFF] phrase — "considerable courage." In nineteen thirty, eight percent of American homes had a refrigerator.

1326
01:04:53,958 --> 01:04:58,758
[JEFF] By nineteen forty-four, eighty-five percent did.

1327
01:04:58,758 --> 01:05:01,558
[CYRUS] Fourteen years. Eight percent to eighty-five percent.

1328
01:05:01,558 --> 01:05:07,158
[JEFF] That's Freon. That's what happens when you remove the toxicity barrier. And today — today

1329
01:05:07,158 --> 01:05:12,758
[JEFF] — seventy percent of all food on Earth passes through the cold chain at some

1330
01:05:12,758 --> 01:05:18,358
[JEFF] point. Refrigeration consumes twenty-five to thirty percent of global electricity. Hundreds of millions of people

1331
01:05:18,358 --> 01:05:23,958
[JEFF] live in climates that are only habitable because of air conditioning.

1332
01:05:23,958 --> 01:05:28,758
[CYRUS] We have built a world that physically cannot function at ambient temperature.

1333
01:05:28,758 --> 01:05:34,758
[JEFF] That's exactly right. And we built it in two centuries. Starting with a twenty-three year

1334
01:05:34,758 --> 01:05:40,758
[JEFF] old from Boston who couldn't get anyone to take his ship full of ice seriously.

1335
01:05:40,758 --> 01:05:43,158
[CYRUS] ... That is quite an arc.

1336
01:05:43,158 --> 01:05:47,758
[JEFF] So that brings us to the Backbone Test — the five questions we ask about

1337
01:05:47,758 --> 01:05:52,358
[JEFF] every technology when it crosses from "remarkable" to "built in." And I have to say,

1338
01:05:52,358 --> 01:05:56,958
[JEFF] refrigeration is maybe the cleanest answer to every single one of these questions I can

1339
01:05:56,958 --> 01:06:01,558
[JEFF] imagine.

1340
01:06:01,558 --> 01:06:04,758
[CYRUS] Let's find out. Number one: is it invisible?

1341
01:06:04,758 --> 01:06:10,225
[JEFF] Completely invisible. Like, think about the last time you thought about your refrigerator. Not when

1342
01:06:10,225 --> 01:06:15,692
[JEFF] you opened it to get food out — that's just automatic at this point. I

1343
01:06:15,692 --> 01:06:21,158
[JEFF] mean actually thought about it as a piece of technology.

1344
01:06:21,158 --> 01:06:22,758
[CYRUS] I genuinely cannot remember.

1345
01:06:22,758 --> 01:06:28,358
[JEFF] No one can. It crossed from "remarkable" to "assumed" sometime in the nineteen-fifties, when the

1346
01:06:28,358 --> 01:06:33,958
[JEFF] last icemen retired and refrigerators stopped being advertised as miracles and started being sold as...

1347
01:06:33,958 --> 01:06:39,558
[JEFF] kitchen furniture. Another box in the kitchen. As forgettable as the sink.

1348
01:06:39,558 --> 01:06:43,158
[CYRUS] And that's the tell, right? The technology disappears into the background. You only notice infrastructure

1349
01:06:43,158 --> 01:06:46,758
[CYRUS] when it fails.

1350
01:06:46,758 --> 01:06:51,158
[JEFF] Which brings me to something I want to talk about, because this is the best

1351
01:06:51,158 --> 01:06:55,558
[JEFF] modern illustration of what happens when this invisible infrastructure goes away. February twenty twenty-one. Texas.

1352
01:06:55,558 --> 01:06:59,958
[JEFF] Winter Storm Uri.

1353
01:06:59,958 --> 01:07:00,758
[CYRUS] Oh wow.

1354
01:07:00,758 --> 01:07:06,758
[JEFF] So Winter Storm Uri hits Texas with temperatures nobody there had planned for. And the

1355
01:07:06,758 --> 01:07:12,758
[JEFF] power grid — and I want to be clear, that is a whole separate story

1356
01:07:12,758 --> 01:07:18,758
[JEFF] that someone should tell, it's a story about grid design and deregulation — goes down.

1357
01:07:18,758 --> 01:07:24,758
[JEFF] Four point eight million customers lose power, some of them for more than a week.

1358
01:07:24,758 --> 01:07:27,558
[CYRUS] Right. And when the power goes down—

1359
01:07:27,558 --> 01:07:33,358
[JEFF] Everything goes down. The grocery stores lose power. H-E-B — Texas's dominant grocery chain, and

1360
01:07:33,358 --> 01:07:39,158
[JEFF] honestly one of the best-run supply chains in the country — H-E-B issues a statement

1361
01:07:39,158 --> 01:07:44,958
[JEFF] saying there has been "severe disruption in the food supply chain" because of "unprecedented weather."

1362
01:07:44,958 --> 01:07:50,758
[JEFF] Which is a very restrained way to say: everything is going wrong simultaneously.

1363
01:07:50,758 --> 01:07:53,958
[CYRUS] What are people actually doing at that point?

1364
01:07:53,958 --> 01:07:59,025
[JEFF] This is the part that gets me. People started storing their remaining food in coolers

1365
01:07:59,025 --> 01:08:04,092
[JEFF] packed with snow outside their homes. Because the machines had stopped, so they went back

1366
01:08:04,092 --> 01:08:09,158
[JEFF] to the original method — using nature's cold.

1367
01:08:09,158 --> 01:08:14,358
[CYRUS] Wait. Is that right? They're literally doing Tudor's ice trade, but in reverse?

1368
01:08:14,358 --> 01:08:19,625
[JEFF] In reverse, in their own front yards, in twenty twenty-one. The most technologically advanced state

1369
01:08:19,625 --> 01:08:24,892
[JEFF] in the most technologically advanced country in the world was reduced, for a week, to

1370
01:08:24,892 --> 01:08:30,158
[JEFF] storing food in snowbanks. One Kroger in Houston — people lined up at four in

1371
01:08:30,158 --> 01:08:35,425
[JEFF] the morning. The store was sold out of bread, milk, cheese, chicken, and water by

1372
01:08:35,425 --> 01:08:40,692
[JEFF] afternoon. A major chicken supplier shut down entirely. Farmers were dumping milk because the processing

1373
01:08:40,692 --> 01:08:45,958
[JEFF] plants had no power.

1374
01:08:45,958 --> 01:08:47,558
[CYRUS] How many people died?

1375
01:08:47,558 --> 01:08:53,258
[JEFF] At least two hundred forty-six. Some estimates put it closer to seven hundred. Mostly hypothermia,

1376
01:08:53,258 --> 01:08:58,958
[JEFF] but the point for us is what the collapse of the cold chain looked like.

1377
01:08:58,958 --> 01:09:04,658
[JEFF] Eighty billion to a hundred and thirty billion dollars in economic damage. The costliest natural

1378
01:09:04,658 --> 01:09:10,358
[JEFF] disaster in Texas history. Agricultural losses alone: over six hundred million dollars.

1379
01:09:10,358 --> 01:09:11,558
[CYRUS] In a week.

1380
01:09:11,558 --> 01:09:17,358
[JEFF] In a week. That's what invisible infrastructure looks like when it becomes visible. And listeners

1381
01:09:17,358 --> 01:09:23,158
[JEFF] — I want you to sit with that for a second. Because when we talk

1382
01:09:23,158 --> 01:09:28,958
[JEFF] about refrigeration being "built in," this is what that actually means. Not built into the

1383
01:09:28,958 --> 01:09:34,758
[JEFF] technology. Built into civilization. Built into the assumption that cold is always available.

1384
01:09:34,758 --> 01:09:37,558
[CYRUS] Okay. Question two: what depends on it?

1385
01:09:37,558 --> 01:09:37,958
[JEFF] Everything.

1386
01:09:37,958 --> 01:09:39,958
[CYRUS] That's your whole answer?

1387
01:09:39,958 --> 01:09:45,158
[JEFF] I mean... yes? Let me put it this way. Seventy percent of all food we

1388
01:09:45,158 --> 01:09:50,358
[JEFF] eat. Essentially all vaccines — the WHO estimates that up to fifty percent of vaccines

1389
01:09:50,358 --> 01:09:55,558
[JEFF] are wasted globally due to cold-chain failures, not just COVID, but all vaccines. Insulin, blood

1390
01:09:55,558 --> 01:10:00,758
[JEFF] products, organ transplants. Semiconductor manufacturing. Data centers — the internet, AI, all of it requires

1391
01:10:00,758 --> 01:10:05,958
[JEFF] massive cooling systems to function.

1392
01:10:05,958 --> 01:10:08,358
[CYRUS] And then there's the political map.

1393
01:10:08,358 --> 01:10:13,958
[JEFF] And then there's the political map. This is the one I find genuinely astonishing when

1394
01:10:13,958 --> 01:10:19,558
[JEFF] I really think about it. Willis Carrier invents modern air conditioning in nineteen oh two.

1395
01:10:19,558 --> 01:10:25,158
[JEFF] By the nineteen-fifties, affordable window units start showing up. And what happens?

1396
01:10:25,158 --> 01:10:25,558
[CYRUS] Phoenix.

1397
01:10:25,558 --> 01:10:30,758
[JEFF] Phoenix. Sixty-five thousand people in nineteen forty. Four hundred thirty-nine thousand in nineteen sixty. A

1398
01:10:30,758 --> 01:10:35,958
[JEFF] four-hundred-seventy-five percent increase in twenty years. Maricopa County — the broader metro area — goes

1399
01:10:35,958 --> 01:10:41,158
[JEFF] from a hundred and eighty-six thousand in nineteen forty to one point five million in

1400
01:10:41,158 --> 01:10:46,358
[JEFF] nineteen eighty. Seven hundred eleven percent growth.

1401
01:10:46,358 --> 01:10:47,958
[CYRUS] Because of air conditioning.

1402
01:10:47,958 --> 01:10:53,558
[JEFF] Without air conditioning, Phoenix in July is not a city. It's a punishment. From nineteen

1403
01:10:53,558 --> 01:10:59,158
[JEFF] sixty to two thousand, Sun Belt states gained eighty-six seats in the House of Representatives

1404
01:10:59,158 --> 01:11:04,758
[JEFF] and the Electoral College. Eighty-six seats. The political map of the United States was redrawn

1405
01:11:04,758 --> 01:11:10,358
[JEFF] by a technology that moves heat from one place to another.

1406
01:11:10,358 --> 01:11:13,158
[CYRUS] That doesn't get taught in civics class.

1407
01:11:13,158 --> 01:11:18,198
[JEFF] It absolutely does not. I think it should be. The same physics that keeps your

1408
01:11:18,198 --> 01:11:23,238
[JEFF] food from spoiling is why American politics looks the way it does. And here's the

1409
01:11:23,238 --> 01:11:28,278
[JEFF] version of this I find most striking: the COVID vaccine cold chain. The Pfizer-BioNTech mRNA

1410
01:11:28,278 --> 01:11:33,318
[JEFF] vaccine required storage at minus seventy degrees Celsius. Colder than the South Pole. Colder than

1411
01:11:33,318 --> 01:11:38,358
[JEFF] winter in Antarctica.

1412
01:11:38,358 --> 01:11:43,558
[CYRUS] Which, if you have the infrastructure, is technically doable. But if you don't—

1413
01:11:43,558 --> 01:11:48,625
[JEFF] If you don't, you have Peru, where only thirty ultra-cold freezers existed in the entire

1414
01:11:48,625 --> 01:11:53,692
[JEFF] country. All thirty of them in Lima. None anywhere else. In Sub-Saharan Africa, one in

1415
01:11:53,692 --> 01:11:58,758
[JEFF] four health facilities had no electricity at all. One in four. And in the United

1416
01:11:58,758 --> 01:12:03,825
[JEFF] States — which does have the infrastructure — we still discarded eighty-two million vaccine doses

1417
01:12:03,825 --> 01:12:08,892
[JEFF] between December twenty twenty and June twenty twenty-one. One point one billion dollars of vaccines.

1418
01:12:08,892 --> 01:12:13,958
[JEFF] Gone.

1419
01:12:13,958 --> 01:12:14,758
[CYRUS] Holy cow.

1420
01:12:14,758 --> 01:12:19,958
[JEFF] And that's us. That's the country that invented the cold chain. So the dependency is

1421
01:12:19,958 --> 01:12:25,158
[JEFF] massive, and the gap between who has the infrastructure and who doesn't is itself one

1422
01:12:25,158 --> 01:12:30,358
[JEFF] of the biggest public health crises in the world.

1423
01:12:30,358 --> 01:12:34,758
[CYRUS] Okay. Question three: what's the hidden cost? And I want to go here, because this

1424
01:12:34,758 --> 01:12:39,158
[CYRUS] is where I think we actually disagree.

1425
01:12:39,158 --> 01:12:40,758
[JEFF] Let's find out.

1426
01:12:40,758 --> 01:12:46,492
[CYRUS] So here's my version. The hidden cost is not just any particular refrigerant. It's the

1427
01:12:46,492 --> 01:12:52,225
[CYRUS] structure of the problem. We need more cooling because the planet is warming. But cooling

1428
01:12:52,225 --> 01:12:57,958
[CYRUS] consumes energy that accelerates warming. And here is the number that I cannot get out

1429
01:12:57,958 --> 01:13:03,692
[CYRUS] of my head: some projections have global cooling demand surging more than thirty-fold by two

1430
01:13:03,692 --> 01:13:09,425
[CYRUS] thousand one hundred — the IEA projects a tripling just by twenty-fifty, and some regional

1431
01:13:09,425 --> 01:13:15,158
[CYRUS] models for the hottest parts of the world go much higher.

1432
01:13:15,158 --> 01:13:15,958
[JEFF] Thirty-three times?

1433
01:13:15,958 --> 01:13:20,658
[CYRUS] Thirty-three times. Because billions of people in the hottest climates on Earth — India, Southeast

1434
01:13:20,658 --> 01:13:25,358
[CYRUS] Asia, sub-Saharan Africa — are gaining access to cooling for the first time. India's air

1435
01:13:25,358 --> 01:13:30,058
[CYRUS] conditioner sales are projected to grow six-fold by twenty thirty-eight. Six times. That's not a

1436
01:13:30,058 --> 01:13:34,758
[CYRUS] rounding error.

1437
01:13:34,758 --> 01:13:36,758
[JEFF] That's not a rounding error.

1438
01:13:36,758 --> 01:13:41,718
[CYRUS] And here's the structural problem. The people who most need cooling are the ones in

1439
01:13:41,718 --> 01:13:46,678
[CYRUS] the hottest, often poorest places. And their adoption of cooling is going to be the

1440
01:13:46,678 --> 01:13:51,638
[CYRUS] thing that most accelerates the warming that makes them need more cooling. There's a feedback

1441
01:13:51,638 --> 01:13:56,598
[CYRUS] loop here that I don't see an institutional solution to. The incentives point directly against

1442
01:13:56,598 --> 01:14:01,558
[CYRUS] solving it.

1443
01:14:01,558 --> 01:14:06,678
[JEFF] Yeah, and I think... I mean, I hear that. And I'm not sure it is

1444
01:14:06,678 --> 01:14:11,798
[JEFF] as structurally unsolvable as you're making it sound. Here's the thing: we've actually done this

1445
01:14:11,798 --> 01:14:16,918
[JEFF] before. The Montreal Protocol — when the ozone crisis hit — that was international cooperation

1446
01:14:16,918 --> 01:14:22,038
[JEFF] working. That was governments agreeing to eliminate CFCs across the board, and it worked. The

1447
01:14:22,038 --> 01:14:27,158
[JEFF] ozone layer is recovering.

1448
01:14:27,158 --> 01:14:31,758
[CYRUS] A hundred percent. The Montreal Protocol worked. But here's what happened. We replaced CFCs with

1449
01:14:31,758 --> 01:14:36,358
[CYRUS] HFCs. And HFCs turned out to have a global warming potential up to fourteen thousand

1450
01:14:36,358 --> 01:14:40,958
[CYRUS] eight hundred times that of CO2. So we fixed the chemistry and created the next

1451
01:14:40,958 --> 01:14:45,558
[CYRUS] problem.

1452
01:14:45,558 --> 01:14:50,625
[JEFF] Which is why the Kigali Amendment in twenty sixteen is supposed to address HFCs. And

1453
01:14:50,625 --> 01:14:55,692
[JEFF] if it gets fully implemented, the modeling says it could avoid zero point four degrees

1454
01:14:55,692 --> 01:15:00,758
[JEFF] Celsius of warming by two thousand one hundred.

1455
01:15:00,758 --> 01:15:01,158
[CYRUS] If.

1456
01:15:01,158 --> 01:15:06,758
[JEFF] If. But that's not nothing. Zero point four degrees is actually a meaningful number.

1457
01:15:06,758 --> 01:15:12,258
[CYRUS] It is. But look — I'm not saying institutions can't do anything. I'm saying the

1458
01:15:12,258 --> 01:15:17,758
[CYRUS] structural problem is bigger than any single amendment can solve. Cooling consumes twenty-five to thirty

1459
01:15:17,758 --> 01:15:23,258
[CYRUS] percent of global electricity right now. And demand is about to explode. The Kigali Amendment

1460
01:15:23,258 --> 01:15:28,758
[CYRUS] is not going to bend that curve on its own.

1461
01:15:28,758 --> 01:15:34,492
[JEFF] I think where I land on this is — yes, it's structural, but I don't

1462
01:15:34,492 --> 01:15:40,225
[JEFF] think that means there's no institutional lever. The Kigali Amendment matters. The UNICEF solar refrigerators

1463
01:15:40,225 --> 01:15:45,958
[JEFF] matter. The Einstein fridge revival matters. These aren't sufficient, but they're not nothing.

1464
01:15:45,958 --> 01:15:50,892
[CYRUS] Right. I'm not saying do nothing. I'm saying don't kid yourself that the institutional fix

1465
01:15:50,892 --> 01:15:55,825
[CYRUS] is going to be enough. The scale of the demand shift is going to require

1466
01:15:55,825 --> 01:16:00,758
[CYRUS] a technological leap, not just better policy.

1467
01:16:00,758 --> 01:16:05,158
[JEFF] I think that's probably right. The way I think about it is — policy buys

1468
01:16:05,158 --> 01:16:09,558
[JEFF] time. Technology has to close the gap.

1469
01:16:09,558 --> 01:16:11,558
[CYRUS] That I can agree with.

1470
01:16:11,558 --> 01:16:17,514
[JEFF] And I want to come back to something I said in the last wave, because

1471
01:16:17,514 --> 01:16:23,470
[JEFF] I promised we'd return to it — the housework paradox. The refrigerator was marketed as

1472
01:16:23,470 --> 01:16:29,425
[JEFF] liberating women from daily shopping and daily spoilage. And it did do that. But what

1473
01:16:29,425 --> 01:16:35,381
[JEFF] actually happened is that the expectation ratcheted up. Weekly shopping replaced daily shopping, yes —

1474
01:16:35,381 --> 01:16:41,336
[JEFF] but now dinner was supposed to have more variety, more courses, higher standards. The time

1475
01:16:41,336 --> 01:16:47,292
[JEFF] saved by the refrigerator got absorbed by rising expectations for what the kitchen was supposed

1476
01:16:47,292 --> 01:16:53,247
[JEFF] to produce. And that's the same dynamic you see with email. Email was supposed to

1477
01:16:53,247 --> 01:16:59,203
[JEFF] save time. What it created was the expectation that you respond to everything within hours.

1478
01:16:59,203 --> 01:17:05,159
[JEFF] The technology that removes friction creates the standard that fills the friction back in.

1479
01:17:05,159 --> 01:17:08,759
[CYRUS] The refrigerator didn't liberate the kitchen. It raised the bar for what the kitchen was

1480
01:17:08,759 --> 01:17:12,359
[CYRUS] supposed to be.

1481
01:17:12,359 --> 01:17:17,759
[JEFF] Right. And that's a hidden cost that is completely invisible until you're looking for it.

1482
01:17:17,759 --> 01:17:23,159
[JEFF] It's not on anyone's balance sheet. But the social reorganization of domestic labor around refrigeration

1483
01:17:23,159 --> 01:17:28,559
[JEFF] is real. Anyway. The hidden cost also isn't just climate. There's something Nicola Twilley points

1484
01:17:28,559 --> 01:17:33,959
[JEFF] out in her book Frostbite — refrigeration reduced our exposure to fermented foods, to the

1485
01:17:33,959 --> 01:17:39,359
[JEFF] beneficial microbes in food that was naturally preserved in the pre-refrigeration era. We've built an

1486
01:17:39,359 --> 01:17:44,759
[JEFF] economy premised on cold equaling fresh, and it's not clear that assumption is even scientifically

1487
01:17:44,759 --> 01:17:50,159
[JEFF] correct. Flash-frozen food is often fresher than refrigerated food that's been sitting in your fridge

1488
01:17:50,159 --> 01:17:55,559
[JEFF] for four days.

1489
01:17:55,559 --> 01:17:58,759
[CYRUS] "Fresh, never frozen." That's still a marketing premium.

1490
01:17:58,759 --> 01:18:03,825
[JEFF] It's a marketing premium that goes back to the nineteen eleven Cold-Storage Banquet. Where they

1491
01:18:03,825 --> 01:18:08,892
[JEFF] literally tried to prove that a year-old egg was fine to eat. We've been arguing

1492
01:18:08,892 --> 01:18:13,959
[JEFF] about this for a hundred and fifteen years.

1493
01:18:13,959 --> 01:18:16,359
[CYRUS] We have not resolved it.

1494
01:18:16,359 --> 01:18:21,159
[JEFF] We have not resolved it. Okay. Question four: could we go back?

1495
01:18:21,159 --> 01:18:21,559
[CYRUS] No.

1496
01:18:21,559 --> 01:18:22,359
[JEFF] That's it?

1497
01:18:22,359 --> 01:18:28,059
[CYRUS] I mean, what do you want me to say? Seven billion people are fed through

1498
01:18:28,059 --> 01:18:33,759
[CYRUS] the cold chain. Hundreds of millions live in climates that are only habitable because of

1499
01:18:33,759 --> 01:18:39,459
[CYRUS] air conditioning. Vaccines, insulin, organ transplants — all require cold. The global food system would

1500
01:18:39,459 --> 01:18:45,159
[CYRUS] collapse within a week without refrigeration. We are not going back.

1501
01:18:45,159 --> 01:18:51,159
[JEFF] And here's the thing I keep coming back to — Tudor's whole business model was

1502
01:18:51,159 --> 01:18:57,159
[JEFF] built on creating dependency. That was his insight. You had to get people to want

1503
01:18:57,159 --> 01:19:03,159
[JEFF] cold before they would pay for cold. He didn't just build a business; he manufactured

1504
01:19:03,159 --> 01:19:09,159
[JEFF] a human expectation. And that expectation, two hundred years later, is so deep that it's

1505
01:19:09,159 --> 01:19:15,159
[JEFF] physically load-bearing. We built cities on it. We built a food supply for seven billion

1506
01:19:15,159 --> 01:19:21,159
[JEFF] people on it. Tudor's question from eighteen oh six — can you make people depend

1507
01:19:21,159 --> 01:19:27,159
[JEFF] on cold? — has been answered. You can. And now you cannot take it back.

1508
01:19:27,159 --> 01:19:28,759
[CYRUS] The dependency is total.

1509
01:19:28,759 --> 01:19:32,759
[JEFF] The dependency is total. Okay — question five. What's next?

1510
01:19:32,759 --> 01:19:37,559
[CYRUS] So this is where I actually get genuinely excited, because a couple of things are

1511
01:19:37,559 --> 01:19:42,359
[CYRUS] happening simultaneously. The first is the Kigali Amendment doing its work on HFCs — targeting

1512
01:19:42,359 --> 01:19:47,159
[CYRUS] the refrigerants that replaced the ozone-destroying ones. If it gets fully implemented, that zero point

1513
01:19:47,159 --> 01:19:51,959
[CYRUS] four degrees matters.

1514
01:19:51,959 --> 01:19:56,859
[JEFF] And natural refrigerants are making a comeback. Ammonia, CO2, hydrocarbons — all of them are

1515
01:19:56,859 --> 01:20:01,759
[JEFF] being revisited, partly because they don't have the HFC problem and partly because the regulatory

1516
01:20:01,759 --> 01:20:06,659
[JEFF] environment is starting to push in that direction. It's a bit of an ironic echo

1517
01:20:06,659 --> 01:20:11,559
[JEFF] of the pre-Freon era.

1518
01:20:11,559 --> 01:20:13,559
[CYRUS] And then there's Einstein's fridge.

1519
01:20:13,559 --> 01:20:18,519
[JEFF] Which — and I will just say this — is one of the great buried

1520
01:20:18,519 --> 01:20:23,479
[JEFF] stories in this whole episode. Oxford University engineers have actually revived Einstein and Szilard's nineteen

1521
01:20:23,479 --> 01:20:28,439
[JEFF] thirty design — the refrigerator with no moving parts, no toxic refrigerants, no electricity required.

1522
01:20:28,439 --> 01:20:33,399
[JEFF] The one Einstein patented after reading about a family killed by refrigerant fumes leaking from

1523
01:20:33,399 --> 01:20:38,359
[JEFF] their icebox.

1524
01:20:38,359 --> 01:20:42,759
[CYRUS] That design might finally find its purpose — a century later.

1525
01:20:42,759 --> 01:20:48,759
[JEFF] A century later. For off-grid developing-world use. Which is exactly the use case Einstein and

1526
01:20:48,759 --> 01:20:54,759
[JEFF] Szilard had in mind when they designed it. UNICEF is also deploying solar-powered vaccine refrigerators

1527
01:20:54,759 --> 01:21:00,759
[JEFF] in rural areas without electricity — leapfrogging the grid entirely. So there's real innovation happening.

1528
01:21:00,759 --> 01:21:06,359
[CYRUS] But the fundamental challenge — how do you provide universal access to cold without making

1529
01:21:06,359 --> 01:21:11,959
[CYRUS] the planet uninhabitable — that is still the open question. And here's the thing: that

1530
01:21:11,959 --> 01:21:17,559
[CYRUS] is actually the same question John Gorrie was trying to answer in eighteen forty-four. He

1531
01:21:17,559 --> 01:21:23,159
[CYRUS] wanted to cool sickrooms to save lives in a warming climate.

1532
01:21:23,159 --> 01:21:25,559
[JEFF] And we're still working on it.

1533
01:21:25,559 --> 01:21:29,959
[CYRUS] We're still working on it. A hundred and eighty years later.

1534
01:21:29,959 --> 01:21:35,559
[JEFF] Okay. We've got a couple of open questions we want to leave with you —

1535
01:21:35,559 --> 01:21:41,159
[JEFF] things we don't have tidy answers to, because they don't have tidy answers.

1536
01:21:41,159 --> 01:21:46,919
[CYRUS] First one's mine, and it's the cooling paradox. I keep coming back to this because

1537
01:21:46,919 --> 01:21:52,679
[CYRUS] it is genuinely structurally alarming. We need more cooling because the planet is warming. Cooling

1538
01:21:52,679 --> 01:21:58,439
[CYRUS] accelerates warming. The people who most need cooling are in the places that are warming

1539
01:21:58,439 --> 01:22:04,199
[CYRUS] fastest and have the least infrastructure to handle it. And a potential thirty-fold-plus surge in

1540
01:22:04,199 --> 01:22:09,959
[CYRUS] cooling demand by two thousand one hundred is not a solved problem.

1541
01:22:09,959 --> 01:22:15,859
[JEFF] And the specific version of this that I think is worth sitting with: India. India

1542
01:22:15,859 --> 01:22:21,759
[JEFF] is projected to grow air conditioner sales six-fold by twenty thirty-eight. Six times. That's not

1543
01:22:21,759 --> 01:22:27,659
[JEFF] a fringe scenario — that's the base case. And HFCs — the refrigerants currently in

1544
01:22:27,659 --> 01:22:33,559
[JEFF] use — have that fourteen-thousand-eight-hundred-times-CO2 warming potential. The math on this is not comfortable.

1545
01:22:33,559 --> 01:22:39,359
[CYRUS] The honest answer is I don't know how you thread that needle without a genuine

1546
01:22:39,359 --> 01:22:45,159
[CYRUS] technology breakthrough. Better efficiency standards help. The Kigali Amendment helps. But none of that bends

1547
01:22:45,159 --> 01:22:50,959
[CYRUS] the curve enough at the scale India alone represents, let alone the full developing world.

1548
01:22:50,959 --> 01:22:56,759
[CYRUS] This is the one where I think we are genuinely racing against ourselves.

1549
01:22:56,759 --> 01:23:01,959
[JEFF] Second question is mine. The cold-chain gap. Thirteen percent of all food produced globally is

1550
01:23:01,959 --> 01:23:07,159
[JEFF] lost to inadequate refrigeration. Enough to feed nearly a billion people. Fifty percent of vaccines

1551
01:23:07,159 --> 01:23:12,359
[JEFF] wasted globally due to cold-chain failures. These numbers are not natural disasters — they're infrastructure

1552
01:23:12,359 --> 01:23:17,559
[JEFF] deficits. And the infrastructure deficit is solvable.

1553
01:23:17,559 --> 01:23:18,359
[CYRUS] In principle.

1554
01:23:18,359 --> 01:23:23,025
[JEFF] In principle. The tools exist. Solar-powered cold storage. Einstein's fridge for off-grid communities. UNICEF's vaccine

1555
01:23:23,025 --> 01:23:27,692
[JEFF] refrigerators. The question is scale and will — both of which are genuine political and

1556
01:23:27,692 --> 01:23:32,359
[JEFF] institutional challenges, not physics problems.

1557
01:23:32,359 --> 01:23:36,759
[CYRUS] The Peru example is brutal. Thirty ultra-cold freezers in the whole country, all in one

1558
01:23:36,759 --> 01:23:41,159
[CYRUS] city, during a global pandemic that required ultra-cold distribution. That's not a technology problem. That's

1559
01:23:41,159 --> 01:23:45,559
[CYRUS] an investment problem.

1560
01:23:45,559 --> 01:23:50,919
[JEFF] It's a will problem. And I think it's worth naming that the cold-chain gap is

1561
01:23:50,919 --> 01:23:56,279
[JEFF] the same gap Tudor identified in eighteen twenty when he showed up in Martinique with

1562
01:23:56,279 --> 01:24:01,639
[JEFF] a ship full of ice and nobody there knew what to do with it. Creating

1563
01:24:01,639 --> 01:24:06,999
[JEFF] demand in places that don't have the infrastructure for it. It's the same problem, two

1564
01:24:06,999 --> 01:24:12,359
[JEFF] hundred years later, at vastly higher stakes.

1565
01:24:12,359 --> 01:24:17,559
[CYRUS] The freshness illusion question — we're going to just briefly flag this one.

1566
01:24:17,559 --> 01:24:23,559
[JEFF] Yeah. We built an entire food economy on the idea that cold equals fresh. And

1567
01:24:23,559 --> 01:24:29,559
[JEFF] Nicola Twilley's argument is that it's not quite right — that flash-frozen food is often

1568
01:24:29,559 --> 01:24:35,559
[JEFF] genuinely fresher than refrigerated food that's been sitting in cold storage for days or weeks.

1569
01:24:35,559 --> 01:24:41,559
[JEFF] "Fresh, never frozen" is a marketing line. That distinction matters more than we're letting on.

1570
01:24:41,559 --> 01:24:47,025
[CYRUS] "Fresh, never frozen" was also the argument at the nineteen eleven Cold-Storage Banquet. And it

1571
01:24:47,025 --> 01:24:52,492
[CYRUS] worked. The fear of old food has been with us for a long time, even

1572
01:24:52,492 --> 01:24:57,959
[CYRUS] as refrigeration made us dependent on old food marketed as fresh.

1573
01:24:57,959 --> 01:25:03,025
[JEFF] This is the worst it will ever be — I actually think that applies here.

1574
01:25:03,025 --> 01:25:08,092
[JEFF] Our ability to understand what "fresh" actually means scientifically is getting better. The marketing claims

1575
01:25:08,092 --> 01:25:13,159
[JEFF] are going to start running into actual data.

1576
01:25:13,159 --> 01:25:17,759
[JEFF] Okay. The part of this I want to leave people with — and this is

1577
01:25:17,759 --> 01:25:22,359
[JEFF] the one portable idea from this whole story.

1578
01:25:22,359 --> 01:25:22,759
[CYRUS] Go.

1579
01:25:22,759 --> 01:25:28,119
[JEFF] Every wave of refrigeration's history required a new chemistry. And every chemistry solution created a

1580
01:25:28,119 --> 01:25:33,479
[JEFF] problem its inventors couldn't see. Gorrie's design used the right principle but technology his era

1581
01:25:33,479 --> 01:25:38,839
[JEFF] couldn't make safe. Linde's ammonia machines were safe for breweries, deadly for home kitchens. Midgley's

1582
01:25:38,839 --> 01:25:44,199
[JEFF] Freon was safe for home kitchens, catastrophic for the ozone layer. HFCs replaced CFCs and

1583
01:25:44,199 --> 01:25:49,559
[JEFF] turned out to be potent greenhouse gases.

1584
01:25:49,559 --> 01:25:52,359
[CYRUS] Every solution is also the next problem.

1585
01:25:52,359 --> 01:25:58,059
[JEFF] But the specific version that I think is the takeaway — and I want to

1586
01:25:58,059 --> 01:26:03,759
[JEFF] be careful here, because I don't want this to collapse into "all technology has unintended

1587
01:26:03,759 --> 01:26:09,459
[JEFF] consequences," which is true but not useful. The specific thing that happened every time is

1588
01:26:09,459 --> 01:26:15,159
[JEFF] this: the cost only appeared after the solution became load-bearing. By the time we understood

1589
01:26:15,159 --> 01:26:20,859
[JEFF] what CFCs were doing to the ozone layer, half the civilized world was running on

1590
01:26:20,859 --> 01:26:26,559
[JEFF] them. By the time Rowland and Molina published their findings in nineteen seventy-four — the

1591
01:26:26,559 --> 01:26:32,259
[JEFF] findings that won them the Nobel Prize — the dependency was already so deep that

1592
01:26:32,259 --> 01:26:37,959
[JEFF] the Montreal Protocol took another thirteen years to sign.

1593
01:26:37,959 --> 01:26:42,759
[CYRUS] Thirteen years. And in those thirteen years, the ozone hole was forming.

1594
01:26:42,759 --> 01:26:48,559
[JEFF] The ozone hole was forming. And the question I keep sitting with — and this

1595
01:26:48,559 --> 01:26:54,359
[JEFF] is the one I want to leave listeners with — is not "does this technology

1596
01:26:54,359 --> 01:27:00,159
[JEFF] work?" Because refrigeration worked. It worked at every stage. The question is: what will we

1597
01:27:00,159 --> 01:27:05,959
[JEFF] discover about this in forty years, when it's too deeply embedded to remove?

1598
01:27:05,959 --> 01:27:11,159
[CYRUS] Right. And you can apply that to basically anything we're building right now.

1599
01:27:11,159 --> 01:27:17,159
[JEFF] You can. I think specifically about AI, because that's where we spend a lot of

1600
01:27:17,159 --> 01:27:23,159
[JEFF] our time. The things we don't know about long-term AI deployment at scale — the

1601
01:27:23,159 --> 01:27:29,159
[JEFF] social effects, the economic effects, the concentration effects — those costs are going to appear

1602
01:27:29,159 --> 01:27:35,159
[JEFF] on the same timeline as refrigeration's hidden costs. After the dependency is load-bearing. Not before.

1603
01:27:35,159 --> 01:27:40,959
[CYRUS] And by that point, you can't uninvent it. You can only invent around it. The

1604
01:27:40,959 --> 01:27:46,759
[CYRUS] same way we're inventing around HFCs now with natural refrigerants and the Kigali Amendment.

1605
01:27:46,759 --> 01:27:52,025
[JEFF] This is specifically the thing that the refrigeration story teaches that other technology stories often

1606
01:27:52,025 --> 01:27:57,292
[JEFF] obscure. The question is not whether the risk is real. The resisters in every wave

1607
01:27:57,292 --> 01:28:02,559
[JEFF] of refrigeration had real arguments. Gorrie's machine was genuinely dangerous. Ammonia machines did kill people.

1608
01:28:02,559 --> 01:28:07,825
[JEFF] The cold-storage food did make people genuinely uncomfortable for very reasonable reasons. The resisters were

1609
01:28:07,825 --> 01:28:13,092
[JEFF] often right about the immediate problem. They just couldn't stop the dependency once it formed.

1610
01:28:13,092 --> 01:28:18,359
[JEFF] And neither can we.

1611
01:28:18,359 --> 01:28:20,759
[CYRUS] So the lesson is... don't resist?

1612
01:28:20,759 --> 01:28:26,225
[JEFF] ... No. I think the lesson is: be honest about the timing gap. We talk

1613
01:28:26,225 --> 01:28:31,692
[JEFF] about technology risk like risk assessment and technology adoption happen at the same speed. They

1614
01:28:31,692 --> 01:28:37,159
[JEFF] don't. The technology spreads. The risk becomes clear later. The Montreal Protocol is actually the

1615
01:28:37,159 --> 01:28:42,625
[JEFF] hopeful version of this story — humanity identified the risk before the damage was irreversible,

1616
01:28:42,625 --> 01:28:48,092
[JEFF] coordinated, and acted. Thirteen years after the science. That's not fast. But it was fast

1617
01:28:48,092 --> 01:28:53,559
[JEFF] enough for the ozone layer to recover.

1618
01:28:53,559 --> 01:28:54,759
[CYRUS] The hopeful version.

1619
01:28:54,759 --> 01:29:00,759
[JEFF] The hopeful version. The less hopeful version is the one we're living with now —

1620
01:29:00,759 --> 01:29:06,759
[JEFF] a cooling paradox where the solution is also the accelerant. And we built it. We

1621
01:29:06,759 --> 01:29:12,759
[JEFF] chose it. Every one of us chose it, every time we bought a refrigerator or

1622
01:29:12,759 --> 01:29:18,759
[JEFF] moved to Phoenix or took a vaccine that needed to be kept at minus seventy.

1623
01:29:18,759 --> 01:29:19,559
[CYRUS] Tudor's legacy.

1624
01:29:19,559 --> 01:29:23,959
[JEFF] Tudor's legacy. Cold, built in. ... You cannot unring that bell.

1625
01:29:23,959 --> 01:29:28,919
[JEFF] Before we go — a couple of things. If this episode got you thinking, there's

1626
01:29:28,919 --> 01:29:33,879
[JEFF] a lot more in the show notes. Every person we mentioned — Frederic Tudor, John

1627
01:29:33,879 --> 01:29:38,839
[JEFF] Gorrie, Carl von Linde, Gustavus Swift, Thomas Midgley, Frederick McKinley Jones, even Einstein and Szilard

1628
01:29:38,839 --> 01:29:43,799
[JEFF] — we've got sources linked there, and some of those rabbit holes are genuinely worth

1629
01:29:43,799 --> 01:29:48,759
[JEFF] going down.

1630
01:29:48,759 --> 01:29:54,059
[CYRUS] And if you've got a friend or a colleague who would love this kind of

1631
01:29:54,059 --> 01:29:59,359
[CYRUS] story — the foundational technology stories, the messy middle of how the world got built

1632
01:29:59,359 --> 01:30:04,659
[CYRUS] — please share this episode with them. That's genuinely how this show grows. Word of

1633
01:30:04,659 --> 01:30:09,959
[CYRUS] mouth from people who love what they found.

1634
01:30:09,959 --> 01:30:14,759
[JEFF] We'd love for you to follow us wherever you get your podcasts. Subscribe, leave a

1635
01:30:14,759 --> 01:30:19,559
[JEFF] review if you feel like it — all of that matters more than you might

1636
01:30:19,559 --> 01:30:24,359
[JEFF] think for a show like this.

1637
01:30:24,359 --> 01:30:30,359
[CYRUS] Listeners, this has been one of the stories I've thought about most since we started

1638
01:30:30,359 --> 01:30:36,359
[CYRUS] working on this show. I keep coming back to that image of families in Texas

1639
01:30:36,359 --> 01:30:42,359
[CYRUS] in twenty twenty-one, storing their food in snowbanks outside their house. Two hundred years after

1640
01:30:42,359 --> 01:30:48,359
[CYRUS] Tudor, using nature's cold because the machines stopped. That's the backbone test in one image.

1641
01:30:48,359 --> 01:30:53,025
[JEFF] And the next time you open your refrigerator and don't think about it — that's

1642
01:30:53,025 --> 01:30:57,692
[JEFF] the whole story right there. That automatic gesture. That's two centuries of obsession, destruction, chemistry,

1643
01:30:57,692 --> 01:31:02,359
[JEFF] and human ingenuity, made invisible.

1644
01:31:02,359 --> 01:31:03,159
[CYRUS] That's it.

1645
01:31:03,159 --> 01:31:04,359
[JEFF] Thanks for listening.

1646
01:31:04,359 --> 01:31:05,559
[CYRUS] Until next time.

